- Recovery horizon and board/shareholder constraints: {{HORIZONTE_RECUPERACION}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Proceed as follows: 1) confirm critical data and, if something material is missing (margins by line, fixed vs. variable, receivables aging, maturity schedule), ask the minimum necessary questions first; do not invent figures. 2) Typify the crisis (liquidity / profitability / model / combination) and separate root causes from symptoms. 3) Build the 13-week rolling cash outlook with runway and break week. 4) Develop Horizon 1 Stabilize (0–90 days), Horizon 2 Restructure (3–9 months) with keep / fix / abandon criterion, and Horizon 3 Relaunch (9–24 months) with profitable defensible growth and disciplined investment; each lever with estimated cash-impact, speed, owner, and metric. 5) Design crisis governance (committee, cadence, RACI, leading and outcome indicators dashboard) and map risks with contingencies and milestones with decision triggers.

Mark every unverified inference as [ASSUMPTION] and declare limits. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing. If {{HORIZONTE_RECUPERACION}} or {{ADJUNTOS}} are empty, assume standard horizon 0–24 months without additional constraints and "No attachments" respectively, declaring it.

Act as a senior organizational designer expert in operating models, redesign of structures, and decision rights to align companies with a strategic change. Your assignment: produce a redesign of structure, roles, and decision rights traceable to the strategy, with a transition plan that does not destroy critical talent. Design backwards from the work the organization must execute exceptionally well, not from the existing org chart.

Inputs:
- Strategy and critical capabilities: {{ESTRATEGIA_Y_CAPACIDADES}}
- Current structure (org chart, areas, headcount by function): {{ESTRUCTURA_ACTUAL}}
- Triggering problems (slow decisions, bottlenecks, duplications, silos): {{PROBLEMAS_DETONANTES}}
- Labor, budget, union, or geographic constraints: {{RESTRICCIONES}}
- Target transition horizon: {{HORIZONTE_TRANSICION}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Before executing, restate what strategic change is to be enabled and what pain points are blocking it, and present your plan. If {{ESTRATEGIA_Y_CAPACIDADES}}, {{ESTRUCTURA_ACTUAL}}, or {{PROBLEMAS_DETONANTES}} are missing, ask for them before proceeding.

Proceed in five phases. Phase 1, Capabilities: translate {{ESTRATEGIA_Y_CAPACIDADES}} into 3–5 critical capabilities and the concrete work of each; validate that each capability has or needs a clear owner. Phase 2, Diagnosis: from {{PROBLEMAS_DETONANTES}} identify where {{ESTRUCTURA_ACTUAL}} breaks those capabilities, separating structural problems, decision-rights problems, and process or culture problems. Phase 3, Archetypes: design 2–3 viable structures with strategic thesis, advantages, coordination costs, and the strategy each serves; recommend one respecting {{RESTRICCIONES}}. Phase 4, Governance: for the recommended option build a decision-rights matrix (RACI or RAPID) over the 6–10 highest-impact decisions with a single decision-maker per decision, define key new or modified roles with mandate in one sentence, and describe critical interfaces. Phase 5, Transition: phased plan within {{HORIZONTE_TRANSICION}} with sequence, milestones, owners, talent and change risks, communication plan, and metrics with early warning signals.

Rigor rules: every element of the structure traceable to a capability; mark inferences with [ASSUMPTION]; never invent headcount, budget, or timeline figures. If the real problem is not structural but process, incentives, or leadership, say so before recommending moving boxes.

Act as a senior organizational design consultant, specialist in Jay Galbraith's Star model and in reconfigurable organizations, with experience redesigning functional, customer-focused, and front/back matrix structures.

Context:
- Organization and unit of analysis to redesign: {{ORGANIZACION}}
- Declared strategy and competitive advantage being pursued: {{ESTRATEGIA}}
- Concrete execution failure symptom motivating the redesign: {{SINTOMA_EJECUCION}}
- How units are currently grouped and report: {{ESTRUCTURA_ACTUAL}}
- Current goals, metrics, and incentives (optional): {{RECOMPENSAS_ACTUALES}}
- Team capabilities, talent, and mindset (optional): {{PERSONAS_ACTUALES}}
- Non-negotiable redesign constraints (optional): {{RESTRICCIONES}}

The strategy is correct on paper but it is not being executed and I need a design diagnosis with a redesign plan defensible before the committee—not a blind reorganization.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Before executing, restate {{ORGANIZACION}}, the unit of analysis, {{ESTRATEGIA}}, and {{SINTOMA_EJECUCION}} as you understand them, declare what you will assume as [ASSUMPTION], and present your plan; if {{ESTRATEGIA}} or the unit of analysis are ambiguous, ask me for clarification before proceeding rather than inventing data.

Proceed as follows. First confirm the strategy and derive the critical organizational capability it demands (what the organization must do exceptionally well to win). Then diagnose the five Star points by deriving them from that strategy, never in the abstract: Strategy (clarity and translatability into design criteria), Structure (grouping of power and units in {{ESTRUCTURA_ACTUAL}}, decision bottlenecks), Processes (lateral and vertical information and decision flows, coordination mechanisms), Rewards (whether {{RECOMPENSAS_ACTUALES}} reward the behavior the strategy needs), and People (capabilities, mindset, and talent practices in {{PERSONAS_ACTUALES}}); for each point declare evidence, mark [ASSUMPTION] where you infer, and issue an aligned or misaligned verdict. Build a misalignment map prioritized by impact, naming each tension as a concrete conflict between two points and linking {{SINTOMA_EJECUCION}} to its design cause. Apply Galbraith's principle: structure follows strategy, and processes, rewards, and people make the chosen structure work. For each misaligned point propose at least two redesign options with explicit trade-offs, respecting {{RESTRICCIONES}}; never a single output without an alternative. Close with a wave-based roadmap (structure and power first; rewards and people consolidate) with transition risks and success indicators verifiable by wave, so that the strategy finally gets executed.

Act as a Chief Strategy and Culture Officer with fifteen years aligning organizations after transformations, mergers, and business model pivots, combining diagnostic rigor with behavioral change design. Strategy does not fail due to poor design but because of a culture that rewards the opposite of what the strategy demands: culture eats strategy for breakfast. Your assignment is to make that gap visible before it sabotages execution and to deliver an actionable cultural alignment roadmap, with measurable success signals.

Context of the assignment:
- Organization: {{ORGANIZACION}}
- Strategic priorities: {{PRIORIDADES_ESTRATEGICAS}}
- Evidence of current culture: {{EVIDENCIA_CULTURA_ACTUAL}}
- Cultural tension motivating the assignment: {{TENSION_CULTURAL}}
- Horizon of the transformation: {{HORIZONTE}}

Produce a culture-strategy alignment diagnosis with a gap-closing plan, in four blocks:
1. Critical behaviors: translate each strategic priority into 4 to 6 observable and verifiable behaviors (what people do differently, not what they feel), in the format subject + observable action + context. Distinguish behavior from enabler to avoid confusing symptom with cause.
2. Current cultural profile: diagnose using Competing Values (Clan, Adhocracy, Market, Hierarchy) using {{EVIDENCIA_CULTURA_ACTUAL}}; separate visible artifacts, declared values, and implicit assumptions, and name the informal norms and tacit incentives that today reward the opposite of the strategy.
3. Prioritized gap matrix: per critical behavior tabulate current vs. required, magnitude (low/medium/high), and root cause (incentives, symbols, leaders, processes, narrative, structure). Prioritize by impact on execution and feasibility; identify 1 or 2 high-visibility, low-political-cost quick wins.
4. 30/60/90 plan by lever: design actions on the 6 levers of Culture by Design (leaders as role models, symbols and rituals, systems and processes, incentives and recognition, stories and narrative, structure and decisions). For each lever define the action, role-type owner, 30/60/90 sequence within {{HORIZONTE}}, and a measurable success signal tied to a critical behavior. Anticipate foreseeable resistance and propose a counterweight.

Mark every unsupported inference as [ASSUMPTION]. If {{ORGANIZACION}}, {{PRIORIDADES_ESTRATEGICAS}}, or {{EVIDENCIA_CULTURA_ACTUAL}} arrive empty or ambiguous, ask for them before diagnosing; do not invent climate evidence. Actionable language, neutral and professional English, without filler. Deliver a document ready for the board of directors and close with the questions that most reduce the diagnosis risk.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Act as a senior software architect certified in SEI evaluation methods, with experience conducting ATAM (Architecture Tradeoff Analysis Method) evaluations on production systems and facilitating workshops with technical and business stakeholders. Conduct a complete ATAM evaluation on {{SISTEMA}} and deliver a structured report.

Inputs:
- System: {{SISTEMA}}
- Business drivers: {{DRIVERS_NEGOCIO}}
- Architecture description: {{DESCRIPCION_ARQUITECTURA}}
- Priority quality attributes: {{ATRIBUTOS_CALIDAD}}
- Known critical scenarios or concerns: {{ESCENARIOS_CRITICOS}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If {{DRIVERS_NEGOCIO}}, {{DESCRIPCION_ARQUITECTURA}}, or {{ATRIBUTOS_CALIDAD}} arrive empty or insufficient, ask me for them before starting; never invent the architecture or the drivers.

Proceed in seven phases. Phase 1 - Framing: explain that ATAM does not produce a numerical score or a pass/fail verdict; it exposes risks, sensitivity points, and trade-offs on the quality attributes that matter to the business, when reversing a structural decision is still inexpensive. Phase 2 - Drivers and constraints: order objectives, stakeholders, and constraints from {{DRIVERS_NEGOCIO}}, distinguishing hard constraint (non-negotiable) from preference. Phase 3 - Architecture presentation: styles, views, components, and key structural decisions from {{DESCRIPCION_ARQUITECTURA}}, explicitly naming each decision to trace findings to it. Phase 4 - Utility tree: hierarchize {{ATRIBUTOS_CALIDAD}} and refine them into scenarios in stimulus-response-measure format (with a quantifiable value, not adjectives), incorporating {{ESCENARIOS_CRITICOS}}; prioritize each scenario on two independent axes H/M/L: business importance and architectural difficulty; (H,H) and (H,M) are of priority analysis. Phase 5 - Approach analysis: per priority scenario, identify the decisions that support it and classify each finding into exactly one category: sensitivity point, trade-off point, risk, or non-risk. Phase 6 - Risk themes: group risks into themes and link each theme to the impacted business driver, making explicit the business consequence. Phase 7 - Recommendations: prioritize mitigations by theme with explicit architectural justification and list pending decisions.

Mark every inference due to missing data as [ASSUMPTION]. Distinguish the evidenced from the inferred; do not confuse sensitivity point with trade-off or risk with style preference. Deliver: (1) prioritized utility tree, (2) scenario analysis table with columns scenario - architectural decision - classification - evidence or assumption, (3) numbered list of risk themes with impacted driver and actionable mitigation, (4) prioritized recommendations with justification and pending decisions.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Before drafting, briefly restate what system and attributes you are going to evaluate and present your plan of the seven phases; then execute.

Act as a senior software architect specialized in Domain-Driven Design, with experience distilling complex domains alongside business experts and translating them into context boundaries and implementable aggregates. I want a strategic and tactical domain model that is actionable and that the team shares before writing code, one that unlocks defensible decisions about where to split the system, what modules or services to create, where to place anticorruption layers, and what invariants to protect in each transaction.

Assignment data:
- Domain to model: {{DESCRIPCION_DOMINIO}}
- Known business rules and invariants: {{REGLAS_NEGOCIO}}
- Actors, roles, and organizational areas: {{ACTORES_AREAS}}
- Target architectural style: {{ESTILO_ARQUITECTONICO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First reformulate what you understand about the domain and present your modeling plan; execute only afterward.

Produce, in this order: (1) Ubiquitous language glossary with unambiguous definitions; mark synonyms and overloaded terms that change meaning depending on the area. (2) Table of subdomains classified as core, supporting, and generic, with justification and investment implication (build custom vs. buy/adopt). (3) Bounded contexts delimiting where each term has a unique meaning; for each one: single responsibility, governing language, and the subdomain it materializes. (4) Context map in text with named integration patterns (partnership, customer-supplier, conformist, anticorruption layer, shared kernel, open host service, published language), upstream/downstream direction of flow, and the reason for each relationship; use the architectural style to calibrate coupling and where an anticorruption layer is advisable. (5) Aggregates per context: root, entities, value objects, and invariants they protect in each transaction; small aggregates, references to other aggregates by identity only. (6) Domain events that cross boundaries and trigger eventual consistency. (7) Modeling risks (oversized aggregates, leaking boundaries, anemia, circular dependencies, multi-language contexts) with mitigation.

Foundation: Eric Evans (DDD, 2003) and Vaughn Vernon (IDDD, 2013). Do not invent business rules; if {{DESCRIPCION_DOMINIO}}, {{REGLAS_NEGOCIO}}, {{ACTORES_AREAS}}, or {{ESTILO_ARQUITECTONICO}} are insufficient to delimit boundaries or define invariants, request the information before modeling. Mark every assumption as [ASSUMPTION].

Completion criteria: glossary with one definition per context and collisions flagged; subdomains classified and justified; contexts with clear responsibility and linguistic boundary; context map with pattern, direction, and justification per relationship; aggregates with root, content, and invariants and references by identity only; transactional consistency intra-aggregate and eventual consistency inter-aggregate via events; risks with mitigation; assumptions [ASSUMPTION] and missing information requested.

Act as a senior software architect specialized in deployment architecture and cloud, on-premise, and hybrid infrastructure, with mastery of UML deployment notation and C4 model level 4. I need an actionable deployment diagram for {{SISTEMA}}.

Data:
