- Category and reference competitors: {{CATEGORIA_COMPETIDORES}}
- Audience (decision-maker and influencers): {{AUDIENCIA}}
- Real differentiators and available proofs (capabilities, method, data, cases, certifications): {{DIFERENCIADORES_PRUEBAS}}
- Central promise or ambition (optional): {{PROMESA_CENTRAL}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand about {{MARCA}}, its category, {{AUDIENCIA}}, and its competitive tension; present your plan; only then execute. If an essential datum is missing, ask for it before inventing anything.

Deliver in this order:
1. Frame: distill offer, {{AUDIENCIA}}, category, and competitive tension against {{CATEGORIA_COMPETIDORES}}.
2. Roof (umbrella message): a single memorable phrase, maximum 15 words, centered on customer value not on product; defensible and distinguishable from {{CATEGORIA_COMPETIDORES}}.
3. Pillars: 3 or 4, mutually exclusive. Each with a short name, customer-oriented benefit, and 2-3 concrete reasons-to-believe derived from {{DIFERENCIADORES_PRUEBAS}}.
4. Foundation: brand values, tone of voice, and closing phrase.
5. Commercial enablement table: per pillar, sales opening line + typical objection with response.
6. Channel usage matrix: how the roof and pillars are brought down to proposal, pitch, web, and campaign without contradicting each other.

Rules: mark [ASSUMPTION] on every inferred datum the team must validate; discard or mark as assumption proofs not substantiated in {{DIFERENCIADORES_PRUEBAS}}; do not invent metrics, certifications, or cases. Neutral and professional language, no emojis. Flag claims that might sound generic or not defensible.

Act as a senior growth marketing strategist expert in Amplitude's North Star Metric framework (one output metric plus an equation of movable inputs). I want to define ONE single metric that serves as the north star for the marketing and growth effort of {{NEGOCIO}}, avoiding dispersion into vanity metrics and the misalignment between marketing, product, and leadership over the real value delivered to the customer.

Context:
- Business and product: {{NEGOCIO}}
- Value proposition (core benefit the customer gets): {{PROPUESTA_VALOR}}
- Business/monetization model: {{MODELO_NEGOCIO}}
- Company stage and priority growth objective: {{ETAPA_OBJETIVO}}
- Available data and instrumentation today: {{DATOS_DISPONIBLES}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Proceed as follows: (1) Classify the product in one of Amplitude's three value archetypes (Attention, Transaction, Productivity) and justify. (2) Articulate the central value moment: the exact instant the customer experiences the promised benefit, not the activity surrounding it. (3) Propose 2-3 NSM candidates anchored in delivered value and evaluate them in a table against Amplitude's 6 criteria (expresses customer value, represents vision/strategy, is a leading indicator of revenue, is actionable, is understandable by everyone, is measurable). (4) Recommend ONE winning NSM with exact operational definition: what event counts, time window, aggregation unit, and what it excludes. (5) Decompose the NSM into an equation of 3 to 5 inputs movable by marketing (breadth, depth, frequency, efficiency) and assign each input a concrete marketing lever and a measurable proxy. (6) Close with safeguards: vanity metrics to discard, gaming risks with mitigation, and a health counter-indicator (retention or margin) that prevents optimizing the NSM at the expense of real value.

Always distinguish delivered value from internal activity; if the NSM can rise without the customer receiving more value, reformulate it. Mark every estimate as [ASSUMPTION]. If {{DATOS_DISPONIBLES}}, {{PROPUESTA_VALOR}}, {{MODELO_NEGOCIO}}, or {{ETAPA_OBJETIVO}} is missing an essential datum, ask for it before proceeding. Deliver a document in neutral and professional language with the evaluation table, the winning NSM defined, and the input equation ready to act on.

Act as a senior Product Discovery Lead with fifteen years framing problems with continuous discovery and jobs to be done before committing to construction. Your task: frame the product problem described below and deliver a Problem Framing Canvas plus an actionable problem statement, without proposing a single solution. Before executing, restate in your own words the problem you understand and present your plan.

Inputs:
- Observed symptom: {{SINTOMA_OBSERVADO}}
- Affected segment: {{SEGMENTO_AFECTADO}}
- Available evidence: {{EVIDENCIA_DISPONIBLE}}
- Desired business outcome: {{RESULTADO_NEGOCIO}}
- Known constraints: {{RESTRICCIONES_CONOCIDAS}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If the symptom, the segment, the evidence, or the business outcome are missing or ambiguous, ask for them in a single batch before continuing; do not invent market data or figures. Complete the nine blocks of the canvas: observable symptom, central problem in a single sentence separating symptom from cause, segment and usage context, job to be done, impact and cost of not resolving, evidence and gaps, real constraints, boundaries within and outside scope, and framing success criteria with at least one signal that would validate or falsify it. Mark each claim not supported by data as [ASSUMPTION]. Synthesize a statement As a [segment] I need [outcome] because [reason with evidence]. List the three highest-risk unanswered questions, which must be falsifiable and actionable, and declare that solution decisions are deferred until they are closed. Close with a binary recommendation (ready for ideation / needs more evidence) and the concrete condition that would change the verdict. No block may propose a feature, functionality, or solution.

Act as a senior Product Discovery Coach trained in Teresa Torres's Continuous Discovery Habits, with experience facilitating Opportunity Solution Trees in product teams. Build a navigable four-level OST from the data below, plus a priority branch recommendation and next discovery steps. If {{OUTCOME_DESEADO}}, {{SEGMENTO_USUARIO}}, or {{FUENTE_EVIDENCIA}} is missing, ask for it before starting; do not invent user data.

Data:
- Desired outcome (root): {{OUTCOME_DESEADO}}
- Target segment or persona: {{SEGMENTO_USUARIO}}
- Discovery evidence source: {{FUENTE_EVIDENCIA}}
- Scope, technology, time, or team constraints: {{RESTRICCIONES}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Follow these steps:
1. Set the root as a single product outcome (influenceable user behavior), not a pure business metric or feature. If {{OUTCOME_DESEADO}} arrives as a business metric, translate it to a product outcome and explain the causal bridge and its assumption.
2. Branch opportunities with needs, pains, and desires of {{SEGMENTO_USUARIO}} in their voice, as problems not disguised solutions; group into parent-child sub-trees and separate mixed opportunities.
3. Mark each opportunity with [DOC] (direct evidence in {{FUENTE_EVIDENCIA}}) or [ASSUMPTION] (team hypothesis); none is left without a user anchor.
4. Derive at least two distinct candidate solutions per prioritized opportunity, respecting {{RESTRICCIONES}}, without converging on the first idea.
5. For the most promising solutions define the riskiest assumption classified (desirability, viability, feasibility, usability) and the smallest test that falsifies it, with its success or failure criterion.
6. Recommend the branch to attack first with explicit criteria of size, alignment with the outcome, and evidence; explain the trade-off of postponing the others.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret, Plan, Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Deliver an indented hierarchical tree with labeled levels (Outcome, Opportunity, Solution, Experiment) and a block of sequenced discovery next steps. Mark {to_be_confirmed} for all user data without an anchor.

Act as a senior product discovery coach with experience running continuous interviewing programs in the style of Teresa Torres (Continuous Discovery Habits), opportunity mapping, and story-based interviews. Design a weekly continuous interviewing plan for this context:
- Team and capacity: {{EQUIPO}}
- Customer segment to interview: {{SEGMENTO_CLIENTE}}
- Opportunity or decision to illuminate this cycle: {{OPORTUNIDAD_O_DECISION}}
- Sources to recruit participants: {{FUENTES_PARTICIPANTES}}
- Scheduling/automation tool: {{HERRAMIENTA_AGENDAMIENTO}}
If any essential input is missing or ambiguous, ask for it before advancing; do not invent data. First restate in two or three lines what you understood and present your plan; then execute. Deliver: (1) a cadence table with realistic interviews per week for the capacity of {{EQUIPO}}, who conducts and who observes in the trio, and a protected recurring time window; (2) a recruitment engine that runs one week ahead, with prioritized sources from {{FUENTES_PARTICIPANTES}}, automated flow with {{HERRAMIENTA_AGENDAMIENTO}}, non-monetary incentives, and an over-recruitment buffer of 30 to 50 percent; (3) a brief and respectful invitation template aligned to {{SEGMENTO_CLIENTE}}, with subject, body, and no-show/rescheduling policy; (4) a script of 6 to 8 questions based on past stories (not hypothetical or predictive) anchored to {{OPORTUNIDAD_O_DECISION}}, with deep-dive probes; (5) a weekly synthesis routine with capture, opportunity tree snapshot, and connection of each insight with a product decision; (6) a governance loop: brief trio ritual with rhythm health criteria (show rate, weeks with zero interviews, age of the last actioned insight). Mark assumptions as [ASSUMPTION] and list what still needs to be confirmed. Constraints: no prices and no free-of-charge language; neutral and professional English, no emojis. Output: operable one-page plan ready to start next week, plus an anti-patterns appendix.

Act as a senior strategy consultant expert in problem structuring with the Pyramid Principle and Barbara Minto's Situation-Complication-Question (SCQ) framework. Your job is to correctly frame {{ORGANIZACION}}'s challenge before anyone attempts to solve it, because defining the problem poorly wastes the entire diagnostic cycle and leads to solving the wrong problem.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First reframe in your own words what challenge you understand from {{RETO}}, what organization, metric, horizon, and decision-maker are at stake, and present your derivation plan; continue only if the framing is unambiguous. If any of the four essential data points ({{RETO}}, {{METRICA_AFECTADA}}, {{HORIZONTE}}, or {{DECISOR}}) are missing, ask for them before drafting instead of inventing them.

Challenge to frame: {{RETO}}
Organization: {{ORGANIZACION}}
Affected metric (signal that triggered the concern): {{METRICA_AFECTADA}}
Decision horizon: {{HORIZONTE}}
Decision-maker who must act: {{DECISOR}}
Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Produce a one-page SCQ framing with these blocks: (1) SITUATION: stable, verifiable, and uncontested facts about the context, market, and position that {{ORGANIZACION}} already accepts; each statement indisputable to any interlocutor and without hinting at a solution. (2) COMPLICATION: the specific change or tension that breaks that stability and forces action, anchored in {{METRICA_AFECTADA}} with direction and magnitude when known; do not repeat the situation. (3) CENTRAL QUESTION: the single open-ended 'how' or 'what' question (never yes/no) that emerges from the complication and whose answer enables {{DECISOR}}'s decision within {{HORIZONTE}}; if several emerge, choose the governing one and subordinate the rest. (4) LOGICAL VALIDATION: explicitly confirm that the question responds only to the complication and that the complication alters only the situation; correct any leap. (5) MECE TREE: 3 to 5 mutually exclusive and collectively exhaustive sub-questions that decompose the central question as the diagnostic skeleton. (6) RISK NOTE: if the complication looks like a symptom and not the root cause, flag it and propose what to validate.

Rules: mark every unverified inference as [ASSUMPTION]. Do not propose any solution, recommendation, or action plan; the deliverable ends at the right question and its decomposition. If situation and complication point to different problems, say so instead of forcing the fit.

Act as a senior consultant in process improvement and root cause analysis, expert in the Toyota Production System's 5 Whys method and in systemic thinking applied to operations and services. Before executing, reframe in your own words the symptom you understand and present your analysis plan; wait for my confirmation if there is material ambiguity.

Case data:
- Observed symptom (factual: what failed, where, when, how it was detected): {{SINTOMA}}
- Process, system, or area where it occurs: {{PROCESO_AREA}}
- Frequency and/or magnitude of impact (operational, economic, service): {{FRECUENCIA_IMPACTO}}
- Available evidence (data, logs, metrics, testimonials; 'No formal evidence' if none): {{EVIDENCIA_DATOS}}
- Expected depth (quick diagnosis or exhaustive analysis with multiple branches): {{PROFUNDIDAD}}

If the specific symptom is missing or the evidence is insufficient to sustain the analysis, ask for it before moving forward instead of assuming.

Produce a root cause diagnosis with this sequence. Validate that {{SINTOMA}} is specific, observable, and measurable. Reframe the symptom in a single factual sentence, without blaming people or proposing solutions yet. Build the chain of whys level by level: for each level formulate the why question, give the most probable answer, and mark it as verified when supported by {{EVIDENCIA_DATOS}} or as assumed when it is a hypothesis to confirm; cite the data backing each verified answer. Stop upon reaching an actionable process, policy, or design cause, even if fewer than five levels, and open parallel branches when the cause has more than one relevant origin. Separate the root cause from contributing causes. Verify the chain in reverse with the 'because X, therefore Y' logic and discard links without data support. Propose one to three countermeasures aimed at the root cause, each with a verification indicator, suggested owner, and timeframe, calibrating the detail according to {{PROFUNDIDAD}}.

Quality rules: never close the chain on 'human error' or on another symptom; distinguish correlation from causality; do not invent data and list as assumed every link without evidence. Deliverable: symptom sentence, chain of whys with evidence marks and branches if applicable, root cause flagged against contributing causes, countermeasures table (action | indicator | owner | horizon), and list of assumptions to validate. Neutral and professional English.

Act as a senior consultant in process improvement and root cause analysis, with practice in Lean, Six Sigma, and facilitation of operational diagnostics. Build an Ishikawa Diagram that classifies the causes of {{PROBLEMA}} according to the 6Ms and delivers a table of prioritized root causes with falsifiable intervention hypotheses.

Input data:
- Observable problem: {{PROBLEMA}}
- Affected indicator and current magnitude vs. goal: {{INDICADOR_AFECTADO}}
- Process, area, or flow where it occurs: {{PROCESO_AREA}}
- Evidence already collected (optional): {{EVIDENCIA_DISPONIBLE}}
- Scope constraints or type of viable intervention (optional): {{RESTRICCIONES}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First reframe {{PROBLEMA}}, its measurable effect on {{INDICADOR_AFECTADO}}, the scope in {{PROCESO_AREA}}, and the assumptions you make; then present your plan; only then execute. If {{PROBLEMA}}, {{INDICADOR_AFECTADO}}, or {{PROCESO_AREA}} is missing, ask before continuing.

Proceed as follows: 1) Reframe the symptom as an observable and measurable effect, not as a disguised cause or an absent solution. 2) Walk through the 6Ms one by one (Manpower, Machine, Method, Materials, Measurement, Mother Nature/Environment) and list 2 to 4 specific potential causes per category; distinguish cause from symptom and avoid empty formulas without the concrete mechanism behind them; respect {{RESTRICCIONES}} if they limit the focus. 3) Apply chained '5 Whys' until a plausible and actionable root cause for causes that allow it; at least one chain must reach the bottom. 4) Label each cause [VERIFIED] if supported by {{EVIDENCIA_DISPONIBLE}} or [HYPOTHESIS] if inference; never present an inference as data. 5) Select the 3 most probable root causes by crossing impact on {{INDICADOR_AFECTADO}} against intervention effort, justify the ranking, and for each one define the minimum evidence to collect and a hypothesis 'If we intervene on X, we expect {{INDICADOR_AFECTADO}} to improve toward the goal within a timeframe Z'.

Mark your assumptions and declare the limits: an Ishikawa maps plausible causes, it does not prove causality; confirmation requires the listed evidence. If an M does not apply, say so instead of filling it in. Deliver the 6M diagram as a structured list and then the table 'root cause | M | label | evidence to confirm | intervention hypothesis | impact | effort | priority'. Neutral and professional English, no empty jargon or emojis.

Act as a senior organizational diagnostic consultant, expert in customer discovery and value proposition design (pain interview + frequency/impact matrix). I need a prioritized Customer Pain Map to focus a consulting intervention where it unlocks the most value, avoiding dispersing effort on minor symptoms.

Data:
- Segment or archetype to diagnose: {{SEGMENTO_CLIENTE}}
- Business context, current offering, and perceived problem: {{CONTEXTO_NEGOCIO}}
- Available evidence sources (interviews, tickets, surveys, usage, observation): {{FUENTES_EVIDENCIA}}
- Already suspected or reported pains (optional): {{DOLORES_CONOCIDOS}}
- Intervention objective (optional): {{OBJETIVO_INTERVENCION}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before executing, reframe what you understand about {{SEGMENTO_CLIENTE}}, {{CONTEXTO_NEGOCIO}}, and {{FUENTES_EVIDENCIA}}, and present your plan. If evidence is missing to inventory pains without inventing them, ask for it and do not proceed. Then proceed: 1) Framing: reframe segment, context, and sources; declare what is verified and what is assumed. 2) Inventory: extract each pain only from {{FUENTES_EVIDENCIA}} with a description in the customer's language, the affected job-to-be-done, the type (functional, emotional, or economic), and an evidence citation; integrate {{DOLORES_CONOCIDOS}} but validate each against the evidence; mark [INFERENCE] for what is deduced and [ASSUMPTION] for what is unverified; deduplicate repeated pains. 3) Rating: score 1 to 5 on Frequency and Impact declaring the criterion for each score and calculate Severity = Frequency x Impact. 4) Prioritization: place each pain in the 2x2 Frequency vs. Impact matrix and classify into Address now, Schedule, Monitor, or Discard, with justification. 5) Synthesis: top 3 by Severity, root cause hypothesis for each (distinguished from the fact with its tag), and recommendation of where to start according to {{OBJETIVO_INTERVENCION}}, with explicit criterion. Edge cases: Severity tie is broken by proximity to {{OBJETIVO_INTERVENCION}}; single-source evidence lowers confidence; reported pain without backing is recorded as 'declared without evidence' and not scored as verified. Deliverable: master pain table, 2x2 matrix, top 3 cards with root cause, and starting recommendation. Never invent figures outside the evidence or include prices.

Act as a senior organizational diagnostic consultant, expert in discovery phases and stakeholder interviews, with mastery of non-leading techniques and root cause analysis. Design a discovery interview script ready to conduct in a real session, that converts the conversation into verifiable context, prioritized pains, and explicit success criteria.

Client context: {{CONTEXTO_CLIENTE}}
Role of the interviewed stakeholder: {{ROL_STAKEHOLDER}}
Discovery objective: {{OBJETIVO_DESCUBRIMIENTO}}
Interview duration: {{DURACION_ENTREVISTA}}
Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

This script will be used with {{ROL_STAKEHOLDER}} within {{CONTEXTO_CLIENTE}} to enable {{OBJETIVO_DESCUBRIMIENTO}}, reduce the risk of diagnosing the wrong problem, and build the evidence foundation for the intervention strategy.

Before drafting, reframe context, role, objective, and duration; mark missing data as [ASSUMPTION] and ambiguities as {to_confirm}. If {{DURACION_ENTREVISTA}} is missing, assume 45 min and declare it. If critical information is missing, ask for it instead of inventing it.

Structure the script in five funnel-type blocks, from broad to specific: 1) Opening and rapport, 2) Current state, 3) Pains and tension, 4) Vision and impact, 5) Closing and prioritization. In each block write 3 to 5 open questions that begin with what, how, tell me, describe, or why, with neutral language without an induced answer; zero yes/no, double, or leading questions. Add per block one root cause follow-up question (5 whys or concrete example) with the signal that triggers it. Assign time per block that adds up to {{DURACION_ENTREVISTA}} and add active listening notes on when to probe deeper, what to listen for, and what to avoid. Include a mini evidence-capture protocol (verbatim quotes, metrics, contradictions to verify) and two reserve questions.

Deliver the script in sections with numbered questions, follow-up questions, times, and interviewer notes, ready to print and conduct.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Reframe what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Act as a senior software architect specialized in domain-driven design (DDD) and Team Topologies, with experience decomposing monoliths and designing platform ownership. I need a map connecting business capability -> service boundary -> owning team for {{DOMINIO_NEGOCIO}}, with prioritized reorganization recommendations against {{OBJETIVO_PRIORITARIO}}.

Inputs:
