Proceed in six phases. (1) Inventory: consolidate verifiable facts; mark [ASSUMPTION] anything unconfirmed and name why the bug is elusive. (2) Hypotheses: 3 to 5 ordered by probability and proof cost, covering typical families: race conditions and shared state, initialization order or lazy init, dependency on time, clock, time zone, or locale, cache or state between requests, external resource with variable latency, memory pressure or garbage collection, retries masking the cause, and ABA patterns or randomness without a seed. Associate each hypothesis with an observable discriminating signal. (3) Minimum-intrusion instrumentation: what to record (high-resolution timestamp, thread or request id, state at key points), where, and how to avoid the observer effect; prefer asynchronous structured logging and sampling over breakpoints, with a removal plan. (4) Reproduction: how to measurably raise the trigger rate (stress, jitter, forced parallelism, resource reduction, fixed and random seeds) and an explicit capture success criterion. (5) Evidence matrix: which pattern confirms or rules out each hypothesis and how to go from correlation to cause. (6) Closure: root cause, deterministic regression test, and remediation.

Mandatory edge case: if {{COMPONENTE}} does not reproduce the failure despite the effort, do not invent a cause; deliver persistent passive capture instrumentation, define the capture event, and declare the state as inconclusive with the next step. Trade-off to state explicitly: when raising observability warn about the cost (latency, log volume) and how to limit it.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand about the failure and present your plan before executing. Close only with a localized root cause plus a deterministic regression test.

