Structure the plan in five sections: (1) Tone of voice for each channel in {{CANALES}} with tone description, 5 to 8 permitted words, 5 to 8 prohibited words, and two contrasted examples (correct vs. incorrect); (2) SLA table by message type (commercial question, support query, public complaint, positive mention, neutral comment, spam or troll) with target time during business hours, maximum time, responsible party, and out-of-hours rule; (3) Moderation matrix by severity levels 1 to 4 with identification criterion, permitted action (respond, hide, delete, block, report, escalate), base script, and what NOT to do, integrating {{TEMAS_SENSIBLES}} in the higher levels; (4) Crisis protocol with objective activation criteria, alert levels, roles and chain of command, first-response window, a ready-to-use holding statement, incident log, and closure and learning criteria; (5) Proactive interaction rules: when to initiate conversation, publication frequency, and community rituals.

Mark as [ASSUMPTION] what you infer and flag critical gaps. Neutral and professional Spanish, no emojis; no prices or discounts in scripts; do not use the word free (say at no additional cost or use courtesy). Close with adoption checklist and decisions pending brand manager approval.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate your understanding and present your plan before executing.

Act as a senior social media and go-to-market strategist, with experience leading product and event launches on social channels. I need a complete social launch kit, ready to execute by a content team or by a solo founder.

Launch data:
- What is being launched: {{PRODUCTO_O_EVENTO}}
- Target audience: {{PUBLICO_OBJETIVO}}
- Day 0 date (YYYY-MM-DD): {{FECHA_LANZAMIENTO}}
- Available channels: {{CANALES}}
- Brand tone and voice: {{TONO_MARCA}}
- Key differentiator (optional): {{DIFERENCIADOR_CLAVE}}

Before drafting, restate what is being launched, who it is for, and the day 0 date; confirm channels, tone, and differentiator; present your phase plan. If an essential data point is missing, ask for it in a single round. If the day 0 date is only a few days away, compress the windows and declare it as an assumption.

Deliver a strategy in four sequential phases: intrigue teasers (before day 0, curiosity without revealing), hype (days before day 0, desire with benefits and social proof), launch day (day 0, reach and conversion with main message, demo, and clear call to action), and follow-up (after day 0, momentum with testimonials, use cases, and close). Per phase define the objective, window in days relative to day 0, and narrative angle; draft three to five pieces, each with a hook, complete ready-to-paste copy, an explicit CTA, a recommended format (carousel, reel, story, static post, thread), and a suggested channel from {{CANALES}}, aligned to the tone {{TONO_MARCA}}. Close with a consolidated day-by-day calendar without gaps or overlaps and a table of three to five metrics per phase, each with a quantifiable goal and decision signal (continue, adjust, or reinforce). Flag assumptions with the label assumption. Neutral, professional Spanish, without emojis and without prices.

Act as a senior conversion rate optimization (CRO) strategist who has redesigned dozens of acquisition landing pages and masters direct-response copy, visual hierarchy, and experiment design. Audit a landing that receives traffic but does not convert, and deliver ready-to-implement corrections.

Context:
- Target conversion action: {{OBJETIVO_CONVERSION}}
- Audience, their main pain point, and awareness level of the offer: {{PUBLICO_OBJETIVO}}
- What is offered and the central promise or transformation: {{OFERTA}}
- Current content and structure of the landing section by section, or URL: {{CONTENIDO_LANDING}}
- Available social proof (testimonials, cases, logos, figures, certifications): {{PRUEBA_SOCIAL}}

Follow the MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret (restate objective, audience, and offer; declare assumptions), Plan (present your plan before executing), Execute. If {{CONTENIDO_LANDING}} is missing, ask for it before auditing; do not invent sections.

Deliver three blocks. First, audit across five axes: value proposition above the fold, clarity of the main benefit, cognitive friction and load, placement of social proof at the point of doubt, and CTA architecture in text, contrast, hierarchy, and repetition. For each finding: what is wrong, why it costs conversions by naming the violated persuasion or cognitive load principle, and the concrete correction already rewritten. Second, ready-to-paste copy: hero headline in 2 or 3 variants, subheadline, and main CTA in verb-plus-value format. Third, hypothesis backlog ordered by impact against effort, each with a success metric and how it would be measured.

Edge cases: if {{PRUEBA_SOCIAL}} is empty, propose which signal to build first; if there are multiple actions, prioritize one primary conversion. Without the word free, without prices, neutral and professional Spanish, without emojis.

Act as a senior demand and conversion strategist, specialist in lead magnets that qualify leads without discounts or giving away value without building commitment. Design a complete lead magnet and capture offer package for my funnel with this data:
- Ideal lead and awareness level: {{LEAD_IDEAL}}
- Most urgent problem: {{PROBLEMA_URGENTE}}
- Funnel stage: {{ETAPA_FUNNEL}}
- Next step after capture: {{SIGUIENTE_PASO}}
- Production capacity: {{CAPACIDAD_PRODUCCION}}
If any critical data is missing or ambiguous, ask me before proceeding; do not invent the context. Deliver six blocks: 1) Problem map: central problem of {{LEAD_IDEAL}}, their awareness level (unaware, problem-aware, solution-aware, product-aware), moment in the journey, and the transformation the asset unlocks in fewer than fifteen minutes regarding {{PROBLEMA_URGENTE}}. 2) Three candidate concepts, each with format, transformation promise in one sentence, consumption effort, fit with {{PROBLEMA_URGENTE}}, and a risk or limitation. 3) Winning concept justified by perceived value against cost within {{CAPACIDAD_PRODUCCION}}, with a discard criterion for the other two. 4) Capture copy: outcome-oriented headline, subheadline, three to five benefit bullets, proof or credibility element, and form call to action. 5) Minimum form fields that balance friction and qualification, indicating for each one what it decides or segments in view of {{SIGUIENTE_PASO}}. 6) Sequence of three messages that delivers the asset and opens {{SIGUIENTE_PASO}}. Before executing, restate what you understood and present your plan. Close with a verification checklist. Use neutral, professional Spanish, without emojis, without prices, and without using the word that refers to giving away value without commitment.

Act as a senior conversion rate optimization (CRO) and funnel strategy specialist, with experience in e-commerce, payment UX, and A/B experimentation. The checkout is the final stretch of the funnel: every step, field, and unresolved question is a revenue leak from a gain already made. Your mission is to diagnose the real checkout friction of {{NEGOCIO_PRODUCTO}} and deliver a prioritized friction dashboard plus an actionable elimination plan, without weakening trust or losing critical business data.

Context:
- Business and product: {{NEGOCIO_PRODUCTO}}
- Checkout platform or stack: {{PLATAFORMA_CHECKOUT}}
- Target audience and predominant device: {{PUBLICO_OBJETIVO}}
- Current flow (steps and fields): {{FLUJO_ACTUAL}}
- Highest-known abandonment point: {{PUNTO_ABANDONO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Proceed in four phases. Phase 1 – Mapping: reconstruct the flow from cart to confirmation using {{FLUJO_ACTUAL}}, listing each screen, field, and decision asked of the buyer; flag as [ASSUMPTION] any unconfirmed data. Phase 2 – Diagnosis: classify each element as essential, deferrable, or removable, label the type of friction (cognitive effort, manual effort, surprise cost, security doubt, lack of trust, technical friction), and cross-reference with doubts and objections per step (shipping, returns, payment security, additional costs, delivery times, payment methods) for {{PUBLICO_OBJETIVO}}, indicating where they are resolved and where they remain open. Phase 3 – Prioritization: place each friction in an impact-by-effort matrix, with special weight on {{PUNTO_ABANDONO}}, and order by expected return. Phase 4 – Intervention: for the highest-impact points, define the concrete intervention, the behavioral rationale, and an A/B hypothesis with primary metric, guard metric, and decision criterion, respecting the constraints of {{PLATAFORMA_CHECKOUT}}.

Draw on recognized frameworks: cognitive load reduction, Hick's law, cost transparency, social proof, trust signals, and minimum effort. Distinguish mobile from desktop. Consider edge cases: returning user vs. first purchase, single-item vs. multi-item cart, discount codes, payment failure, and retry. Do not invent figures; every estimate goes as [ASSUMPTION] with its reasoning. If critical information is missing, ask for it before continuing.

Deliver: (1) prioritized friction dashboard (table with step, element, classification, friction type, objection, impact, effort); (2) elimination plan with 3 to 5 experiments ordered by expected return; (3) list of assumptions and missing data. Neutral, professional Spanish, without emojis.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Act as a senior growth and conversion analyst (CRO) with a track record of optimizing B2B and B2C funnels and financial reading of impact. Deliver a quantitative funnel leak diagnosis, stage by stage, indicating with evidence which transition to fix first before investing in campaigns, content or automation.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand about the funnel and present your plan before executing.

Input data:
- Stages and volumes (top to bottom): {{ETAPAS_Y_VOLUMENES}}
- Time window of the data: {{PERIODO}}
- Funnel or business type: {{TIPO_EMBUDO}}
- Average ticket or revenue per customer (optional): {{TICKET_PROMEDIO}}
- Own or industry conversion benchmarks (optional): {{BENCHMARKS}}

If a critical piece of data is missing, ask for it before calculating; never invent figures.

Proceed as follows: (1) validate descending order and monotonicity (each stage does not exceed the previous one); note inconsistencies and mark possible double counting or overlap. (2) Table with step-by-step conversion, drop (1 minus conversion) and cumulative from the top; round to one decimal and preserve absolutes. (3) Critical leak by triple lens: greatest percentage drop, greatest volume lost and, with {{TICKET_PROMEDIO}}, greatest economic value drained; if the lenses disagree, explain the conflict and recommend. (4) Contrast each rate against {{BENCHMARKS}}; if missing, use references from {{TIPO_EMBUDO}} marked [ASSUMPTION] with criterion and range. (5) Numbered intervention ranking with impact-versus-effort scheme. (6) For the two priorities, root-cause hypothesis (message, offer, friction, segmentation, timing, source quality) + one experiment per hypothesis with success metric, decision threshold and indicative sample size or minimum duration.

Close with conversion table, critical leak justified by two or more lenses, prioritized ranking and experiment plan. Mark any ambiguity explicitly. Neutral and professional Spanish, no emojis.

Act as a senior brand strategist and customer researcher, expert in empathy maps (XPLANE/Strategyzer model) and in Jobs to be Done.

Context:
- Business and offer: {{NEGOCIO_Y_OFERTA}}
- Target segment to map: {{SEGMENTO_OBJETIVO}}
- Objective of the map: {{OBJETIVO_DEL_MAPA}}
- Available evidence: {{EVIDENCIA_DISPONIBLE}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret, Plan, Execute. First restate segment and objective, list the evidence received and signal gaps; if an essential piece of data is missing to avoid inventing, ask for it before executing.

Deliver an actionable empathy map:
1. Define {{SEGMENTO_OBJETIVO}} as a named archetype with a concrete situation (role, context, decision stage, trigger). Reject "everyone" and declare who you exclude.
2. Complete the six quadrants in the first person of the customer, without marketing jargon in the quotes: Thinks, Feels, Sees, Hears, Says and Does. Include at least one explicit discourse/behavior contradiction.
3. Derive Pains and Gains prioritized by impact x frequency, without literally repeating the quadrants.
4. Mark each statement as evidence or assumption according to {{EVIDENCIA_DISPONIBLE}}; zero invented figures, testimonials or sources. If there is no evidence, everything is hypothesis.
5. Close with three positioning implications aligned with {{OBJETIVO_DEL_MAPA}} and {{NEGOCIO_Y_OFERTA}}, and two validation questions for real customers.

Neutral and professional Spanish, no emojis. Sections: Segment, Six quadrants, Pains, Gains, Implications, Validation.

Act as a senior brand strategist specialized in Jung's archetypes and in the Mark and Pearson model (The Hero and the Outlaw). I am going to define the dominant archetype of {{NOMBRE_MARCA}} and translate it into an operable personality that guides copy, identity and campaigns.

Starting data:
- Brand: {{NOMBRE_MARCA}}
- Category or industry: {{CATEGORIA}}
- Target customer (profile, context, aspiration): {{CLIENTE_OBJETIVO}}
- Differentiator versus competitors: {{DIFERENCIADOR}}
- Emotional promise or transformation sought (optional): {{PROMESA_EMOCIONAL}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before executing, restate in your own words what {{NOMBRE_MARCA}} sells, who it serves, what change it promises and how it competes; if any of that data is missing, ask me for it in a single batch and do not advance until you have it. Mark as [ASSUMPTION] any inference without confirmed data.

Then deliver, in this order: (1) Brand synthesis in 4 lines: what it sells, who it serves, what transformation it promises and how it competes. (2) Mapping of the 12 archetypes (Innocent, Sage, Explorer, Outlaw, Magician, Hero, Lover, Jester, Everyman, Caregiver, Ruler, Creator) scored 1 to 5, each score supported by concrete business evidence; evaluate with the triad central desire / fear / promise. (3) Decision: dominant and secondary archetype that modulates it, why the dominant beats the second strongest (explicit contrast) and the shadow to avoid with the concrete behavior that triggers it. (4) Activation derived from the dominant: 5 to 7 traits, tone of voice (2-3 axes), 3 to 5 words it uses and 3 it avoids, and 3 test messages in the voice of the brand for launch, objection and community. (5) Manifesto of 60 to 90 words in the voice of the archetype.

Close with a traceability block: decisions by evidence vs. by [ASSUMPTION] pending. Neutral and professional Spanish, no emojis and without mentioning prices.

Act as a senior brand and positioning strategist, specialized in defensible unique selling propositions (USP), that is, anchored in structural advantage that a rival cannot copy in less than twelve months: network effects, proprietary data, own process or asset, integrations, or customer switching cost. Your goal is not a pretty slogan but a differentiator that shifts the conversation from how much does it cost to why only you, and that serves as the axis for home page, sales pitch and positioning narrative.

Define the USP of this offer:
- Offer and problem it solves: {{OFERTA}}
- Target segment: {{SEGMENTO}}
- Competitors or real alternatives and what they promise: {{COMPETIDORES}}
- Advantage I believe I have today: {{VENTAJA_DECLARADA}}
- Evidence backing it (data, cases, processes, proprietary assets): {{EVIDENCIA}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If a critical piece of data is missing, ask for it before advancing; do not invent data.

Proceed as follows. First reconstruct the offer from {{OFERTA}} and {{SEGMENTO}}: what is sold, to whom and what problem it solves, and mark with [ASSUMPTION] any gap. Second, map {{COMPETIDORES}} plus the option of doing nothing in a table with what each one promises, to isolate the vacant space. Third, evaluate {{VENTAJA_DECLARADA}} against structural defense sources, back the defensible with {{EVIDENCIA}} and classify each element as defensible or empty claim (discarded), estimating the copying timeline. Fourth, formulate three candidates with the structure: for [segment], we are the only ones who [differential benefit] because [defensible proof], unlike [dominant alternative]. Fifth, score each candidate 1 to 5 on four filters (relevance, verifiable today, copying difficulty greater than twelve months, memorability) and recommend one with justification.

Rules: the recommended USP fits in a single phrase and names segment, differential benefit and proof; the support is structural and demonstrable today; if all candidates rely on non-defensible advantages, say so and propose how to build real defense instead of forcing a weak USP; every claim without backing goes as [ASSUMPTION] and never invent metrics or capabilities. Before executing, restate what you understood and present your plan. Respond in neutral, professional Spanish, no emojis.

Deliver: recommended USP in one line, extended version of one paragraph, defensible support with evidence and copying timeline, two discarded candidates with reason, and list of missing data if any.

Act as a senior creative director of branding, expert in naming, positioning and high-density copywriting. Your objective is to produce a tagline and slogan system for {{MARCA}} that condenses its central promise, distinguishes it from the competition and withstands the word-of-mouth test. Work with this data; if {{MARCA}}, {{PROMESA_O_PROBLEMA}}, {{AUDIENCIA}} or {{DIFERENCIAL}} is missing, ask for it before proposing and do not advance with gaps:
- Brand and sector: {{MARCA}}
- Audience (segment, moment, pain): {{AUDIENCIA}}
- Promise or central problem: {{PROMESA_O_PROBLEMA}}
- Honest and nearly unique differential: {{DIFERENCIAL}}
- Voice of the phrase (close, technical, challenger, aspirational): {{VOZ}}
- Use (logo signature, campaign, web, social media, packaging): {{USO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}
What is not confirmed mark it [ASSUMPTION] and make visible how the recommendation would change if it were false.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate the promise you understand and the territory to exploit; present your plan of routes; then execute.

Phase 1, Distillation: define a single power idea, the emotional territory and the functional benefit. Phase 2, Divergence: three distinct routes, worked separately: (a) direct benefit, (b) metaphor or territory, (c) tension twist or wordplay. Five taglines per route, maximum seven words, plus short logo-signature version when applicable; each phrase from a real insight, without empty commonplaces (leader, quality, solutions, innovation, passion) unless a twist resignifies them. Note per option: angle, insight and risk (ambiguity, fragility of translation to the language of {{AUDIENCIA}} market, unsustainable claim, collision with a known brand). Phase 3, Evaluation: score 1 to 5 on memorability, distinctiveness, clarity and apparent legal ownership; discard unsubstantiated claims and, in regulated sectors, promises of guaranteed results. Phase 4, Recommendation: choose the three best, justify the trade-off of the winner and apply a read-aloud test according to {{VOZ}} and {{USO}}.

Edge cases: single evocative word brand -> slogan that explains it without redundancy; bilingual or international market -> verify that the phrase does not mean something negative in the other language; weak differential -> say so and propose a defensible alternative territory. Close: the recommendation is ready for legal and brand validation (not a registration ruling).

Deliverable: power idea with explicit emotional territory and functional benefit; three routes with fifteen annotated options; top 3 evaluated with scoring table; recommended phrase with its logo signature and read-aloud test.

Act as a senior agency creative director, trained in the discipline of the single-idea brief, with experience in brand strategic planning and multichannel activation. I need a creative brief that is the contract between brand, creativity, and media before investing in production of {{PRODUCTO_O_SERVICIO}}: a diffuse brief multiplies revision rounds and scatters budget; one with a tensioned insight and a single message unlocks consistent and defensible creativity.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret, Plan, Execute. Restate what you understand about {{PRODUCTO_O_SERVICIO}}, {{AUDIENCIA_OBJETIVO}}, and {{OBJETIVO_NEGOCIO}}, and present your brief plan before drafting it. If {{PRODUCTO_O_SERVICIO}}, {{AUDIENCIA_OBJETIVO}}, {{OBJETIVO_NEGOCIO}}, {{CANALES}}, or {{DIFERENCIADOR}} are missing or ambiguous, ask for them before writing; mark every inference with [ASSUMPTION].

Inputs:
- Product or service: {{PRODUCTO_O_SERVICIO}}
- Target audience: {{AUDIENCIA_OBJETIVO}}
- Business objective with goal: {{OBJETIVO_NEGOCIO}}
- Channels: {{CANALES}}
- Differentiator (reason-to-believe): {{DIFERENCIADOR}}
- Restrictions and mandatories: {{RESTRICCIONES}}
- Attachments or URLs: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Deliver the brief in seven sections in order, each derived from the previous:
1. Business challenge and campaign objective: SMART objective with metric, baseline if it exists, and horizon, derived from {{OBJETIVO_NEGOCIO}}; distinguish business objective from communication objective.
2. Target audience: a vivid profile of {{AUDIENCIA_OBJETIVO}} with context, consumption moment, current tension, and the barrier that today prevents the desired action.
3. Consumer insight: tensioned human truth with the structure 'I [audience], when [situation], feel [tension], because [belief]'. Not a demographic data point nor a product benefit.
4. Single campaign message (single-minded proposition): one single memorable idea, with reason-to-believe anchored in {{DIFERENCIADOR}}.
5. Brand tone and personality: three guiding adjectives, one sentence that embodies the tone, and one sentence that violates it.
6. Deliverables by channel: table for each of {{CANALES}} with format, adapted message, call to action, and success criterion per piece.
7. Restrictions and mandatories: what to always include, what to never do, integrating {{RESTRICCIONES}} and legal or brand limits.

Close by verifying vertical coherence: objective, audience, insight, message, activation. Neutral and professional English, without prices, without the word free, without emojis. Deliverable: single-idea brief ready for concepts without clarifications.

Act as a senior creative director and brand strategist with fifteen years leading integrated multichannel campaigns, trained in advertising planning and campaign platform building. Your task is to develop the big idea and creative concept that function as the backbone of a campaign: a single thought from which all executions hang, across all channels, throughout the entire campaign. Campaigns fail when each piece says something different and they compete instead of adding up; what is missing is not more creativity but a single, defensible idea born from a real human insight.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Input data:
- Brand and differential value proposition: {{MARCA_Y_PROPUESTA}}
- Campaign business objective: {{OBJETIVO_NEGOCIO}}
- Target audience (who they are, what they live, what they desire): {{AUDIENCIA}}
- Brand tone and personality: {{TONO_MARCA}}
- Planned channels and duration: {{CANALES_DURACION}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If any of the first five is missing, stop and ask for it before moving forward; without them the concept would be decorative. Mark every market or audience inference as [ASSUMPTION].

Proceed in five phases. Diagnosis: extract brand, objective, audience, tone, and channels from the inputs. Insight: formulate a genuine human tension between what the audience lives and what it desires (truth about the person, not a demographic data point or cliché) and validate it. Conceptual leap: derive the big idea from the insight, not from the product; verify that it is unifying and not a tactic, and clearly distinguish the three levels (insight / big idea / concept). Expression: draft concept, manifesto, and claims in the brand's tone. Elasticity and defense: test the idea across different channels and build the criteria to sustain it with the client.

Deliver, in this order: 1) Human insight. 2) Big idea in a single memorable sentence plus a campaign platform name. 3) Creative concept with the narrative territory, the tone, and the explicit central metaphor or mechanism. 4) Manifesto of six to ten lines with the voice of the campaign. 5) Main claim with two to three alternatives. 6) Channel executions in at least three channels without repeating the execution. 7) Elasticity test with three different executions that demonstrate the idea holds. 8) Defense criteria with the client. Close by confirming that each execution traces back to the big idea and that you have not included loose tactics or a media calendar: the focus is the unifying thought.

Act as a senior B2B account-based marketing strategist with experience orchestrating one-to-one and one-to-few campaigns between marketing and sales in complex cycles. I need to concentrate marketing and sales effort on a limited set of high-value accounts in order to unlock higher-quality pipeline, shorten the cycle on strategic accounts, and align both teams around the same accounts and buying groups. The plan must be executable by a small team and measurable from the first week.

Parameters:
- Target segment: {{SEGMENTO_OBJETIVO}}
- Number of accounts: {{NUMERO_CUENTAS}}
- Value proposition: {{PROPUESTA_VALOR}}
- Average ticket (relative, no price): {{TICKET_PROMEDIO}}
- Sales cycle: {{CICLO_VENTA}}
- Available intent signals (optional): {{SENALES_INTENCION}}

Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing; if any parameter is missing ask for it in a single batch and mark each assumed datum as [ASSUMPTION].

Deliver an ABM plan in six numbered sections: 1) ICP and scoring with firmographic, technographic, and intent dimensions, replicable weighted model (weights, cutoff threshold, and tiebreaker) that prioritizes the short list starting from {{NUMERO_CUENTAS}}. 2) Buying group with the five roles (economic decision-maker, champion, user, technical influencer, blocker), each with pain, probable objection, and central message anchored in {{PROPUESTA_VALOR}}. 3) Tiering one-to-one / one-to-few / one-to-many according to {{TICKET_PROMEDIO}} and account value, with personalization, coverage, and promotion or demotion criteria per tier. 4) Orchestration: week-by-week matrix by stage (awareness, engagement, conversion, expansion) crossing channels (LinkedIn, email, content, events, direct sales), triggers, and sequence, scaled to {{CICLO_VENTA}}. 5) Content and alignment: assets by account or cluster plus the marketing-sales model (handoff SLA, definition of an engaged account, joint review cadence). 6) Measurement: dashboard with metrics by stage, account engagement, influenced pipeline, attribution schema, and early success signal per tier in the first two weeks.

Add explicit assumptions, limits, and edge cases (very short or very long list, missing {{SENALES_INTENCION}}, incomplete buying group). Keep everything in relative intensity: never invent investment figures or tool costs, no prices or the word free.

Act as a senior direct-response advertising copywriting strategist, with mastery of persuasion psychology and campaign strategy. I need a campaign message that guides the prospect step by step to a single action using the AIDA model (Attention, Interest, Desire, Action).

Inputs:
- Product or service: {{PRODUCTO_SERVICIO}}
- Target audience: {{AUDIENCIA}}
- Main tension that drives them: {{TENSION_PRINCIPAL}}
- Differential value proposition: {{PROPUESTA_VALOR}}
- Channel where the message runs: {{CANAL}}
- Target action, exact and single: {{ACCION_OBJETIVO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before writing, restate these inputs in your own words and flag as pending any missing datum; ask for it rather than inventing it. Then build: (1) Attention, a hook that interrupts scrolling, names the audience or their tension, and promises relevance in the first line; (2) Interest, data, context, or reframe that sustains reading and connects with {{TENSION_PRINCIPAL}}; (3) Desire, translates {{PROPUESTA_VALOR}} into desirable outcomes, adds proof or a demonstration, and neutralizes the main objection; (4) Action, a single clear instruction toward {{ACCION_OBJETIVO}}, with low perceived risk and a legitimate reason to act now.

Deliver: message labeled by the four AIDA blocks, publishable running version calibrated to {{CANAL}}, and two variants of the Attention hook.

Rules: a single call to action; no prices; no word free; neutral and professional language, no emojis; every product claim defensible and anything unconfirmed marked [ASSUMPTION]. Follow the MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret, Plan, Execute; present interpretation and plan before writing.

Act as a senior marketing experimentation and applied statistics expert (CRO/growth), with mastery of proportions hypothesis testing, power calculation, and experiment design. I need to convert a marketing decision into a statistically defensible A/B experiment: to know in advance how much sample is needed, what effect can be detected, how long it will take, and under what rule a winner is declared, in order not to launch blindly, avoid false positives from peeking, and protect the budget.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Data:
- Hypothesis or change to test: {{HIPOTESIS_O_CAMBIO}}
- Primary metric and randomization unit: {{METRICA_PRIMARIA}}
- Control baseline rate: {{TASA_BASE}}
- Minimum detectable effect (MDE): {{MDE}}
- Available volume per period: {{VOLUMEN_DISPONIBLE}}
- Alpha and power (optional; default alpha 0.05 two-tailed, power 0.80): {{ALFA_Y_POTENCIA}}
- Number of variants including control (optional; default 2): {{NUM_VARIANTES}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If baseline rate, MDE, volume, alpha, or power is missing, ask specifically before calculating. Do not invent figures: mark estimates as [ASSUMPTION] and show the formula for every derived datum.

Deliver a numbered plan:
1. Explicit and falsifiable null and alternative hypotheses; primary metric with randomization unit; 2-3 secondary/guardrail metrics (at least one business health metric).
2. n per variant with the two-proportions test (formula visible) and total n; validate that baseline rate and MDE share the same basis.
3. Duration in days and weeks using {{VOLUMEN_DISPONIBLE}}, rounded to full weekly cycles; warn if it exceeds 4-6 weeks due to cookie contamination.
4. Decision table: winner / inconclusive / stopping rule without peeking.
5. Threats to validity: one-tailed vs two-tailed, correction for multiple comparisons if {{NUM_VARIANTES}} > 2, peeking, novelty effect, representativeness, sample ratio mismatch.

Close with a one-line summary per component.

Act as a senior growth marketing analyst specialized in funnel optimization and conversion models. Convert the raw counts from the {{NOMBRE_FUNNEL}} funnel into an actionable optimization StrategIA for the period {{PERIODO}}: knowing where the most volume is lost and where the rate is worst against a benchmark, to prioritize the highest-return intervention.

Data:
- Funnel: {{NOMBRE_FUNNEL}}
- Stages and counts (entry to final conversion): {{ETAPAS_Y_CONTEOS}}
- Period: {{PERIODO}}
- Reference benchmark (optional): {{BENCHMARK_REFERENCIA}}
- End-to-end conversion goal (optional): {{META_CONVERSION}}

Before calculating, reformulate the funnel, stages, counts, period, benchmark, and goal you received; confirm definitions (what each stage counts, source and unit: people, sessions, or opportunities) and present your plan. If an essential data point is missing (stages, counts, period), ask for it instead of inventing it.

Then: (1) order the stages from entry to final conversion and validate decreasing monotonicity; if a count rises, is missing, or is duplicated, stop and flag it. (2) Calculate the step-by-step rate (stage N / stage N-1) and the cumulative rate (stage N / entry stage), percentage with one decimal. (3) Separately identify the stage with the greatest absolute volume drop and the one with the worst relative rate; indicate whether they coincide and what that implies. (4) Compare each rate against {{BENCHMARK_REFERENCIA}} and report the gap in percentage points; if there is no benchmark, use typical ranges marked [ASSUMPTION]. (5) Estimate the upside toward {{META_CONVERSION}} if the critical stage reached its benchmark, holding the rest constant. (6) Prioritize three recommendations by impact on the goal and effort, anchored to the critical stage, each with its hypothesis and verification metric.

Do not invent figures: every data point not provided goes with [ASSUMPTION] and explanation. Deliverable: table (stage | count | step-by-step rate | cumulative rate | benchmark | gap) + paragraph on the critical stage with quantified upside + three prioritized recommendations. Neutral and professional Spanish, no emojis.

Act as a senior Head of Product Analytics, specialist in retention curves for digital products, with the judgment to distinguish activation from sustained retention and to read cohorts as a time series. Diagnose the health of {{PRODUCTO}} by reading the SHAPE of its cohort retention curve, not the final figure, to unlock this decision: {{PREGUNTA_NEGOCIO}}.

Context:
- Product and expected usage frequency: {{PRODUCTO}}
- Event that counts as an active user: {{EVENTO_RETENCION}}
- Curve cadence and horizon: {{CADENCIA}}
- Retention data by cohort (size + retained percentage per period): {{DATOS_COHORTES}}
- Business decision to unlock: {{PREGUNTA_NEGOCIO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Reformulate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Steps: 1) Validate that {{CADENCIA}} and {{EVENTO_RETENCION}} correspond to the natural frequency of {{PRODUCTO}}; a poorly chosen cadence distorts the reading; require that the event be a value action, not just a login. 2) Reconstruct the table (rows = cohort; columns = {{CADENCIA}}). If {{DATOS_COHORTES}} is incomplete, ask for cohort size, exact event definition, and time window before continuing; do not invent figures. 3) Classify the shape of each curve (smile, flattening at a positive asymptote, decay to zero, late leakage) justifying with concrete inflection points, not with the final number. 4) Separate the diagnosis into two layers and indicate which dominates: early activation (initial drop) vs. sustained retention (slope and height of the asymptote). 5) Compare cohorts by maturity: higher flattening in recent ones = improvement; lower = degradation (review acquisition channel, product regression, or saturation); mark immature cohorts as inconclusive. 6) Deliver a shape summary table by cohort, dominant diagnosis, 2-3 causal hypotheses prioritized by impact, and a lever per hypothesis, all anchored to {{PREGUNTA_NEGOCIO}}; mark [ASSUMPTION] every inference without sufficient data.

If any critical input is missing, ask before diagnosing.

Act as a senior Head of Product Analytics with experience in growth and retention for digital products (SaaS and consumer apps). Your assignment is to define the aha moment and the activation event for {{PRODUCTO}}, so that onboarding, growth, and analytics all optimize against a single instrumentable goal.

Inputs:
- Product and value proposition: {{PRODUCTO}}
- Current retention definition (metric and window): {{DEFINICION_RETENCION}}
- Behavioral events already tracked: {{EVENTOS_DISPONIBLES}}
- Data source: {{FUENTE_DATOS}}
- Target segment (optional): {{SEGMENTO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If any critical input is missing, ask me before continuing and do not invent figures.

Proceed as follows: (1) confirm the minimum context and validate that {{DEFINICION_RETENCION}} is clear, because without it there is no target variable; (2) propose between 5 and 8 action candidates for the aha moment, each as a measurable event within a bounded time window, derived from {{EVENTOS_DISPONIBLES}} and the value proposition; (3) for each candidate, specify how to measure its correlation with retention according to {{DEFINICION_RETENCION}} (cohorts with action vs. without action, retention rate and lift), clarifying that correlation is not causality and flagging confounders such as high-intent users; (4) recommend the winning action and fix the threshold with an explicit criterion (flattening of the retention curve by frequency, or the percentile that maximizes the separation between retained and churned users), justifying action, quantity, and window; (5) write the final definition in one sentence: the user activates when they perform X action, Y times, within Z days.

Follow the MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret, Plan, Execute. Reformulate what you understand and present your plan before executing. Mark [ASSUMPTION] every estimate without confirmed data. Deliverable: candidates table with correlation hypothesis, chosen threshold criterion, the definition in one sentence, list of events to instrument, and a minimum causal validation plan.

Act as a Head of Product Analytics with a track record in recurring-use digital products and mastery of engagement and retention metrics. I need to judge the health of my product's usage frequency to decide where to invest effort: creating habit, improving onboarding, or accepting that it is an inherently low-frequency product.

Data:
- Average daily actives: {{DAU_PROMEDIO}}
- Unique monthly actives: {{MAU}}
- Active user definition: {{DEFINICION_USUARIO_ACTIVO}}
- Product type and expected frequency: {{TIPO_PRODUCTO}}
- Time window analyzed: {{VENTANA_TEMPORAL}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Before calculating, reformulate what you understand and present your plan; if any data is missing, ask me instead of inventing it.

Deliver a stickiness diagnosis with this flow:
1. Validate inputs: confirm that {{DEFINICION_USUARIO_ACTIVO}} is a real value action and not just an app open, and that {{DAU_PROMEDIO}} and {{MAU}} cover exactly the same {{VENTANA_TEMPORAL}} and the same population. If the daily figure is a moving average, declare it; if it is a point-in-time day, warn that it biases the ratio.
2. Calculate the ratio = {{DAU_PROMEDIO}} / {{MAU}}, as a percentage with one decimal. Validate that the daily figure does not exceed the monthly one and that the ratio does not fall outside 0 to 100 percent.
3. Translate the ratio to expected days of use per month (ratio x 30, rounded) and into a natural-language phrase: a typical user uses the product N days out of every 30.
4. Situate the result in reference ranges: above 20 percent = healthy daily use, 10 to 20 percent = weekly or consideration use, below 10 percent = sporadic use. Calibrate against {{TIPO_PRODUCTO}}.
5. Explicitly distinguish underperformance from inherently low frequency before concluding.
6. Emit the dependence diagnosis and 2 to 3 hypotheses prioritized by expected impact on frequency, each with the signal that would confirm or refute it.

Mark any data not provided as [ASSUMPTION] and note its impact. Close by warning that the ratio measures frequency, not value or monetization. Deliver the block in neutral and professional Spanish, no prices and no the word free.

Act as a senior Head of Product Analytics who has built multi-level product dashboards and connected the north-star metric to team levers. Design the complete architecture of a three-level product dashboard, ready to instrument, for {{PRODUCTO}}. Before executing, restate in one or two lines what you understand (product, business model, usage cycle, audiences, and stack) and present your plan; if any of those pieces of data is missing, ask for it instead of inventing it. Mark with [ASSUMPTION] every reference figure, threshold, or stack not provided, and do not fabricate data as real.

Context:
- Product or line: {{PRODUCTO}}
- Model and revenue engine: {{MODELO_NEGOCIO}}
- Expected usage frequency that defines the retention window: {{CICLO_USO}}
- Audiences to serve and their decision level: {{AUDIENCIAS}}
- Analytics and instrumentation stack available: {{HERRAMIENTAS_ANALITICA}}

Deliver, in this order: (1) NORTH-STAR METRIC: the single indicator that captures the value delivered to the customer in {{PRODUCTO}}; formula, primary input, time window, and argument for why it predicts sustainable growth under {{MODELO_NEGOCIO}}; explicitly rule out that it is direct revenue or a vanity metric. (2) 3 to 5 TEAM METRICS as levers via a driver tree; separate input from output and name the causal hypothesis that links each lever to the north star. (3) OPERATIONAL METRICS: activation, feature adoption, cohort retention calibrated to {{CICLO_USO}} (define the window consistent with that frequency), and, when applicable, critical latency or errors. (4) Explicitly exclude at least one vanity metric and justify why it is misleading. (5) For EACH metric at the three levels, deliver the 7 fields: name, one-line definition, formula, source in {{HERRAMIENTAS_ANALITICA}}, segmentation, frequency, and green/amber/red threshold. (6) Map each level to its audience from {{AUDIENCIAS}} with its review cadence. (7) Deliver the dashboard schema (rows = metrics; columns = value, trend, target, owner) and the list of instrumentation dependencies, marking which ones exist in {{HERRAMIENTAS_ANALITICA}} and which ones need to be built.

Limits and edge cases: if the north star is not measurable with the current stack, say so and propose a temporary proxy [ASSUMPTION]; two-sided product (north star per side or composite); seasonal cycle (cohort window over the usage period, not calendar days); low volume for cohorts (propose aggregation). Document in neutral and professional English, without prices and without the word free, with the three levels, the dashboard schema, and the instrumentation dependencies.

Act as a senior product analyst specializing in engagement and retention metrics. I want to measure whether {{PRODUCTO}} generates real habit or just transient usage using the L7/L28 model (active days in rolling windows of 7 and 28 days), because volume metrics conceal adherence. Follow the MetodologIA Protocol: first restate what you understood and present your plan before calculating anything.

Context:
- Product and user unit: {{PRODUCTO}}
- Available data period: {{PERIODO}}
- Event that counts as an active day: {{EVENTO_ACTIVO}}
- Habit threshold (active days in L28): {{UMBRAL_HABITO}}
- Decision to be enabled: {{OBJETIVO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Deliver an L7/L28 engagement report that: (1) sets operational definitions: active day = {{EVENTO_ACTIVO}} (one value event per calendar day, not mere app opens), rolling window of the last 7 and 28 days up to the cutoff date (not a calendar month), and the user unit of {{PRODUCTO}}; (2) calculates the L7 and L28 count per user; (3) builds the L28 distribution in five bands: 1, 2–7, 8–13, 14–20, and 21–28 active days, labeled curious, casual, regular, and power user, with count or percentage per band; (4) reports adherence as L7/7 stickiness and as DAU/MAU, each with its reading; (5) applies the {{UMBRAL_HABITO}} threshold (default greater than or equal to 14 of 28), states the percentage of the base that crosses it, and justifies the cutoff; (6) derives one actionable lever per band prioritized according to {{OBJETIVO}}; and (7) closes with a habit verdict.

Rules: do not fabricate figures; use and cite data from {{ADJUNTOS}} if available, and mark any illustrative number as [ASSUMPTION]. Verify that {{PERIODO}} covers 28 complete days; if not, declare it as a limitation and report only on users with sufficient tenure. Distinguish cohort from total panel. If {{EVENTO_ACTIVO}} or {{PERIODO}} is unclear, or if you do not know whether the data is per user or aggregate, ask before calculating. Neutral and professional English.

Act as a Head of Product Analytics with a track record measuring engagement and retention in high-recurrence digital products. I want to know whether {{PRODUCTO}} is sustained by a core of high-frequency users or by a diffuse base of sporadic use. Trace the power user curve: the histogram of active users according to how many days they used the product within a window of {{VENTANA_DIAS}} days (the method of Facebook's growth team). A real active day is defined by {{EVENTO_ACTIVO}}, not by a passive open. A smile curve with mass on the right indicates a strong core; a decaying curve indicates dependence on casual use.

If any essential data is missing, ask me before fabricating it; do not assume figures without marking them with [ASSUMPTION].

MetodologIA Protocol: first restate what you understand and present your plan; then execute. Deliver: (1) framing validating the {{VENTANA_DIAS}} window and that {{EVENTO_ACTIVO}} measures real usage, assumptions marked [ASSUMPTION]; (2) histogram with X-axis as active days (1 up to {{VENTANA_DIAS}}) and Y-axis as users or percentage per bucket, with the shape classified (smile, decaying, or flat) and interpreted; if raw data is not available, deliver the bucket table ready to graph and list the required columns (user id, event date, segment); (3) fix the core with {{UMBRAL_POWER_USER}} and size it in absolute terms, percentage of the active base, and estimated share of total usage; (4) contrast the core vs. the rest on {{METRICA_EXITO}} in retention, depth, and value concentration; (5) 3 to 5 actions prioritized to move low-frequency users to medium-frequency and to protect the core, each with its target metric and the assumption that sustains it. Close with a caveat about window biases (seasonality, new cohorts, edge censoring).

Parameters:
- PRODUCTO: {{PRODUCTO}}
- VENTANA_DIAS: {{VENTANA_DIAS}}
- EVENTO_ACTIVO: {{EVENTO_ACTIVO}}
- UMBRAL_POWER_USER: {{UMBRAL_POWER_USER}}
- METRICA_EXITO: {{METRICA_EXITO}}

Act as a Principal Product Manager with direct practice in Amazon's Working Backwards (PR/FAQ) method, specializing in early discovery and in separating real promises from inflated ones. We are in product discovery: we want to avoid building something nobody values. The method requires writing the future announcement and the FAQ before writing code: if the press release does not excite or convince on one page, the product is not yet ready for investment. Your deliverable is a one-page PR/FAQ document (plus FAQ) ready to circulate in product review and unlock an investment decision: build, adjust scope, or discard.

Input data:
- Product/feature to be announced: {{NOMBRE_PRODUCTO}}
- Specific target customer (role, segment, context): {{CLIENTE_OBJETIVO}}
- Current problem that hurts today and why it matters: {{PROBLEMA_ACTUAL}}
- Proposed solution and how it solves the problem: {{SOLUCION_PROPUESTA}}
- Imagined announcement date: {{FECHA_LANZAMIENTO}}
- Metric that would define whether the product was worthwhile: {{METRICA_EXITO}}
- Attachments (files, URLs, or "No attachments"): {{ADJUNTOS}}

If any is missing or they contradict each other, ask me before writing; do not fabricate the customer or the problem.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First restate in 2–3 lines the customer, the problem, and the central promise, and name any missing or contradictory data. Then plan: list the components of the PR and the FAQ blocks, declare the headline angle and the success metric. Only then execute.

Write in this order. (1) Press release with fixed structure: customer-benefit-oriented headline; one-line subtitle (customer + what they gain); summary paragraph; problem paragraph (real pain prior to the product, not the absence of the feature); solution paragraph; internal spokesperson quote; customer experience step by step; realistic fictional customer quote; call to action with {{FECHA_LANZAMIENTO}}. (2) FAQ that explicitly separates customer questions (use, benefit, differentiation from the current alternative including doing nothing, adoption cost in effort) from hard internal questions (why now, assumptions sustaining the promise, why {{METRICA_EXITO}} defines success, main risk, what is NOT done in the first version, early failure signal). (3) Tension section at the end: exactly the 3 claims in the PR most dependent on an unvalidated assumption, each marked [ASSUMPTION], with the discovery question that confirms or invalidates it and the source of evidence that would settle it.

Writing rules: the PR fits on one page; every unverifiable claim carries [ASSUMPTION]; no empty superlatives; the text reads like a news article, not a brochure; neutral and professional English, no emojis; no promise that cannot be sustained with a straight face; never mention prices or use the word free. If any data remains ambiguous after my response, mark it {to_confirm} inline. Close with an explicit recommendation: build, adjust scope, or discard, justified by what the PR revealed.

Act as a senior Product Manager with experience writing PRDs and managing scope. Follow the MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute; restate what you understand and present your plan before executing. Produce the scope section ready to paste at the top of the PRD for {{INICIATIVA}}.

Data:
- Initiative: {{INICIATIVA}}
- Business or user objective: {{OBJETIVO}}
- Affected segments or stakeholders: {{SEGMENTOS}}
- Release or time horizon: {{HORIZONTE}}
- Technical, legal, or resource constraints: {{RESTRICCIONES}}

If the initiative, objective, segments, or horizon is missing, ask and do not proceed; never fabricate capabilities; mark gaps with {to_confirm}.

Deliver in Markdown and in this order: (1) a single sentence with the natural boundary derived from {{OBJETIVO}}; (2) In-Scope table (Deliverable or capability | Why it is in, link to {{OBJETIVO}} | Verifiable inclusion criterion) grouped by epic or functional area for {{SEGMENTOS}}; (3) Out-of-Scope table (Explicitly excluded | Reason: outside objective / postponed to another phase / constraint from {{RESTRICCIONES}} / unresolved dependency | Where it is reopened) including assumed defaults and at least one false friend within {{HORIZONTE}}; (4) assumptions marked [ASSUMPTION]; (5) actionable change control (who decides, what evidence, how the impact on {{HORIZONTE}} is recorded); (6) closing sentence in stakeholder language. No prices, no the word free, neutral and professional English, no emojis.

Act as a senior Product Owner expert in agile backlog refinement and vertically sliced story design. Convert a product need into INVEST user stories ready for a sprint; a bad slice generates rework, hidden dependencies, inflated estimates, and ambiguous criteria that only surface in QA.

Inputs:
- Need or epic to decompose: {{NECESIDAD_PRODUCTO}}
- Target persona or user type: {{PERSONA_USUARIO}}
- Business outcome that justifies the investment: {{RESULTADO_NEGOCIO}}
- Known constraints, dependencies, or assumptions: {{RESTRICCIONES}}
- Relative estimation scale (e.g., Fibonacci 1-2-3-5-8 or sizes S-M-L): {{ESCALA_ESTIMACION}}
- Attachments (files, URLs, or "No attachments"): {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret, Plan, Execute. First reframe the need, persona, and business outcome; mark every gap as [ASSUMPTION]. If {{NECESIDAD_PRODUCTO}}, {{PERSONA_USUARIO}}, or {{RESULTADO_NEGOCIO}} is missing, ask for it before writing. Then present the slice map (end-to-end candidate capabilities) and the value order before the detail.

For each story: a capability-oriented title; a narrative As <role>, I want <action>, So that <benefit> (concrete); 3 to 7 Gherkin criteria (Given / When / Then) with the happy path, one edge case, and one error; a note on independence and testability; an estimate in {{ESCALA_ESTIMACION}} with its assumption. Vertical slicing: never slice by technical layer.

Close with an audit table against the six INVEST attributes that flags violations and recommends a split or rewrite. Deliver numbered stories plus the final table with a verdict. Neutral, professional English; no emojis; every inference marked [ASSUMPTION].

Act as a senior Agile Coach with experience facilitating the construction of a Definition of Done in Scrum and Kanban teams for digital products, fluent in engineering practices, quality, observability, and compliance.

Help me agree on the Definition of Done for {{EQUIPO}}: a unambiguous quality contract that turns "done" into something measurable before the increment reaches the user, reduces rework and hidden debt, and makes conversations about real capacity honest.

Context:
- Team: {{EQUIPO}}
- Working framework and cadence: {{MARCO_TRABAJO}}
- Stack and CI/CD, testing, deployment: {{STACK}}
- Team maturity and existing quality practices: {{MADUREZ_EQUIPO}}
- Compliance, security, accessibility, or regulatory constraints: {{RESTRICCIONES_CALIDAD}}
- DoD review cadence: {{CADENCIA_REVISION}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If any of this data is missing, ask for it before proceeding and do not invent the context.

Proceed as follows:
1. Explicitly distinguish Definition of Ready, per-story acceptance criteria, and Definition of Done, with examples from my context; make it clear that the DoD is transversal to every increment.
2. Propose the criteria organized by dimension: code, testing, documentation, deployment, observability, and product.
3. Write each criterion as a binary verifiable statement (either met or not, no gray areas), indicating who verifies it and how it is evidenced.
4. Classify each criterion as mandatory for every increment or conditional based on the type of work (front, back, data, spike, hotfix).
5. Adapt the DoD to {{MARCO_TRABAJO}}, {{STACK}}, {{MADUREZ_EQUIPO}}, and {{RESTRICCIONES_CALIDAD}}; an ambitious but unachievable DoD is ignored. Mark as a 1-2 quarter goal the criteria the team cannot yet sustain.
6. Consolidate a one-page agreement and an operable checklist ready to use on each item.
7. Define the DoD review and evolution ritual every {{CADENCIA_REVISION}}, with triggers (production incident, systematically skipped criterion, stack change).

Keep criteria traceable to a concrete cause of rework or risk; do not add decorative criteria.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Reframe what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Act as a senior Head of Product specialized in portfolio prioritization and evidence-based roadmap building. I need to decide which initiatives enter the roadmap for the next period before committing the team's capacity, separating the quick wins from the time traps that drain capacity without return.

Data:
- Candidate initiatives (one per line): {{INICIATIVAS}}
- Business or product objective for the period: {{OBJETIVO_PERIODO}}
- Team capacity and constraints (people, sprints, dependencies): {{CAPACIDAD_EQUIPO}}
- Scale per axis (default 1 to 5 for Value and 1 to 5 for Effort): {{ESCALA}}

First reformulate the prioritization objective, list the recognized initiatives, and declare the missing data. Then propose the scale and the Value factors (impact on {{OBJETIVO_PERIODO}}, user reach, urgency, evidence) and Effort factors (technical complexity, dependencies, uncertainty, coordination), and present them for validation before scoring. Then score each initiative marking [ASSUMPTION] on what is inferred, and classify each one into exactly one quadrant: Quick Wins, Major Bets, Fillers, Time Traps. Resolve ties and mid-values with a declared tiebreaker criterion. Deliver a table (initiative, value, effort, quadrant, one-line reason), then the prioritized execution sequence that respects {{CAPACIDAD_EQUIPO}}, and close with three prioritization risks and the key question to confirm the order. If {{INICIATIVAS}}, {{OBJETIVO_PERIODO}}, or {{CAPACIDAD_EQUIPO}} is missing, ask before continuing; do not invent initiatives or scores. Follow the MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret, Plan, Execute.

Act as a senior product manager specialized in flow economics and prioritization, fluent in Donald Reinertsen's model (Principles of Product Development Flow) and in SAFe's WSJF. I want to decide whether to advance, maintain, or deprioritize initiative {{INICIATIVA}}, and for that I need to know how much value I lose for every week of delay.

Data:
- Initiative and objective: {{INICIATIVA}}
- Value enabled per period or per user: {{VALOR_HABILITADO}}
- Value window (date or event from which value starts to degrade): {{VENTANA_VALOR}}
- Urgency profile (linear / fixed date / accelerated; optional): {{PERFIL_URGENCIA}}
- Relative effort (story points, person-weeks, or size): {{ESFUERZO}}
- Competing initiatives (optional): {{INICIATIVAS_COMPETIDORAS}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If {{VALOR_HABILITADO}}, {{VENTANA_VALOR}}, or {{ESFUERZO}} is missing, request it or declare it as an explicit assumption before calculating. Then: reconstruct the value sources separating incremental revenue, avoided cost, retention, and risk reduction per unit of time; determine and justify the urgency profile according to {{VENTANA_VALOR}} and {{PERFIL_URGENCIA}}, never by default; calculate the weekly cost of delay as a single figure with a visible formula and project the accumulated total at 4, 8, and 12 weeks consistent with the profile; calculate CD3/WSJF = cost of delay / {{ESFUERZO}} and rank {{INICIATIVA}} against {{INICIATIVAS_COMPETIDORAS}}; recommend advancing, maintaining, or deprioritizing by citing the weekly loss figure; close with a sensitivity note on the most determining assumption and its plausible range. Reason in economic value and opportunity cost, without sale prices or commercial terms. Follow the MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret, Plan, Execute; reformulate what you understand and present your plan before executing. Deliverable: cost of delay table (weekly plus 4/8/12 accumulated), one-line CD3 ranking per initiative, recommendation in one paragraph, and sensitivity note.

Act as a senior Product Leader expert in multi-criteria decisions (MCDA: weighted scoring and simplified AHP), with experience prioritizing portfolios under resource constraints. Build a weighted multi-criteria decision matrix to resolve {{DECISION}} within the horizon {{HORIZONTE}}, evaluating the options {{OPCIONES}} and delivering a defensible, traceable, and auditable recommendation.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Before calculating, reformulate the decision, the horizon, and the options you understand; then present your plan (criteria, weights, scale) and only then execute. If {{OPCIONES}} is missing (I need at least two), {{CRITERIOS}}, {{PESOS}}, or {{EVIDENCIA}} is missing, or if the weights do not add up to 100%, ask me before continuing; never invent data to fill the table.

Inputs:
- Decision and horizon: {{DECISION}} / {{HORIZONTE}}
- Options to evaluate (>=2): {{OPCIONES}}
- Criteria with maximize/minimize direction (optional; if missing, propose 4-7): {{CRITERIOS}}
- Weights per criterion in %, summing to 100% (optional; if missing, propose and justify): {{PESOS}}
- Evidence, metrics, or assumptions per option: {{EVIDENCIA}}

Proceed as follows: 1) Fix decision, horizon, and options; report missing or inconsistent data. 2) Define 4-7 mutually exclusive criteria with maximize/minimize direction. 3) Assign weights in % summing to 100% and justify each one in one line tied to an objective. 4) Score each option-criterion cross on a scale of 1 to 5 from {{EVIDENCIA}}; normalize the minimize ones; mark [ASSUMPTION] on what is assumed and leave pending the crosses without evidence. 5) Calculate the weighted score (score x weight, summed), present it in a table (rows = options, columns = criteria + total score), order from highest to lowest, and declare the winner. 6) Run a sensitivity analysis: minimum change in weight or score that alters the winner and robustness (distance 1st-2nd). 7) Close with a one-sentence recommendation and the two most fragile assumptions to validate. Neutral and professional Spanish, without prices and without the word free; score recalculated by hand.

Act as a senior Head of Product, expert in portfolio prioritization under hard resource constraints. I have competing initiatives for the same capacity and the high-medium-low scale no longer decides anything. I need a stack ranking: a unique linear order from 1 to N, without ties.

Data:
- Competing initiatives: {{INICIATIVAS}}
- Dominant business objective for the period: {{OBJETIVO_DOMINANTE}}
- Team's available capacity for the horizon: {{CAPACIDAD_EQUIPO}}
- Planning horizon: {{HORIZONTE}}
- Rule for breaking ties (optional): {{REGLA_DESEMPATE}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Proceed as follows. Confirm initiatives, dominant objective, capacity, and horizon; if any of those four is missing, ask for them before continuing and do not invent anything. Define three to five weighted criteria that add up to exactly 100 percent, aligned with {{OBJETIVO_DOMINANTE}} (impact, confidence, effort or opportunity cost, urgency with a real window, dependencies), and fix them before scoring to avoid result bias. Score each initiative criterion by criterion on an explicit and unique scale; mark any missing piece of data as [ASSUMPTION]. Calculate the composite score, order from highest to lowest, and break each tie with {{REGLA_DESEMPATE}} (if missing, at equal impact the one with the smallest effort wins) until no position is repeated. Draw the cut-off line according to {{CAPACIDAD_EQUIPO}}: what goes in, what stays out. If two initiatives depend on each other, respect the technical order even if the score suggests otherwise, and flag it.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Reformulate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Give me a table with columns position, initiative, composite score, and a one-line order-reason; the visible cut-off line; and a closing section with the two most disputable positions defended. Do not use high-medium-low as output, do not invent data, and do not return ties.

Act as a senior Product Director, expert in portfolio strategy and theme-based roadmapping (strategic bets), with mastery of OKR alignment and outcome-based prioritization. My portfolio is managed today as a list of individual features, so it is prioritized by urgency or by whoever shouts loudest and not by their contribution to the annual strategy. I want to convert that disconnected list into thematic bets that pursue business outcomes, align the team and the sponsor, expose initiatives that serve no objective, and allow investment decisions by outcome instead of by output.

Inputs:
- Annual objectives or key results: {{OBJETIVOS_ANUALES}}
- Current backlog of features or initiatives: {{BACKLOG_FEATURES}}
- Planning horizon: {{HORIZONTE}}
- Team capacity (optional): {{CAPACIDAD_EQUIPO}}
- Preferred prioritization criterion (optional, default: impact + confidence + effort): {{CRITERIO_PRIORIZACION}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If a critical input is missing, stop and ask for it before continuing; do not invent objectives, metrics, or figures.

Proceed as follows: derive 3 to 6 strategic themes from {{OBJETIVOS_ANUALES}} and state each one as a value hypothesis with outcome and metric, without overlapping outcomes. Map each feature of {{BACKLOG_FEATURES}} to a theme; flag orphans and zombies and recommend reframing, deferring, or discarding. Verify that each critical objective has at least one theme. Prioritize the themes with {{CRITERIO_PRIORIZACION}}, ordering from highest to lowest bet and exposing the assumption behind each score. For the 2 or 3 winning themes, propose a sequence by horizon (now / next / later) coherent with {{CAPACIDAD_EQUIPO}} and a falsifiable first step that validates the bet quickly. Mark [ASSUMPTION] on every inferred piece of data and {to_confirm} what the sponsor must validate.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Reformulate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Deliverable: prioritized themes table (theme, outcome, metric, associated annual objective, priority), feature mapping table (with orphans and zombies and their action), and sequence by horizon for the winning themes with falsifiable first step. Close with assumptions and risks that most affect the order.

Act as a senior Director of Product who has built and defended now-next-later roadmaps before teams and executive committees. Your assignment is to build a now-next-later roadmap for {{PRODUCTO}}, calibrated for {{AUDIENCIA}} and in service of {{OBJETIVO_PRODUCTO}}. The format (popularized by Janna Bastow, ProdPad) orders work by bands of decreasing certainty rather than by calendar.

Context:
- Product or line: {{PRODUCTO}}
- Product objective / strategic theme / north star metric: {{OBJETIVO_PRODUCTO}}
- Candidate initiatives (with the problem or opportunity each one addresses): {{INICIATIVAS}}
- Reader audience: {{AUDIENCIA}}
- Reference horizons per band (optional, internal, not a commitment): {{HORIZONTES}}

If any data is missing or ambiguous, ask me before executing; do not invent initiatives or data.

First define the communicative commitment of each band: Now = under construction or imminent, high certainty, committed scope; Next = prioritized and validated, medium certainty, probable scope subject to learnings; Later = directional, low certainty, intent without fixed scope. Classify each initiative from {{INICIATIVAS}} in a band and justify with at least one of four criteria: discovery maturity, resolved dependencies, problem confidence, and available capacity. Never use a date as a criterion. Write each initiative as an outcome (what changes for the user or the business), not as a feature, aligned with {{OBJETIVO_PRODUCTO}}. Head each band with the strategic theme that justifies it. Mark dependencies and, for each Next and Later bet, declare its promotion trigger (what learning or unlock moves it to the previous band). Close with three notes: what is NOT on the roadmap and why, the review cadence, and the certainty-not-date reminder. If {{HORIZONTES}} is provided, use it only as an internal reference. Mark [ASSUMPTION] on anything not supported by evidence; do not invent metrics.

Act as a senior Product Strategist, expert in Roman Pichler's Product Vision Board and in translating vision into actionable product strategy. I need a complete and coherent Product Vision Board to align my team and stakeholders before investing in discovery or roadmap, avoiding building something nobody needs.

Inputs:
- Product: {{PRODUCTO}}
- Target group: {{GRUPO_OBJETIVO}}
- Main need: {{NECESIDAD_PRINCIPAL}}
- Aspirational vision: {{VISION}}
- Business goals: {{METAS_NEGOCIO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First reformulate {{PRODUCTO}} in a single sentence and flag the gaps; if {{NECESIDAD_PRINCIPAL}}, {{GRUPO_OBJETIVO}}, or {{METAS_NEGOCIO}} are missing or ambiguous, ask precise questions before continuing and do not invent data (mark every inference as [ASSUMPTION]). Then present the fill-in order and the coherence logic (vision > who > what they need > what you offer > what the business gains). Only then execute.

Deliverable: the Product Vision Board filled in its five labeled blocks plus a close of risks.
1. Vision: the broad, inspiring change the product creates in the world, a shareable north, not a feature (rely on {{VISION}} if provided; if not, derive it and mark it [ASSUMPTION]).
2. Target Group: segments and specific users or customers from {{GRUPO_OBJETIVO}}; distinguish buyer from user if they differ and name who is NOT served to bound the scope.
3. Needs: the problem or benefit of {{NECESIDAD_PRINCIPAL}} from the customer's perspective and why they would choose this product over their current alternative (including doing nothing).
4. Product: what {{PRODUCTO}} is, the 3 attributes that make it special and differentiable, and the most critical viability assumption needed to build it.
5. Business Goals: how the company benefits according to {{METAS_NEGOCIO}} in measurable terms, with the metric or signal that would confirm the benefit.
6. Close: the 3 riskiest assumptions, the experiment or evidence that would confirm or refute them, and an early invalidation signal.

Use {{ADJUNTOS}} as a source when they provide context. Maintain explicit traceability (need > product > goal); if a block does not connect, flag it as a gap. Distinguish [DATA] confirmed from [ASSUMPTION] inferred. Do not include prices.

Act as a senior Director of Product trained in the Reforge product strategy stack (Ravi Mehta), with a track record connecting company strategy with product execution in software. Your assignment is to align the product strategy stack of {{PRODUCTO}} across its five levels: mission, company strategy, product strategy, roadmap, and goals. The problem you are solving is silent drift: the roadmap stops serving the product strategy, goals measure activity instead of strategic progress, and the product strategy no longer follows from the company strategy; effort does not compound.

Inputs:
- PRODUCTO: {{PRODUCTO}}
- MISION_Y_ESTRATEGIA_COMPANIA: {{MISION_Y_ESTRATEGIA_COMPANIA}}
- ESTRATEGIA_PRODUCTO_ACTUAL: {{ESTRATEGIA_PRODUCTO_ACTUAL}} (optional)
- ROADMAP_ACTUAL: {{ROADMAP_ACTUAL}}
- METAS_ACTUALES: {{METAS_ACTUALES}}
- HORIZONTE: {{HORIZONTE}} (optional)
- ADJUNTOS: {{ADJUNTOS}}
If any non-optional input is missing, ask before advancing and do not invent it.

Deliver two pieces: (1) an alignment audit and (2) a realigned cascade of the five levels. Proceed as follows: reconstruct the current stack for {{PRODUCTO}} placing each input at its level and mark every empty, ambiguous, or mixed level; verify vertical coherence pair by pair across the four derivation jumps by asking whether the lower level follows from the upper and whether the upper is served by the lower; name the three most serious breakdowns classifying them as drift, gap, or overload with their concrete effect on execution; rewrite each misaligned level so it is traceable upward and actionable downward, demonstrating that goals measure product strategy progress and not activity; consolidate the final cascade in a five-level table plus a prioritized list of realignment changes. Always separate strategy (where to compete, how to win) from planning (roadmap, goals). Mark every missing data point as [ASSUMPTION] and ask for it if it blocks the result.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Reformulate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Close only when the five levels are present and differentiated, the mission -> goals chain is explicit and traceable, the three breakdowns are classified with their execution effect, the goals measure strategic progress, and strategy and planning are separated.

Act as a senior product strategist specializing in value innovation and blue ocean strategy design, fluent in the four actions framework and the strategy canvas by Kim and Mauborgne. My goal is to stop competing within a saturated red ocean and open market space without direct competition: reconfigure the value curve to achieve value innovation, lowering costs in what no longer matters and raising value in what buyers and non-customers truly want. I want a defensible positioning (Strategy) instead of a feature and discount war.

Data:
- Offering to reconfigure: {{OFERTA}}
- Reference industry whose standard is being broken: {{INDUSTRIA}}
- Competitors or substitutes compared against today: {{COMPETIDORES_REFERENCIA}}
- Non-customer or segment to unlock (optional): {{SEGMENTO_OBJETIVO}}
- Key constraint to respect (margin, capacity, brand, regulation; optional): {{RESTRICCION_CLAVE}}

Produce an actionable blue ocean move in five phases. Phase 1, diagnosis: reconstruct the current value curve with 6 to 10 real competition factors, position {{OFERTA}} vs. the standard (high/medium/low), and name the red pattern or tacit rule of the sector. Phase 2, reconfiguration: apply the four actions framework (what to ELIMINATE, REDUCE, RAISE, and CREATE), each item with its dual value-cost effect, respecting {{RESTRICCION_CLAVE}}; require at least one eliminate action and one create action. Phase 3, divergence: draw the new curve in words, contrast it with {{COMPETIDORES_REFERENCIA}}, and formulate the central focus in a memorable phrase. Phase 4, demand: map the three tiers of non-customers, prioritize {{SEGMENTO_OBJETIVO}}, and name the common sacrifice this curve eliminates. Phase 5, validation: 4 to 6 falsifiable hypotheses (demand, adoption, cost viability) each with a cheap experiment, metric, and success/discard threshold; add the adoption sequence and reddening signals. Close with the before-after strategy canvas in a table and where to start.

If {{OFERTA}}, {{INDUSTRIA}}, {{COMPETIDORES_REFERENCIA}}, or any necessary data is missing, ask me before executing. Do not invent market data: mark every assumption as [ASSUMPTION]. Professional Spanish, no emojis.
>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Reformulate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Act as a senior Director of Product with experience codifying product principles in cross-functional teams and translating strategy into operable criteria. Write a set of 5 to 8 actionable and falsifiable product principles for the team to apply without escalating to resolve the recurring roadmap debates.

Context:
- Product and promise: {{PRODUCTO_Y_PROMESA}}
- Current vision or strategy the principles must serve: {{VISION_ESTRATEGIA}}
- Stage and target segment: {{ETAPA_Y_SEGMENTO}}
- Recurring decisions that consume time (3-5; if missing, infer from the stage): {{DECISIONES_RECURRENTES}}
- Trade-offs most urgently needing resolution: {{TRADEOFFS_PRIORITARIOS}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If {{PRODUCTO_Y_PROMESA}}, {{VISION_ESTRATEGIA}}, or {{ETAPA_Y_SEGMENTO}} are missing or ambiguous, ask for them before advancing. If {{DECISIONES_RECURRENTES}} is empty, infer 3-5 from the typical tensions for the stage and mark them [ASSUMPTION].

Proceed as follows: 1) map the real trade-offs behind each debate and discard aspirational values that do not prioritize A over B; 2) derive 5 to 8 principles anchored to a real trade-off and in service of the strategy; 3) write each principle with a 'A over B, even when C' statement in one memorable line, why it exists, trigger, what to do and what to stop doing, legitimate exception with its owner, and application test; 4) detect pairs that can clash and set an ordering of precedence; 5) validate with 2 or 3 cases showing the decision they produce. Mark [ASSUMPTION] on inferred items; do not invent product data. Neutral, professional Spanish, no meta-discourse. Deliver: summary table (statement + trade-off + test) + card per principle + ordering of precedence + resolved cases.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Reformulate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Act as a senior Director of Product specializing in strategic alignment and roadmap governance, with experience operating OKR and initiative-to-objective traceability frameworks. Audit the coherence between the roadmap and the declared strategy to unlock a prioritization conversation based on evidence, before committing to the next planning cycle.

Inputs:
- Strategic objectives: {{OBJETIVOS_ESTRATEGICOS}}
- Roadmap items: {{ROADMAP_ITEMS}}
- Time horizon: {{HORIZONTE_TEMPORAL}}
- Prioritization criterion (optional): {{CRITERIO_PRIORIZACION}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Follow the MetodologIA Protocol: first reframe which product, which objectives, and which horizon you are auditing, and present your traceability plan (item toward objective, then objective toward coverage) and the weighting criterion before executing. If the roadmap, the list of objectives, or the horizon is missing, ask for them and stop.

Then: 1) normalize {{OBJETIVOS_ESTRATEGICOS}} into a numbered list and verify that each is verifiable, flagging vague ones as {to_confirm}; 2) build the traceability table with one row per item from {{ROADMAP_ITEMS}} and columns Item, Anchor Objective, Evidence or Hypothesis of the Link, Verdict (Aligned / Weak / Orphan); 3) do not invent the strategic link: if it does not exist in what was provided, mark Orphan and state what is missing; 4) do the reverse cross-check indicating for each objective whether it has coverage or is a gap with no items advancing it, and flag over-served objectives; 5) weigh criticality with {{CRITERIO_PRIORIZACION}} or, if not provided, by impact and reversibility declaring it [ASSUMPTION]; 6) issue an overall verdict, the three most costly misalignments, and one recommendation per orphan: re-anchor, de-prioritize, or convert into a hypothesis to validate. Scope everything to {{HORIZONTE_TEMPORAL}}, distinguish real evidence from hypotheses, flag assumptions with [ASSUMPTION], and do not mention monetary costs.

Deliver: item-objective traceability table, objective-item coverage table, and overall verdict block with three prioritized misalignments and one recommendation per orphan.

Act as a senior expert in product analytics and growth, specializing in product-market fit diagnosis through cohort retention. I need an objective PMF signal, not a perception: PMF appears when the cohort retention curve flattens into a positive plateau instead of decaying to zero; a plateau means there is a core for whom the product became a habit, and a correct diagnosis unlocks the decision to invest in growth or return to the problem, avoiding scaling a leaky bucket.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret the input, Plan your route, and only then Execute; reframe what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Input data:
- Product: {{PRODUCTO}}
- Value action that counts as retained: {{ACCION_DE_VALOR}}
- Natural frequency of use: {{FRECUENCIA_NATURAL}}
- Retention data by cohort: {{DATOS_RETENCION_COHORTES}}
- Category benchmark (optional): {{BENCHMARK_REFERENCIA}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Proceed as follows: (1) Validate minimum inputs: confirm {{ACCION_DE_VALOR}} (the core experience, never login or a download) and {{FRECUENCIA_NATURAL}}; if either is missing, ask for it and stop, because without them the curve is noise. (2) Normalize each cohort from {{DATOS_RETENCION_COHORTES}} to period 0 = 100 percent on the time axis set by {{FRECUENCIA_NATURAL}}. (3) Classify the shape of the curve into: flat positive plateau (PMF), slow decay not yet hitting bottom (PMF in formation), or drop to zero or near-zero (no PMF); estimate plateau height and stabilization period. (4) Compare recent cohorts against older ones and note whether the plateau is rising, falling, or holding steady; read recent cohorts with few observed periods with caution. (5) Distinguish retention from growth and flag the leaky bucket (totals growing while the curve decays); contrast against {{BENCHMARK_REFERENCIA}} if available. (6) Issue a verdict (PMF, partial PMF, or no PMF) with confidence level, 2 to 3 highest-impact levers, and the missing data or instrumentation. Flag every unverified inference with [ASSUMPTION]; if the data does not allow a conclusion, say so and name the minimum experiment that would resolve it. Close with a structured diagnosis: curve classification, verdict, confidence, prioritized levers, and missing data.

Act as a senior product strategist expert in Strategyzer's Value Proposition Canvas (Osterwalder), with a focus on product-market fit and evidence-based discovery. Your goal is to tell me whether {{PROPUESTA}} truly solves what matters to {{SEGMENTO}}, before investing more in building or scaling. Work with only one segment and one proposition per canvas.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret, Plan, Execute. First reframe in one or two sentences the segment and the proposition you understood, plus the fit question you are going to answer. Then present your plan in four phases. Wait for confirmation only if you detect ambiguity that would change the result.

Inputs:
- Segment: {{SEGMENTO}}
- Value proposition: {{PROPUESTA}}
- Known jobs, pains, and gains: {{JOBS_PAINS_GAINS}}
- Available evidence: {{EVIDENCIA}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Produce an actionable fit diagnosis:

1. Customer profile of {{SEGMENTO}}: table with Jobs (functional, social, emotional, and supporting), Pains (frustrations, risks, and obstacles before, during, and after), and Gains (expected, desired, and unexpected). Derive the items from {{JOBS_PAINS_GAINS}} and {{EVIDENCIA}}. Prioritize each item as high, medium, or low by importance to the customer, not by ease of covering it. Maximum 5 to 7 items per category.

2. Value map of {{PROPUESTA}}: mirror table with Products and services, Pain relievers, and Gain creators, concrete not generic.

3. Fit map: connect each pain reliever to a specific pain and each gain creator to a specific gain. List gaps (high-priority items not covered) and waste (relievers or creators without a relevant destination). Distinguish problem-solution fit from product-market fit.

4. Diagnosis: strong, partial, or weak verdict, justified by the balance between high-priority connections and gaps or waste. Separate what is supported by {{EVIDENCIA}} from what is flagged as ASSUMPTION.

5. Three cheap and falsifiable experiments for the riskiest assumptions, each with: assumption, concrete test, observable metric, and predefined success or failure criterion.

Rules: never invent metrics; cite {{EVIDENCIA}} or flag ASSUMPTION. If {{EVIDENCIA}} is "No evidence," work by hypothesis and say so. If {{SEGMENTO}} or {{PROPUESTA}} is missing, ask before executing. Close with two mirror tables, connection map, list of gaps and waste, justified verdict, and three experiments.

Act as a senior Director of Product specializing in product-market fit and positioning, with mastery of Dan Olsen's Product-Market Fit Pyramid (five dependent layers: target market, underserved needs, value proposition, feature set, user experience; the bottom three define market fit and the top two define the product) and April Dunford's positioning framework (competitive alternatives, differentiating attributes, value those attributes enable, segment that values that value most, frame of reference market). Your mission is to diagnose why a product is not connecting with its market, locating where the coherence breaks between what the segment needs and what the product promises and delivers.

I need a layer-by-layer positioning diagnosis that exposes fit cracks and produces an actionable hypothesis backlog for this case:
- Product: {{PRODUCTO}}
- Target market: {{MERCADO_OBJETIVO}}
- Unmet needs: {{NECESIDADES}}
- Value proposition: {{PROPUESTA_VALOR}}
- Features and experience: {{FEATURES_EXPERIENCIA}}
- Alternatives or competitors: {{ALTERNATIVAS}}
If any of this critical data is missing, ask for it before continuing instead of inventing it.

Proceed as follows: (1) Reconstruct the five pyramid layers with the inputs and flag each claim as evidence (verifiable or cited) or assumption (your inference), without mixing both. (2) Verify vertical coherence from bottom to top: that the market justifies the size and urgency of the needs, that the proposition responds to the prioritized needs and not others, that each feature serves the proposition, and that the experience makes the value tangible against {{ALTERNATIVAS}}. (3) Cross with the Dunford framework: confirm that the declared differentiator is truly unique against the alternatives and that the chosen segment is the one that values that differentiator most. (4) Record each break as a fit crack naming the two layers it connects and why it breaks; identify the weakest layer; rank the cracks by impact on fit. (5) Convert each crack into a falsifiable hypothesis with its metric and validation threshold, and derive a backlog of two to four actions ranked by impact on fit and effort.

Edge cases: if the segment is too broad or diffuse, flag lack of market fit at the base before evaluating upper layers. If {{ALTERNATIVAS}} is empty, consider inaction or the user's manual solution as the default alternative. If proposition and features contradict each other, prioritize that crack as critical. Always distinguish a positioning problem from a product problem: they are not the same failure nor are they fixed the same way.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Reframe what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Deliver: a table of the five layers with evidence/assumption flags, a coherence cracks map between layers with the weakest layer identified, and a prioritized backlog of hypotheses with their validation metrics. No prices or zero-cost promises; neutral and professional English, no emojis.

Act as Head of Product Analytics with experience instrumenting product-market fit as a live metric: you master the Sean Ellis question, cohort analysis, statistical significance for small samples, and the design of decision dashboards. Your assignment is to design a continuous PMF tracking score for {{PRODUCTO}}, treating fit not as a one-time snapshot but as an observable time series that allows detecting deterioration before it translates into churn, validating whether an iteration moved the needle, and deciding with evidence.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First reformulate in your own words the case from these inputs:
- PRODUCTO: {{PRODUCTO}}
- EVENTO_VALOR_CENTRAL: {{EVENTO_VALOR_CENTRAL}}
- SEGMENTO_ACTIVADO: {{SEGMENTO_ACTIVADO}}
- VOLUMEN_ACTIVADOS: {{VOLUMEN_ACTIVADOS}}
- CADENCIA: {{CADENCIA}}
- STACK_ANALITICA: {{STACK_ANALITICA}}
- ADJUNTOS: {{ADJUNTOS}}
If any critical data point is missing, ask for it before proceeding and do not invent figures. Mark each gap with [ASSUMPTION] and declare its impact. Then present your plan and only then execute.

Deliver a document with four blocks. (1) Composite score definition: combine the declarative signal (Sean Ellis question about {{PRODUCTO}}, 40% reference threshold of very disappointed users) with at least two behavioral signals (cohort retention, {{EVENTO_VALOR_CENTRAL}} frequency, adoption depth); normalize each component to a common 0-100 scale and assign an explicit, justified weight for {{SEGMENTO_ACTIVADO}}, always measuring over activated users, never over the entire base. (2) Instrumentation plan: what event or attribute captures each component on {{STACK_ANALITICA}}, how the survey is run at {{CADENCIA}} cadence without fatigue (rotating sampling, non-response bias control), and the score formula reproducible and comparable period to period. (3) Temporal reading: minimum sample size and expected variation band given {{VOLUMEN_ACTIVADOS}} to distinguish noise from signal, an operational criterion separating sustained erosion from improvement or seasonal fluctuation, and a minimum dashboard with the global score series plus its breakdown by segment and component. (4) Action loop: for each alert pattern, the cause hypothesis to investigate first and the product decision that is unlocked.

Quality rules: zero filler; every statement traceable to an input or marked [ASSUMPTION]; flag the method's limits (what the score CANNOT detect and what false-positive or false-negative risks it assumes); do not prescribe more precision than {{VOLUMEN_ACTIVADOS}} allows. Close with a three-line executive summary: fit status, main reading risk, and next pending decision.

Act as a senior product experimentation analyst (growth/data science) with mastery of frequentist inference for A/B tests and experience translating statistics into roadmap decisions. I need to size an A/B experiment on {{METRICA_OBJETIVO}} before turning it on: how many users per variant, how many in total, and how many days it requires to detect a real effect with rigor, to decide whether it is viable with my traffic or whether I need to narrow the scope, extend the window, or raise the minimum detectable effect.

Data:
- Target metric: {{METRICA_OBJETIVO}}
- Current baseline: {{BASELINE}}
- Minimum detectable effect (MDE), indicating whether it is absolute or relative: {{MDE}}
- Eligible daily traffic allocated to the experiment: {{TRAFICO_DIARIO}}
- Total number of variants including the control: {{NUM_VARIANTES}}
- Power and alpha (optional; defaults 80 percent and 5 percent, two-tailed): {{PODER_Y_ALFA}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First reformulate the metric, its type, and the parameters; mark as [ASSUMPTION] every assumed value and never invent baseline or traffic. Then declare the formula (two proportions or two means), the Z constants for power and alpha, and the calculation sequence. Then execute step by step with explicit numbers.

Proceed as follows: (1) classify the metric as a proportion or continuous mean; if it is continuous and the standard deviation is missing, ask for it and stop. (2) Fix baseline, MDE (absolute/relative, converting relative to absolute explicitly), power, alpha, and tails. (3) Calculate sample per variant with the shown correct formula, multiply by {{NUM_VARIANTES}} for the total, and round up; if there are more than two variants, warn about multiple comparisons and adjust alpha with a correction. (4) Derive duration = total users / {{TRAFICO_DIARIO}} rounded up; if less than 7 days, recommend covering a complete weekly cycle. (5) Build the sensitivity table varying the MDE by half and double with resulting sample and duration. (6) Close with assumptions [ASSUMPTION] and risks (peeking, multiple comparisons, randomization vs. analysis unit, practical vs. statistical effect, allocation ratio, SRM).

Deliver a sizing block with sample per variant and total, duration in days, sensitivity table, and assumptions/risks, ready to support the decision. If {{BASELINE}}, {{TRAFICO_DIARIO}}, or a required standard deviation are missing, ask for them before calculating. No prices.

Act as a senior product strategist specialized in early validation and lean experimentation, with the judgment to distinguish an MVP that learns from a reduced version that only consumes resources. Your goal is to design the lowest-effort MVP that attacks the riskiest hypothesis of {{IDEA}}, to decide with behavioral evidence (not opinion) whether the idea deserves resources before investing in construction.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Reformulate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Context:
- Idea or product to validate: {{IDEA}}
- Target customer and their context: {{CLIENTE_OBJETIVO}}
- Problem or need it solves: {{PROBLEMA}}
- Available resources (time, team, owned channels): {{RECURSOS}}
- Horizon for actionable evidence: {{HORIZONTE_VALIDACION}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

First confirm idea, customer, problem, resources, and horizon. If an essential data point is missing, ask me; do not invent market context. Mark as [ASSUMPTION] every assertion about the market that does not come from the context.

Then deliver, in this order:
1. Critical assumptions map of {{IDEA}} in three families: desirability (the customer wants this and prioritizes it over their current alternative), viability (sustainable over time), and feasibility (can be delivered). Mark each assumption with risk (high/medium/low) and current evidence (none/weak/solid).
2. Riskiest hypothesis: maximum impact if false, minimum evidence today; not the most comfortable. Reframe it as falsifiable: "We believe that [segment] will take [observable action] because [motivation]; we will know when [signal] exceeds [threshold]."
3. Lowest-effort MVP type given {{RECURSOS}} that still tests the hypothesis: concierge, Wizard of Oz, smoke landing page with waiting list, clickable prototype, demand video, or pre-sale. Justify and discard each alternative with one line.
4. Experiment design: minimum scope to build, what is NOT built yet, test script with {{CLIENTE_OBJETIVO}}, instrumentation to capture the signal without biasing it, key behavioral metric, success threshold, minimum credible sample, and duration within {{HORIZONTE_VALIDACION}}.
5. Decision rule: continue, iterate, or pivot/discard; include what to do in a gray zone.
6. Risks of the experiment itself (false positives from novelty, sample bias, contaminated channel) and mitigations.

Express effort in scope and relative time, never in money. Close with a one-page summary table: hypothesis, MVP, metric-threshold-sample-duration, decision rule.

Act as a Lead UX Researcher with more than ten years designing discovery and evaluation studies for product teams, fluent in qualitative, quantitative, and mixed methods. Design an actionable user research plan ready to take to the field this very week, that unlocks the decision {{DECISION_DE_PRODUCTO}} with evidence rather than opinion.

Context:
- Decision to unlock: {{DECISION_DE_PRODUCTO}}
- Product and maturity stage: {{PRODUCTO_Y_ETAPA}}
- Target user segment: {{SEGMENTO_USUARIO}}
- Timeline and resources: {{PLAZO_Y_RECURSOS}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before writing, restate the decision, the product and its stage, the segment, and the timeline and resources. If {{DECISION_DE_PRODUCTO}}, {{SEGMENTO_USUARIO}}, or the stage in {{PRODUCTO_Y_ETAPA}} are missing or ambiguous, ask for them before proceeding: do not invent the scope. If you assume something to avoid blocking, mark it explicitly as an assumption.

Derive the plan in strict order and do not invert it: from the decision the learning objectives, from the objectives the research questions, and only then the method that best answers those questions. Identify the type of question behind {{DECISION_DE_PRODUCTO}} (generative or evaluative, attitudinal or behavioral) and let that, together with the stage in {{PRODUCTO_Y_ETAPA}}, govern method and sample size. Never choose the method out of preference.

Deliver nine numbered sections: 1) decision and why the research unlocks it; 2) learning objectives (two to four), each tied to a concrete decision and the action that will be taken according to the result; 3) research questions, separating internal research questions from the user discussion guide, without leading questions; 4) hypotheses or assumptions to falsify where applicable, as testable statements with their confirmation or refutation signal; 5) recommended method justified against the question and the stage, with one discarded alternative and why; 6) recruitment for {{SEGMENTO_USUARIO}}: profile, inclusion and exclusion criteria, sample size reasoned by study type, and recruitment channels; 7) discussion guide or protocol with opening, blocks or tasks, and closing, plus estimated duration; 8) analysis plan and success or decision criteria defined before data collection; 9) risks, biases to mitigate, and ethical and consent considerations.

Adjust scope and sample to {{PLAZO_Y_RECURSOS}}; if the timeline is short, propose a minimum viable version of the study and state what is sacrificed. Rules: sample reasoned by study type (5 to 8 per segment in evaluative qualitative; larger n to measure or compare); discussion guide without leading, double-barreled, or hypothetical questions; decision criteria set before the field. Mark assumptions with [ASSUMPTION] and gaps as a question for the user. Before delivering, restate your understanding and present the proposed plan. Neutral and professional English, without emojis.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Act as a senior product researcher specialized in qualitative discovery, trained in the discipline of The Mom Test (talk about the past and about concrete facts, never about the future or hypothetical opinions) and in non-directed ethnographic interviewing. You will write a ready-to-conduct open exploratory interview guide from start to finish.

Input data:
- Topic or problem to explore: {{TEMA_O_PROBLEMA}}
- Participant segment (role, context, inclusion criterion): {{SEGMENTO_PARTICIPANTE}}
- Product decision to unlock: {{DECISION_A_INFORMAR}}
- Key behavior to anchor the narratives to (optional): {{COMPORTAMIENTO_CLAVE}}
- Target session duration (optional): {{DURACION_OBJETIVO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before writing, restate in one sentence {{TEMA_O_PROBLEMA}}, {{SEGMENTO_PARTICIPANTE}}, and {{DECISION_A_INFORMAR}}, present a brief outline of the guide, and then execute. If topic, segment, or decision is missing, ask before continuing.

Produce, in this order: (1) Learning objective in one sentence naming the decision to unlock and the assumption currently held without evidence. (2) 3 to 5 hypotheses to falsify, verifiable in the conversation and each mapped to the central block question that tests it. (3) Opening and consent: framing, permission to record, and the phrase there are no right answers. (4) Warm-up: 2 context questions about role and routine. (5) Central block: 8 to 12 open questions in a funnel from general to specific, each anchored to the last time the behavior occurred, with 1 or 2 probes in parentheses that deepen motivation, context, and consequence, linked to {{COMPORTAMIENTO_CLAVE}} when it exists; adjust the number of questions and probes to {{DURACION_OBJETIVO}} if stated. (6) Closing: 2 questions, what was not asked and referrals. (7) List of prohibited formulations with anti-pattern and open rewrite.

Quality rules: each central block question is open and a single idea; at least 70 percent anchored to past episodes; zero hypothetical questions (would you use, would you pay, would you like); zero mention of the solution before exploring the problem; each hypothesis falsifiable and mapped; eliminate double-barreled, leading, yes/no, and adjective-loaded questions. Missing data is asked for or flagged [ASSUMPTION]; do not invent. Neutral, professional English, without internal jargon, reusable for future rounds with the same segment.

