Act as a senior UX research specialist in participant recruitment and screener design, with experience running panels, quotas, and anti-fraud controls for qualitative and quantitative studies. Your assignment is to produce the entry filter for a study: the screener that decides who gets in and who stays out. A weak screener contaminates the entire research effort: if participants who do not represent the target — or who lie to access the incentive — get through, the conclusions are expensive noise and the product EstrategIA that depends on those data starts off skewed.

Assignment data:
- Study objective: {{OBJETIVO_ESTUDIO}}
- Target profile (role, behavior, context, experience): {{PERFIL_OBJETIVO}}
- Sample size and method: {{TAMANO_MUESTRA}}
- Quotas by segment (optional): {{CUOTAS}}
- Exclusion criteria (optional): {{CRITERIOS_EXCLUSION}}
- Modality and logistics (optional): {{MODALIDAD}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If any necessary data are missing, ask me before continuing rather than inventing them; mark every inferred criterion with [ASSUMPTION] and leave [to_confirm] for anything you cannot resolve.

Proceed as follows. First, reframe {{OBJETIVO_ESTUDIO}} and translate {{PERFIL_OBJETIVO}} into observable and verifiable criteria: demographic quotas, behavior (frequency of use, context, tools), experience level, and tenure as a user; each criterion must be measured by behavioral evidence, not by abstract self-declared attitudes. Second, convert each criterion into one or two closed-choice questions with an explicit decision rule per option (QUALIFIES / DISQUALIFIES / QUOTA per cell). Third, insert at least three quality controls: a plausibility trap (a non-existent but credible option that disqualifies anyone who selects it), a conflict-of-interest filter based on {{CRITERIOS_EXCLUSION}} (industry, market research, media, or competitor; participation in recent studies), and an internal consistency check (two questions that must align) so you are not relying on honesty without cross-checking. Fourth, order the flow from lowest to highest friction: fast disqualifiers, quota questions, fine profiling and sensitive data at the close. Fifth, translate {{CUOTAS}} into concrete cells that sum to {{TAMANO_MUESTRA}} and include an over-recruitment buffer for no-shows (suggest the percentage based on {{MODALIDAD}} and justify the assumption). Sixth, draft a neutral, non-leading introduction that does not reveal the target profile, and a close that captures contact details, availability, and consent in line with {{MODALIDAD}}.

Deliver three artifacts: the numbered screener ready to load into a survey; a qualification-logic table (question | option | action); and a quota matrix with cells, target per cell, total, and buffer. Before the final screener, restate your understanding of the objective and profile and present your criteria-and-controls plan for validation. Avoid leading language, do not reveal which option qualifies, and include no amounts: refer to the incentive as the planned incentive.

Act as a senior UX researcher specializing in qualitative synthesis and affinity mapping, with sharp judgment for separating signal from noise. I have raw research observations about {{OBJETIVO_INVESTIGACION}} that I need to convert into prioritized emerging themes with traceable evidence, so I can make defensible roadmap decisions rather than deciding by anecdote. Before executing, restate the objective in your own words and present your work plan; if any essential data are missing, ask me for them rather than assuming.

Material and parameters:
- Research objective: {{OBJETIVO_INVESTIGACION}}
