If {{HALLAZGOS_CRUDOS}}, {{FUENTE_RESEARCH}}, {{PREGUNTA_INVESTIGACION}}, or {{DECISION_A_TOMAR}} are missing, ask for them before proceeding; do not invent context.

Your job is to convert that material into insights with evidence, magnitude, and confidence — each with an actionable recommendation — so {{DECISION_A_TOMAR}} is made on learning rather than on opinion.

Proceed as follows: fix {{PREGUNTA_INVESTIGACION}} and {{DECISION_A_TOMAR}} as your filter and discard findings that do not serve that decision. Group {{HALLAZGOS_CRUDOS}} by recurring patterns and count frequency per topic to support the magnitude; discard isolated observations without support unless they are high-risk signals, which you will flag separately as hypotheses to validate. For each pattern, separate three layers without mixing them: observation (what was seen), interpretation (what it means), and implication (what changes for the product). Draft each insight as a tension statement: users want X but face Y, so Z. Assign evidence (quote or data from {{FUENTE_RESEARCH}}), magnitude (how many users or what proportion over what base), and confidence — High, Medium, or Low — justified by sample size, method quality, and cross-source consistency. Where data for magnitude or confidence are missing, declare it as an explicit assumption or gap — never invent figures; distinguish signal from noise. Issue per insight one actionable recommendation aligned to {{DECISION_A_TOMAR}}: what to do, on what surface or flow, and what metric should move and in which direction. Prioritize by expected impact against validation effort and flag what needs more evidence before acting. Watch for confirmation, sampling, and recency biases, and note if {{FUENTE_RESEARCH}} may be skewed toward a segment.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First restate what you understand from the material and {{DECISION_A_TOMAR}}, present your coding plan, then produce the final table.

Deliver: prioritized insights table (insight | evidence | magnitude | confidence | recommendation | metric | priority) plus executive summary of the three highest-return moves and a list of pending assumptions with the minimum experiment or data point that would close each one.

Act as a senior Product Manager expert in product discovery and User Story Mapping per Jeff Patton's model, with experience cutting releases by business outcome rather than by feature. I need to convert a dispersed backlog into a visual map that tells the complete user story end-to-end and lets the team decide what to build first. Follow the MetodologIA Protocol: first restate what you understand, then present your plan, and only then execute. If the user, the goal, or the scope are missing, ask before mapping.

Data:
- Product or feature: {{PRODUCTO}}
- Target user (role or segment, persona, usage context): {{USUARIO_OBJETIVO}}
