- Team: {{EQUIPO}}
- Product: {{PRODUCTO}}
- Discovery maturity: {{MADUREZ_DISCOVERY}}
- Existing delivery cadence: {{CADENCIA_ENTREGA}}
- Backlog/research/analytics tools: {{HERRAMIENTAS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Before executing, restate what you understood and present your plan. Mark with [ASSUMPTION] any missing data you must assume; if something essential is missing to avoid inventing the model, ask for it.

Execute in four phases: (1) Diagnosis: based on {{MADUREZ_DISCOVERY}} and {{CADENCIA_ENTREGA}}, locate where waste originates today (decision without evidence, evidence without decision, or rhythm desynchronization). (2) Track design: define both tracks and their typical week (participants, decisions); anchor discovery in opportunities and delivery in already-validated items; make it explicit that discovery leads delivery. (3) Synchronization and gates: define at least one sync where discovery feeds delivery and one where delivery feeds back into discovery, aligned to {{CADENCIA_ENTREGA}}, with frequency and participants; define promotion criteria (ready for discovery, ready for delivery) as gates with an explicit evidence threshold. (4) Traceability and health: close the opportunity-evidence-release thread supported by {{HERRAMIENTAS}}, so that given a release, its evidence can be recovered; define three to five metrics separating discovery from delivery; list anti-patterns (theater discovery, hungry delivery, desynchronized tracks, retroactive validation) with their observable early-detection signal.

Deliver an operational model document ready to adopt: map of the two tracks, synchronization cadence, promotion criteria, traceability schema, metrics dashboard, and anti-patterns with signals. Neutral professional Spanish, no emojis.

Act as a senior Head of Product Discovery with experience in continuous discovery, product-led growth, and metrics trees. I want to frame a discovery around an objective outcome—not an output.

Context:
