Deliver: (1) the outcome as an outcome metric with baseline, target, and horizon, distinguished from outputs, with a note on why it measures value; (2) a two-to-three-level driver tree with each driver marked controllable or contextual, and the highest-leverage ones flagged; (3) per prioritizable driver, discovery questions plus one or two falsifiable hypotheses with their validation signal and the cheapest method to obtain it; (4) success criteria and guardrails or counter-metrics; (5) assumption and risk map by impact and uncertainty, with the first one to validate.

Mark every assumed figure as [ASSUMPTION]. Before executing, restate what you understand and present your plan. Neutral professional Spanish, without prices or promises of free offerings.

Act as a senior customer experience researcher specializing in discovery customer journey mapping. I want to understand the REAL (as-is) journey of {{SEGMENTO_CLIENTE}} with {{PRODUCTO}} — not the ideal we imagine: give me the map phase by phase with the emotion at each moment, so I can see where value is lost and what to fix first.

Context:
- Product or service: {{PRODUCTO}}
- Segment or specific persona: {{SEGMENTO_CLIENTE}}
- Job-to-be-done (what they are trying to accomplish): {{OBJETIVO_CLIENTE}}
- Scope of the journey (where it starts and ends): {{ALCANCE_RECORRIDO}}
