MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First restate what you understand about my situation and present your plan; only then execute.

Project data:
- BAC (Budget at Completion, with currency and unit): {{BAC}}
- Earned Value at the data date, or physical progress percentage to derive it (EV = BAC × %progress): {{EV_O_AVANCE}}
- Actual Cost accumulated (AC) at the data date: {{AC}}
- Planned Value (PV) at the data date: {{PV}}
- Cut-off date of the measurement: {{FECHA_CORTE}}
- Assumption about future performance (deviation continues, is corrected, or the remaining work is re-estimated from scratch): {{SUPUESTO_DESEMPENO}}
- Attachments (files, URLs, or "No attachments"): {{ADJUNTOS}}

Phase 1 - Interpretation: re-express BAC, EV, AC, and PV with their units and the data date. If progress comes as a percentage, derive EV = BAC × %progress and state it. If an essential data point is missing, a unit is ambiguous, currencies do not match, or the data date does not align with the values, ask for it before calculating. Mark with [ASSUMPTION] every derived or assumed data point.

Phase 2 - Diagnosis: show the formula before each result and calculate CV = EV − AC, SV = EV − PV, CPI = EV / AC, SPI = EV / PV. Interpret each index in one sentence and summarize the cost/schedule health in two lines. If AC or PV are near zero, warn that the indices are not reliable.

Phase 3 - Projection: apply the four EAC formulas with their use assumption: (a) EAC = AC + (BAC − EV); (b) EAC = BAC / CPI; (c) EAC = AC + (BAC − EV) / (CPI × SPI); (d) EAC = AC + bottom-up estimate of remaining work. Calculate VAC = BAC − EAC per scenario and TCPI = (BAC − EV) / (BAC − AC); if the BAC is unachievable, also calculate TCPI on the recommended EAC. Build the optimistic, base, and pessimistic scenario table consistent with {{SUPUESTO_DESEMPENO}}, each with formula, assumption, and VAC.

Phase 4 - Decision: recommend the likely amount of additional funding (range), quantitative alert triggers, the suggested official EAC formula and why. Distinguish data from professional judgment.

Constraints: do not invent figures; do not cite market prices or external rates; use only the magnitudes provided; every formula is shown before the result; no emojis.

Deliverable: EAC report with re-expressed inputs, index table, four-EAC table with VAC and TCPI, scenario table, and executive recommendation paragraph for the steering committee.

Act as a Project Risk Manager (PMP / PMI-RMP) expert in the PMBOK process "Perform Qualitative Risk Analysis" and ISO 31000. Convert a register of identified but unscored risks into a prioritized and actionable probability x impact matrix on the objective to protect.

Context:
- Project type and context: {{TIPO_PROYECTO}}
- Priority objective to protect (scope / schedule / cost / quality): {{OBJETIVO_PRIORITARIO}}
- Risk register (id, description, cause, effect): {{REGISTRO_RIESGOS}}
- Probability scale (optional; default 5 levels 0.1/0.3/0.5/0.7/0.9): {{ESCALA_PROBABILIDAD}}
- Impact scale (optional; default 5 levels 0.05/0.1/0.2/0.4/0.8): {{ESCALA_IMPACTO}}
- Zone thresholds (optional; default Red >=0.18, Amber 0.05-0.17, Green <0.05): {{UMBRALES_ZONA}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present the plan (scales, zone cut-offs, and data quality criterion) before executing. If {{REGISTRO_RIESGOS}}, {{TIPO_PROYECTO}}, or {{OBJETIVO_PRIORITARIO}} are missing, request them before scoring.

Execution: set scales (declare assumption if using defaults); score each risk from {{REGISTRO_RIESGOS}} with probability and impact on {{OBJETIVO_PRIORITARIO}} and a one-line justification anchored in cause/effect; calculate severity = probability x impact and assign zone per {{UMBRALES_ZONA}}; evaluate data quality (high/medium/low) and flag those requiring validation; sort by severity descending and deliver matrix table, count by zone, critical top 5 with suggested owner and trigger, and list of assumptions.

Rules: do not invent risks outside {{REGISTRO_RIESGOS}}; mark [ASSUMPTION] on every inferred data point; use only levels from the declared scales; no prices and no references to no-cost resources.

Act as a senior PMO manager with experience operating issue governance under PMBOK (issue and dependency management) and ITIL (severity, priority, and service SLA). Design a complete and actionable issue escalation matrix for project {{TIPO_PROYECTO}}, so that no one decides by intuition who handles what, in how much time, and when a problem moves up a level.

Context:
- Project type: {{TIPO_PROYECTO}}
- Team structure: {{ESTRUCTURA_EQUIPO}}
- Level 2 owner: {{NIVEL_2_RESPONSABLE}}
- Level 3 owner or instance: {{NIVEL_3_RESPONSABLE}}
- Tracking tool and channels: {{HERRAMIENTA_SEGUIMIENTO}}
- Already agreed base SLAs (optional): {{SLA_BASE}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First restate in your own words domain, team structure, Level 2 and 3 roles, tool, and base SLAs; flag gaps. Then present your plan in one line. Only then execute. If a critical datum is missing, ask for it before inventing.

Produce, in this order:
1. Four severity levels (critical, high, medium, low). Per level: objective criterion crossing impact (scope, cost, schedule, quality, security/compliance) and urgency, plus at least one concrete example from the {{TIPO_PROYECTO}} domain. No subjective criteria.
2. Per severity, first-response time and target resolution time, aligned to {{SLA_BASE}} if it exists; if not, propose reasoned values tagged [ASSUMPTION], stating business hours or calendar hours.
3. Per severity, escalation trigger with exact threshold by elapsed time (percentage of SLA consumed or SLA expired) and by dependency blockage (days stopped or impact on critical path).
4. Three-level route: Level 1 team {{ESTRUCTURA_EQUIPO}}, Level 2 {{NIVEL_2_RESPONSABLE}}, Level 3 {{NIVEL_3_RESPONSABLE}}. Per level: primary, backup (no single point of failure), channel in {{HERRAMIENTA_SEGUIMIENTO}}, and who is notified on the jump.
5. A single consolidated matrix table: severity × level × time × responsible party × channel.
6. Three golden rules against late and unnecessary escalation; at least one de-escalation or closure rule.

Acceptance criteria: 4 severities with objective criterion and domain example; first response and resolution per severity (unsupported = [ASSUMPTION]); trigger by time and by blockage with threshold; 3-level route with primary, backup, channel, and notification recipient; a single matrix table with 5 dimensions; 3 golden rules. Zero invented project data: what is not provided is asked for or tagged [ASSUMPTION]. Deliver ready to socialize and attach to the issue management plan.

Act as a senior project manager (PMP) specialized in multi-team coordination and cross-team dependency management in programs with multiple workstreams. Build an inter-team dependency map from the perspective of {{MI_EQUIPO}}, within {{PROGRAMA}}, to anticipate cross-team blockages before they compromise {{HITO_PROTEGIDO}}.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First restate the reference team, program, inventory, and milestone; present your plan; only then execute. If {{MI_EQUIPO}}, {{DEPENDENCIAS}}, or {{HITO_PROTEGIDO}} are missing, stop and ask for them.

Inputs:
- MI_EQUIPO: {{MI_EQUIPO}}
- PROGRAMA: {{PROGRAMA}}
- DEPENDENCIAS: {{DEPENDENCIAS}} (for each: source team, destination team, expected deliverable or decision, committed date, responsible parties if known)
- HITO_PROTEGIDO: {{HITO_PROTEGIDO}}
- HORIZONTE: {{HORIZONTE}} (follow-up window; optional)
- ADJUNTOS: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Produce: (1) two separate tables, INCOMING (others toward {{MI_EQUIPO}}) and OUTGOING ({{MI_EQUIPO}} toward others), without duplicating dependencies between them; (2) prioritized block of risks and blockers with mitigation and escalation; (3) follow-up plan with cadence and map update owner.

Per dependency record: source team, destination team, deliverable/decision, committed date, date actually needed, slack in days (needed minus committed), status (agreed / at risk / overdue / unconfirmed), criticality (blocking / high / medium / low), and named responsible party on each side. Flag at risk every dependency with negative slack, without a confirmed responsible party, or without an agreed date. Derive the critical path toward {{HITO_PROTEGIDO}} within {{HORIZONTE}}; distinguish zero slack (critical path, no margin) from negative (already compromised). Per blocker: concrete mitigation action with owner and escalation date prior to the committed date with the level to escalate to. Propagate transitive dependencies and normalize dates with declared assumption. Inferred data goes as assumption; missing data is requested. No emojis, neutral and professional English.

Act as a senior root cause analyst and continuous improvement specialist, with practice in RCA applied to operational and project incidents, trained in the 5-whys discipline and in the cause-vs-symptom distinction. Take the described incident from the symptom to its verifiable root cause and translate it into actions.

Inputs:
- Recurring incident: {{INCIDENCIA}}
- Frequency and observed pattern: {{FRECUENCIA_PATRON}}
- Measurable impact: {{IMPACTO}}
- Available evidence: {{EVIDENCIA_DISPONIBLE}}
- Previous attempts and their results: {{INTENTOS_PREVIOS}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If {{FRECUENCIA_PATRON}} or {{IMPACTO}} is missing, ask for them before proceeding: without them you cannot prioritize or verify.

Proceed as follows: (1) Reformulate the incident in a single precise sentence (what is happening, where, how often, measurable impact). (2) Chain the whys: per level formulate a why, answer with the most likely cause according to {{EVIDENCIA_DISPONIBLE}}, and mark [ASSUMPTION] on anything not confirmed; dig deeper until the next why no longer has an actionable answer or you reach a system boundary. (3) Separate cause from symptom at each level; if causes converge, open a branch and prioritize with explicit criteria (frequency, impact, or ease of verification). (4) Isolate the candidate root cause and define its verification proof: what data/record/observation confirms it and what result refutes it. (5) Translate the root cause into a corrective action with a suggested owner, effectiveness signal, and review horizon; add temporary containment marked as provisional if the ongoing impact warrants it. Use {{INTENTOS_PREVIOS}} to discard previously tried actions that had no effect.

Edge cases: multiple independent root causes → one per branch, prioritized; cause outside the team's control → say so and propose mitigation instead of elimination; insufficient evidence to advance a level → stop and declare what evidence is missing. A valid root cause is actionable, distinct from the symptom, and, once eliminated, the incident stops recurring.

Deliverable: numbered why-chain (facts vs. [ASSUMPTION]) + root cause(s) with verification proof + action table (containment and corrective with owner, effectiveness signal, and review date). Close by noting the most fragile assumption in the chain. Do not invent data; do not propose actions without an owner or effectiveness signal. No emojis, neutral and professional English.

Act as a senior root cause analysis (RCA) facilitator with experience in project management, quality, and continuous improvement. Build a diagnostic Ishikawa diagram to avoid jumping to solutions for symptoms: separate potential causes by category, expose where to look first, and unlock a verifiable action plan. Before executing, reformulate what you understand and present your plan.

Data:
- Problem or undesired effect and how it manifests: {{PROBLEMA}}
- Since when it has occurred and in what phase, module, or area it is observed: {{CUANDO_DONDE}}
- Quantifiable magnitude (frequency, cost, delay, defects, or users affected): {{MAGNITUD}}
- Diagram categories (classic 6Ms or adapted to software/services; default 6Ms): {{CATEGORIAS}}
- Previous attempts, discarded hypotheses, or available evidence: {{INTENTOS_PREVIOS}}
- Attachments (files, URLs, or "No attachments"): {{ADJUNTOS}}

If {{PROBLEMA}}, {{CUANDO_DONDE}}, or {{MAGNITUD}} are missing or ambiguous, ask for them before continuing instead of inventing them.

Proceed as follows: (1) reformulate {{PROBLEMA}} as a single observable, measurable, and specific effect (what is happening, where according to {{CUANDO_DONDE}}, with what magnitude according to {{MAGNITUD}}) and place it at the head of the fishbone; one effect only. (2) Organize causes into {{CATEGORIAS}}; if empty, use the 6Ms (Manpower, Method, Machine or tool, Material or input, Measurement, Milieu or environment) or their adaptation to software/services (People, Process, Platform, Data, External dependencies, Environment). Each category with at least one cause; if one remains empty, justify it. (3) Drill down with 5 whys where it adds value, down to root causes, not symptoms. (4) Mark [ASSUMPTION] on every cause not supported by {{INTENTOS_PREVIOS}} or provided data. (5) Prioritize 3 to 5 root causes by impact and verifiability. (6) For each prioritized cause define a concrete verification action (what data/evidence/test confirms or rules it out) and an owner or role.

Deliverable: reformulated effect; diagram as an indented text tree by category and sub-cause; table of prioritized root causes with verification and owner; closing note with the most likely root cause and the next step. Zero invented data. Neutral and professional English, no emojis.

Act as a senior project director (PMP) with a background in stakeholder management and executive communication. I need a power-interest matrix (Mendelow model) to support a defensible engagement plan before the sponsor.

Project: {{PROYECTO}}
Business objective: {{OBJETIVO}}
Stakeholders (role and engagement context): {{INTERESADOS}}
Current project phase: {{FASE}}
Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before executing, reformulate in your own words {{PROYECTO}}, {{OBJETIVO}}, the list {{INTERESADOS}}, and {{FASE}}; declare any power or interest ratings that are your own inferences (mark them as ASSUMPTION) and present your plan. If {{PROYECTO}}, {{OBJETIVO}}, {{INTERESADOS}} with their role, or {{FASE}} are missing, ask for them before continuing instead of inventing them.

Deliver, numbered: (1) Stakeholder table with name or role, power (high/low), interest (high/low), and a brief justification traceable to {{INTERESADOS}}; criteria: power = capacity to decide, fund, veto, escalate, or block resources and decisions; interest = how much the outcome affects them and their expected involvement. For boundary cases, declare the threshold used. (2) Classification by the four Mendelow quadrants: manage closely, keep satisfied, keep informed, monitor with minimum effort; each stakeholder in only one quadrant. (3) A 2x2 text matrix with each stakeholder placed: vertical axis = power, horizontal axis = interest. (4) Strategy per quadrant: frequency and channel, involvement level, key message for that audience, and a concrete action adjusted to {{FASE}}. (5) Three priority engagement risks with an observable trigger, project impact, and mitigation with a suggested owner. (6) Stakeholders with potential to move quadrant and the triggering event.

Do not invent stakeholders or attribute power or interest without basis; if the list appears incomplete for {{OBJETIVO}}, note the typically absent roles as a gap to confirm without adding them to the matrix. Close by indicating which engagement decisions are ready and which assumptions the sponsor must validate.

Act as a senior project manager and stakeholder mediator, with mastery of PMBOK stakeholder management and interest-based negotiation (Harvard model). Produce a negotiation and agreements dossier to resolve an expectation conflict.

Project and objective: {{PROYECTO}}
Stakeholders (role and, if known, power and interest): {{INTERESADOS}}
Object of conflict (what the expectations diverge on): {{OBJETO_CONFLICTO}}
Firm constraints (scope, time, cost, quality): {{RESTRICCIONES}}
Escalation instance if no agreement is reached: {{NIVEL_ESCALAMIENTO}}
Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Before executing, reformulate in two or three lines the conflict and the parties, and present your plan. If {{NIVEL_ESCALAMIENTO}} is empty, propose a default escalation path and mark it [ASSUMPTION]. If another essential piece of data is missing, ask for it concisely; do not invent data.

Deliver, in this order:
1. Power/interest matrix of {{INTERESADOS}} (Keep satisfied · Manage closely · Monitor · Keep informed), distinguishing declared position from underlying interest; infer interest only if not given and mark it [ASSUMPTION].
2. Prioritized tensions table on {{OBJETO_CONFLICTO}}: each divergence classified by impact on scope/time/cost/quality, with severity and the constraint from {{RESTRICCIONES}} that bounds it.
3. Agreement options (minimum two per relevant tension) that separate people from the problem, expand common value, and are measured against an objective criterion; state the best alternative without agreement (BATNA) for each party.
4. Agreements register: commitment, sole responsible party, deadline, measurable verification criterion, and traceability of the decision.
5. Communication plan segmented by influence (message, channel, timing, sender) and escalation path to {{NIVEL_ESCALAMIENTO}} with explicit triggers for each breaking point.
6. Triggers for reopening the agreement and residual risks of the negotiation.

