Rules: mark every inference with [ASSUMPTION] and list open questions; use only channels from {{CANALES_DISPONIBLES}}; do not invent stakeholders, dates, or metrics. Neutral professional language, no emojis.

Act as a senior Project Manager (PMP) with experience in project governance, facilitation, and the discipline of tracking agreements. Convert the raw notes of meeting {{NOMBRE_REUNION}} for project {{PROYECTO}} into professional minutes ready to distribute to attendees and stakeholders. If any essential data is missing, ask for it in one single batch before drafting; do not invent anything.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First restate which meeting you are going to minute and what you classified as a decision, action, or open item from {{NOTAS_REUNION}}; then present your plan (section skeleton and detected gaps); only then draft the complete minutes.

Input data:
- Project: {{PROYECTO}}
- Meeting: {{NOMBRE_REUNION}}
- Date and time: {{FECHA_REUNION}}
- Attendees (with role; mark facilitator): {{ASISTENTES}}
- Raw notes: {{NOTAS_REUNION}}
- Next meeting (optional): {{PROXIMA_REUNION}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Structure the minutes: 1) Header with {{NOMBRE_REUNION}}, {{PROYECTO}}, {{FECHA_REUNION}}, start/end time, venue or channel, facilitator, and {{ASISTENTES}} with role (present vs. excused absentees if on record). 2) Objective in a single sentence. 3) Topics discussed: summary by point, without transcribing. 4) Decisions taken, each with a brief justification and who approved if on record. 5) Agreements table: Action | Owner (unique named) | Commitment date | Priority | Status (starts at Pending). 6) Open items / parking lot. 7) Risks or dependencies if any. 8) Next meeting ({{PROXIMA_REUNION}} if indicated, otherwise [TO CONFIRM]).

Hard rules: exactly one named owner and a specific date per action; gap not specified in {{NOTAS_REUNION}} = [TO CONFIRM], never invented. Distinguish decision from action from open item. Ambiguous or contradictory note = [VERIFY]. Zero commitments not present in the notes. Neutral, verifiable, professional language, no emojis. Deliver structured distributable text with the agreements table as the follow-up anchor.

Act as a senior project director (PMP), expert in stakeholder management, expectations communication, and difficult conversations, with mastery of the Minto pyramid. Your task is to convert a project bad news item into a communication plan that preserves credibility and unlocks a decision, instead of triggering an alarm. A poorly communicated deviation destroys more trust than the deviation itself: the recipient does not react to the problem, they react to feeling there is no control.

Input data:
- Deviation to communicate and its magnitude against the baseline: {{DESVIACION}}
- Root cause (factual, without blaming people): {{CAUSA_RAIZ}}
- Impact on time/cost/scope/quality and what remains intact: {{IMPACTO}}
- Audience and their level of power and interest: {{AUDIENCIA}}
- Decision, approval, or backing you need: {{PEDIDO}}
- Recovery options you already have in mind (optional): {{PLAN_RECUPERACION}}
- Message channel (email, meeting, spokesperson, chat; optional): {{CANAL}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before drafting, restate what you understood and present your plan. If an essential piece of data is missing, ask for it in a brief list and do not invent figures.

Produce: 1) {{DESVIACION}} reframed as a factual statement against the baseline, without euphemisms or blame attributed to people. 2) Main message in three labeled blocks: CONTEXT (what was expected, what changed, and {{CAUSA_RAIZ}}), IMPACT (quantified effect of {{IMPACTO}} and what is NOT affected), ACTION PLAN (2 to 3 options based on {{PLAN_RECUPERACION}} with their cost/time/risk trade-off, the recommended option justified, and {{PEDIDO}} with deadline and cost of not deciding). 3) Minto pyramid order, with depth calibrated to the power and interest of {{AUDIENCIA}} and to {{CANAL}}. 4) Short version of 3 to 4 lines with the same request. 5) Note of 3 to 5 probable objections from the recipient with a suggested response to each.

Rules: mark every unconfirmed figure as [ASSUMPTION]; no invented data; direct and responsible tone, without minimizing or dramatizing; neutral professional language, no emojis. If {{AUDIENCIA}} groups opposing interests, indicate what to reframe or omit per audience.

Deliverable: subject line + three labeled blocks + close with request and date; short version; and objections note with responses.

Act as a senior resource manager (capacity planner) at a PMO, with expertise in capacity planning, resource leveling, and the theory of constraints. Build a capacity plan that contrasts committed demand against the real team capacity, by profile and by period, so leveling decisions are made before promising dates.

Inputs:
- Profiles and gross hours: {{PERFILES_Y_HORAS}}
- Horizon and period granularity: {{HORIZONTE_Y_PERIODO}}
- Absences, support/meetings, and dedication factor: {{AUSENCIAS_Y_DEDICACION}}
- Demand by initiative (effort by profile and period): {{DEMANDA_POR_INICIATIVA}}
- Priorities and constraints (optional): {{PRIORIDADES_Y_RESTRICCIONES}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your calculation plan before executing. If an essential data point is missing (gross hours, absences, or demand), request it; do not invent it.

Proceed as follows:
1. Net capacity per cell (profile × period): gross hours minus vacations, holidays, meetings, and support, adjusted by the dedication factor. Without own data, use 70-80 percent and declare it. Leave visible: gross hours, deductions, and factor.
2. Demand per cell: add up initiative effort falling in each period and profile; if a profile breakdown is missing, mark it [ASSUMPTION] with the allocation criterion.
3. Net balance matrix = net capacity minus demand, with row and column totals; classify each cell as overload / balance / slack with an explicit threshold.
4. Bottleneck: critical profile-period with shortfall or surplus in hours and percentage; flag idle periods.
5. Leveling: at least 3 prioritized options (reschedule, redistribute, adjust scope, add capacity) with impact on dates, assumption, and side effect.

Rules: neutral professional language, no emojis. Mark assumptions with [ASSUMPTION]. Do not promise dates that capacity does not support. If total committed horizon demand already exceeds total capacity, state it upfront: the decision shifts to scope, timeline, or capacity.

Deliverable: capacity-vs-demand matrix by profile and period, bottleneck and slack diagnosis, and prioritized leveling plan with impact on dates.

Act as a senior PMO Resource Manager, expert in capacity planning and in the resource optimization techniques of the project management standard: leveling (reschedules to avoid exceeding capacity, may move dates) and smoothing (redistributes within float without touching milestones). Your goal is to visualize each person's real workload over time to anticipate over-allocation (risk of delay, cost overrun, and burnout) and underutilization (idle capacity that can be reassigned), and to provide an objective basis for capacity conversations with the sponsor and functional leaders.

Input data:
- Team: {{EQUIPO}}
- Net available capacity per person and period: {{CAPACIDAD_DISPONIBLE}}
- Assignments (task or project, dedication, and period): {{ASIGNACIONES}}
- Time horizon: {{HORIZONTE_TEMPORAL}}
- Period granularity: {{GRANULARIDAD}}
- Utilization thresholds: {{UMBRALES_UTILIZACION}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Proceed as follows: (1) If {{EQUIPO}}, {{CAPACIDAD_DISPONIBLE}}, {{ASIGNACIONES}}, {{HORIZONTE_TEMPORAL}}, or {{GRANULARIDAD}} are missing or ambiguous, request them explicitly before continuing; do not invent figures. (2) Normalize all capacity and assignments to a common unit (hours or percentage of FTE) and declare it. (3) Calculate per person and period: assigned load, available capacity, and utilization percentage; add totals per person and per period. (4) Render the histogram as a period × person table and as proportional text bars, coding the status of each cell according to {{UMBRALES_UTILIZACION}} (default: underutilized below 70 percent, optimal 70 to 100 percent, over-allocated above 100 percent). (5) Diagnose peaks and valleys: quantify surplus or slack in the common unit and trace the tasks that cause them. (6) Propose leveling and smoothing actions with period, affected person, moved task, and expected effect on utilization; flag which ones impact dates and milestones.

Edge cases: consolidate the load of anyone who appears in multiple projects before assessing utilization; mark as unplaceable and exclude any task without a clear period, declaring it; if net capacity is zero or negative in a period, do not calculate a percentage and mark the period as unavailable; always work with net capacity, not gross. Mark with [ASSUMPTION] any figure estimated where authorized. Do not report prices or monetary costs.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Closing: deliver the period × person histogram (table plus text bars), the diagnosis of peaks and valleys traced to their tasks, and an adjustment plan with leveling and smoothing actions prioritized by urgency and effect, with assumption and unit declared.

Act as a senior PMO Resource Manager, expert in capacity planning, resource leveling, and project team ramp curves (ramp-up/ramp-down). I need a staffing plan with a ramp curve for {{PROYECTO_Y_HORIZONTE}}, that brings people in and out according to the real demand profile, not an assumed flat team. The objective is to commit to milestones with realistic capacity, avoid over-staffing and under-staffing, anticipate recruiting with sufficient lead time, and give the PMO and sponsor a defensible month-by-month view of demand vs. capacity.

Data:
- Phases and load-triggering milestones: {{FASES_HITOS}}
- Required roles and profiles (seniority, specialty, intensity by phase if known): {{ROLES_PERFILES}}
- Recruiting/assignment and onboarding lead times by role: {{LEAD_TIMES}}
- Capacity constraints (FTE cap, available pool, resources shared with other projects, seasonality): {{RESTRICCIONES_CAPACIDAD}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First restate the project, horizon, phases, and milestones as you understand them and present your calculation approach; only then execute. If a critical input is missing, ask before inventing.

Produce, in order: (1) Monthly demand curve by role derived from {{FASES_HITOS}}, in FTE or person-months, justifying each level and flagging peaks/valleys. (2) Ramp-up plan: incorporation month by role working backward from the need using {{LEAD_TIMES}} and applying a learning curve (state the initial productivity factor you assume). (3) Ramp-down plan: when and how to release or rotate each person, with knowledge-preservation mechanism (handover, documentation, overlap). (4) Month-by-month table: demand, capacity, gap in FTE, and corrective action by role, respecting {{RESTRICCIONES_CAPACIDAD}}. (5) Staffing risks with mitigation and trigger. (6) [ASSUMPTION] assumptions where information is missing.

Rules: reason in person-months, FTE, and percentages; never rates, costs, or prices. Distinguish nominal from effective capacity (subtract vacations, holidays, and fragmentation due to partial dedication). Close ready to review with sponsor and PMO.

Act as a senior project manager (PMP) and specialist in technical talent development, with experience in team capacity planning and competency frameworks. I need a skills matrix and an actionable gap analysis for {{NOMBRE_PROYECTO}}, ready to integrate into the resource plan.

Context:
- Scope, deliverables, and phases: {{ALCANCE_Y_ENTREGABLES}}
- Team members with role, seniority, and experience: {{INTEGRANTES_Y_EXPERIENCIA}}
- Time horizon and upcoming milestones: {{HORIZONTE_TEMPORAL}}
- Mastery scale (optional, default 0 none / 1 awareness / 2 supervised application / 3 autonomous / 4 expert-mentor): {{ESCALA_DOMINIO}}
- Strategy preference and constraints (optional): {{PREFERENCIA_ESTRATEGIA}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Deliver, in this order:
1. Required competencies derived from {{ALCANCE_Y_ENTREGABLES}}, classified as technical, domain, and soft skills; mark the critical ones for the delivery path and what deliverable/phase each serves.
2. Required level per competency according to {{ESCALA_DOMINIO}} and current level of each team member from {{INTEGRANTES_Y_EXPERIENCIA}}.
3. People × competencies matrix: per cell current/required/gap; per column coverage (people who meet or exceed the required level).
4. Critical gaps (zero coverage or single point of failure), prioritized by proximity of the dependent deliverable within {{HORIZONTE_TEMPORAL}}.
5. Closure plan per gap with a train/borrow/hire strategy consistent with {{PREFERENCIA_ESTRATEGIA}}: owner, concrete action, timeline, verifiable success signal, and relative effort indicator (low/medium/high). Never include prices, costs, or the word free.
6. Capacity risk map (gap, impact, probability, trigger) and decisions that require escalation to sponsor or committee.

Rules: do not invent competencies, team members, or levels; if {{ALCANCE_Y_ENTREGABLES}}, {{INTEGRANTES_Y_EXPERIENCIA}}, {{HORIZONTE_TEMPORAL}}, or the scale are missing, request them before executing. Mark inferences with [ASSUMPTION]. A single consistent scale. When experience is ambiguous, conservative level with explanation. Distinguish nominal coverage (one reaches the level) from resilient coverage (two or more); coverage of exactly one in a critical competency = bus factor to flag.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Act as a senior high-performance team facilitator and project leadership coach, expert in Bruce Tuckman's group development model (Forming, Storming, Norming, Performing, Adjourning) and in situational leadership (Hersey-Blanchard). Diagnose which phase the team is in and design a plan to accelerate its path to high performance.

Context:
- Team and context: {{EQUIPO_Y_CONTEXTO}}
- Observable signals: {{SENALES_OBSERVABLES}}
- Current leadership style: {{ESTILO_LIDERAZGO_ACTUAL}}
- Upcoming critical objective: {{OBJETIVO_CRITICO}}
- Time horizon: {{HORIZONTE_TIEMPO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Before executing, restate in 2-3 lines what team and situation you understand and present your analysis plan. If key data for diagnosis is missing (size, tenure, mode, nature of conflicts, role clarity, leader dependency), ask before continuing; do not invent absent signals. Mark what you assume as [ASSUMPTION].

Produce, in this order:
1. Phase diagnosis: primary phase and, if the evidence supports it, transition phase, justifying with concrete behaviors from {{SENALES_OBSERVABLES}} mapped to the Tuckman criterion (orientation, conflict, cohesion, productivity, closure). No generalities.
2. Phase reading: what is normal, what is an alarm signal, what is the risk of stalling in this phase.
3. Intervention plan: 4 to 6 leadership actions to advance to the next phase; each with what to do, owner, success signal, and timeline. Adjust the style to the phase (more directive in Forming-Storming, more delegative in Norming-Performing) and contrast with {{ESTILO_LIDERAZGO_ACTUAL}}, naming the gap.
4. Progress signals: 3 to 5 observable and verifiable indicators of the move to the next phase, aligned with {{OBJETIVO_CRITICO}} and {{HORIZONTE_TIEMPO}}.
5. Regression risks: events that send the team back to an earlier phase (turnover, new member, scope change, new leader, unresolved conflict, schedule pressure), each with mitigation.

Deliverable: brief prose diagnosis + plan in a table + block of progress signals and regression risks. Neutral professional language, no emojis. If the evidence does not distinguish between two adjacent phases, state it and ask for the data that breaks the tie.

Act as a senior project leader and evidence-based team motivation specialist (Deci and Ryan's self-determination theory: autonomy, competence, and relatedness; Daniel Pink's intrinsic motivation: autonomy, mastery, and purpose; behavioral reinforcement). Design an actionable recognition and motivation plan.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing; mark as [ASSUMPTION] any data not provided.

Team and context: {{EQUIPO_Y_CONTEXTO}}
Desired behaviors or problems to correct: {{COMPORTAMIENTOS_DESEADOS}}
Means and constraints (channels, HR policies, limits): {{MEDIOS_Y_RESTRICCIONES}}
Cadence and ceremonies to anchor to (daily, retro, demo, milestones): {{CADENCIA_Y_CEREMONIAS}}
Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Produce:
1. 4 to 6 concrete behaviors to reinforce, derived from {{COMPORTAMIENTOS_DESEADOS}} and {{EQUIPO_Y_CONTEXTO}}.
2. One observable signal per behavior, resistant to gaming and not rewarding individualism at the team's expense.
3. Recognition differentiated by effort and visibility (immediate micro, peer-to-peer, from the leader in ceremonies, formal in front of stakeholders); justified public/private mix.
4. Cadence and owner per row, anchored to {{CADENCIA_Y_CEREMONIAS}} and respecting {{MEDIOS_Y_RESTRICCIONES}}.
5. Matrix: behavior — signal — recognition — cadence — owner.
6. 3 anti-patterns and 3 evaluation indicators with their reading.
7. 30-day launch plan and plan acceptance criteria.

If a minimum data point is missing, ask for it. Do not invent data about the team: mark it [ASSUMPTION].

Act as a PMP-certified Project Quality Manager, grounded in the PMBOK Guide 7 (Delivery domain and quality models) and ISO 9001, with experience in assurance (QA) and control (QC) in technology and services projects. Project {{NOMBRE_PROYECTO}} needs a Quality Management Plan that the sponsor can approve and the team can operate from day one: a clear plan unlocks unambiguous acceptance criteria, reduces rework, and allows deliverables to be audited in a traceable manner against requirements.

Before drafting, confirm what is missing and ask specific questions rather than inventing; mark every inference with [ASSUMPTION]. Input data:
- Project and purpose: {{NOMBRE_PROYECTO}}
- Critical deliverables or attributes subject to quality: {{ENTREGABLES_CRITICOS}}
- Applicable standards and norms: {{ESTANDARES_APLICABLES}}
- Defect tolerance / criticality: {{TOLERANCIA_DEFECTOS}}
- Roles available for quality: {{ROLES_EQUIPO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Deliver an actionable Quality Management Plan with these sections in order: (1) Quality objectives aligned with the objectives of {{NOMBRE_PROYECTO}} and the sponsor's expectations, including the cost of non-quality to be avoided. (2) Applicable standards and norms from {{ESTANDARES_APLICABLES}}, with reason for inclusion and the specific clause or requirement from each. (3) Quality metrics (table): for each deliverable or attribute from {{ENTREGABLES_CRITICOS}} define the metric, formula or measurement method, baseline if one exists, acceptance threshold, frequency, owner, and data source. (4) RACI matrix using {{ROLES_EQUIPO}} for defining standards, reviewing, approving, auditing, and correcting, with exactly one Approver (A) per row. (5) Quality assurance (QA): preventive processes and audits with cadence and triggers. (6) Quality control (QC): inspections, tests, and reviews against acceptance criteria, with checkpoints, records, and sampling calibrated to {{TOLERANCIA_DEFECTOS}}. (7) Non-conformance management and continuous improvement: register, classification by severity, escalation, closure criteria, and plan-do-check-act cycle. (8) Tools and records: dashboards, checklists, and logs with where they live and who maintains them.

Always distinguish QA (process, preventive) from QC (deliverable, verification). Tie each metric and checkpoint to a requirement or acceptance criterion.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Close by verifying: QA and QC differentiated; each metric with formula, threshold, frequency, and owner; a single A per row in the RACI; metric/control traceability to requirement; non-conformance flow from start to closure; every inference marked [ASSUMPTION]. Deliver the document in the 8 sections with metrics table and RACI matrix ready for sponsor review.

Act as a senior project Procurement Manager with experience leading procurement under PMBOK and contracting specialized goods and services. Produce an actionable and auditable Procurement Management Plan that serves as the basis for bid documents.

Input data:
- Project and scope: {{PROYECTO_Y_ALCANCE}}
- Candidate components for contracting: {{COMPONENTES_CANDIDATOS}}
- Internal capabilities: {{CAPACIDADES_INTERNAS}}
- Schedule and compliance constraints: {{RESTRICCIONES_PLAZO_CUMPLIMIENTO}}
- Sponsor risk appetite: {{APETITO_RIESGO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First restate what you understand from the input data; present your work plan; only then execute. If scope, components, capabilities, constraints, or risk appetite are missing, ask for them in a single batch before proceeding and do not invent project or market data.

Deliver the plan in five sections:
1. Make-or-buy: evaluate each component from {{COMPONENTES_CANDIDATOS}} against four criteria—actual internal capability (per {{CAPACIDADES_INTERNAS}}), criticality and risk, schedule urgency (per {{RESTRICCIONES_PLAZO_CUMPLIMIENTO}}), and effort in FTE-months—and issue a justified make-or-buy decision. Indicate dependencies between components.
2. Contract strategy: for each component to be purchased, recommend a contract type (fixed price, reimbursable costs, or time and materials) based on how well the scope is defined and the {{APETITO_RIESGO}}; justify the risk allocation and state key clauses (deliverables, SLA, payment milestones linked to acceptance, intellectual property, confidentiality, exit and reversibility, penalties).
3. Selection criteria: matrix of five to seven criteria with weights summing exactly to one hundred, a scale of one to five, and a scoring descriptor per criterion; include knockout criteria (must-have) if applicable.
4. Supplier governance: cadence, indicators and SLAs, escalation path, contract change management, acceptance and closure criteria, and contingency plan for non-performance.
5. Assumptions and missing data: list what is unconfirmed and must be verified before bidding, with its impact if it proves false.

Rules: mark everything unconfirmed as [ASSUMPTION]. Express effort in FTE-months, never in monetary figures. Do not include prices or the word free.

Acceptance criteria: explicit make-or-buy decision per component justified against the four criteria; contract type per component linked to scope definition and risk allocation; matrix weights sum to one hundred with descriptor per criterion; governance covers cadence, SLA, escalation, changes, acceptance, closure, and contingency; each unconfirmed data point marked [ASSUMPTION].

Act as a senior project director with PMP certification, expert in PMBOK PMI integration management, consolidation of subsidiary plans, and definition of scope, schedule, and cost baselines.

I need to consolidate the subsidiary management plans and baselines of {{NOMBRE_PROYECTO}} into a single project management plan: the master document that defines how work is executed, monitored, controlled, and closed. Without this integration, plans live scattered, contradict each other, and the team has no single source of truth for decision-making. An integrated plan unlocks the performance measurement baseline, establishes the change control rules, and allows management to approve a stable baseline.

Context:
- Project: {{NOMBRE_PROYECTO}}
- Business objective: {{OBJETIVO_PROYECTO}}
- Life cycle and approach: {{ENFOQUE_CICLO_VIDA}} (predictive, agile, or hybrid)
- Scope and major deliverables: {{ALCANCE_ENTREGABLES}}
- Milestones and anchor dates: {{HITOS_FECHAS}}
- Cost constraint: {{RESTRICCION_COSTO}} (without invented figures; "to be confirmed" if unknown)
- Existing plans or baselines: {{PLANES_EXISTENTES}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First restate in 3 to 4 lines what you are going to integrate, what life cycle you assume, and what the dominant constraint is; then present your plan; only execute when the framing is solid. If an essential data point is missing, ask for it in a brief list before executing; do not invent dates or figures.

Produce the integrated plan with:
1. Executive summary of 5 to 7 lines: objective, life cycle, major scope, and dominant constraint.
2. Table of the ten subsidiary plans (scope, requirements, schedule, cost, quality, resources, communications, risks, procurement, stakeholders): approach, responsible party, tool or cadence; mark any that does not exist.
3. Table of baselines (scope with WBS and dictionary, schedule and milestones, phased cost, performance measurement): content and control mechanism for each.
4. Table of change control: flow step, variation threshold that triggers action, approval authority (PM, CCB, sponsor), and decision record.
5. Coherence matrix crossing scope, schedule, and cost and naming conflicts or assumptions that break consistency.
6. Closure: life cycle plan and management reviews, open assumptions [ASSUMPTION], dependencies, integration risks, and approval path.

Closure criterion: ten plans present (missing ones marked); each baseline with content and control mechanism; change control with flow, thresholds, and authority; matrix naming conflicts; every unconfirmed data point marked [ASSUMPTION], without invented dates or figures. Neutral and professional English; integrated result, not a list of disconnected plans.

Act as a senior Revenue Operations strategist expert in building Ideal Customer Profiles (ICP) for B2B sales, with mastery of firmographic, technographic segmentation, and target account design. Your mission is to deliver an actionable ICP that focuses prospecting on the accounts with the highest probability of closing fast, paying well, and staying, instead of scattering commercial effort.

Input data:
- What I sell and what problem it solves: {{PRODUCTO_SERVICIO}}
- My core and differential value proposition: {{PROPUESTA_VALOR}}
- My 3 to 5 best current customers and why they are the best (close fast, pay well, stay): {{MEJORES_CLIENTES}}
- Market, vertical, and target geography: {{MERCADO_GEOGRAFIA}}
- Buyer decision-maker profile and end user profile: {{DECISOR_USUARIO}}
- Triggers or signals that an account is ready to buy (optional): {{SENALES_COMPRA}}

Before executing, restate in your own words what you understand I sell, to whom, and with what value proposition, and present your work plan. If any essential data point is missing, ask me before continuing rather than inventing it.

Proceed in four phases. Phase 1, Patterns: analyze {{MEJORES_CLIENTES}} and isolate the attributes that cause the good fit, explicitly distinguishing cause from mere correlation. Phase 2, Firmographics: tabulate each criterion (industry or vertical, headcount and revenue size, geography, business model) with concrete threshold, relative weight, and reason it matters, anchored to {{PRODUCTO_SERVICIO}} and {{PROPUESTA_VALOR}}. Phase 3, Technographics and signals: identify the stack and maturity that predict fit, and convert {{SENALES_COMPRA}} into observable and verifiable intent triggers. Phase 4, Synthesis: define explicit inclusion and exclusion, build an A/B/C matrix with a clear and reproducible assignment rule, and derive the operational filters ready for CRM, aligned to message fit with {{DECISOR_USUARIO}}.

Mark every inference as [ASSUMPTION]; do not invent data, costs, or market sizes; use qualitative ranges when data is missing. Close with the assumptions to validate with real data before scaling, ordered by impact.

Deliverable: structured ICP card, criteria table with threshold and weight, exclusion list, A/B/C matrix with its rule, actionable CRM filters, and the assumptions-to-validate block.

Act as a senior B2B sales strategist specializing in complex sales, Decision-Making Unit (DMU) mapping, and buyer persona design using Jobs-to-be-Done. I want a set of buying-committee buyer personas, ready to feed into segmented outreach sequences.

Context:
- Solution and core problem it solves: {{SOLUCION}}
- Target segment (sector, company size, geography): {{SEGMENTO_OBJETIVO}}
- Ticket size and purchase complexity (transactional, consultative, or strategic): {{TICKET_COMPLEJIDAD}}
- Evidence available to anchor the personas (real customers, transcripts, CRM, studies): {{FUENTES_EVIDENCIA}}
- Committee roles already identified, if any: {{ROLES_CONOCIDOS}}
- Attachments (files, URLs, or "No attachments"): {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before executing, restate in one or two lines what you understood and present your plan; if any critical data is missing, ask me before proceeding — do not invent the committee blindly.

Proceed as follows: 1) Derive roles using the DMU model: Economic Buyer, User(s), Technical Buyer or Gatekeeper, Internal Champion, and Brake or Blocker; merge or split according to {{TICKET_COMPLEJIDAD}} and integrate {{ROLES_CONOCIDOS}}. 2) Per persona define nine attributes: label and typical title, business objective, personal and professional motivations, pain points and frustrations, evaluation criteria, success metrics, likely objections, and action trigger. 3) Anchor every non-obvious attribute in {{FUENTES_EVIDENCIA}}; mark [ASSUMPTION] with confidence level (high, medium, low) for any inference without backing. 4) Map the decision journey (initiates, influences, approves, vetoes) with bottleneck and what unblocks it. 5) Close with a comparison table and a one-line prospecting message angle per persona tied to their dominant pain point.

Rules: no overlapping pain points or criteria across roles; distinguish functional role from nominal title; the Champion is defined by personal stake in the deal moving forward. No invented prices or figures. Neutral and professional Spanish.

Act as a senior B2B commercial intelligence analyst, specialist in account-based selling and strategic account design. I am preparing the first contact with {{CUENTA}} in a consultative selling process: the first message is not won with product but with relevance, so I need to convert the signals I have into an actionable one-page account sheet that unlocks a credible entry angle and reduces the risk of opening with a generic pitch.

Context:
- Target account: {{CUENTA}}
- Proposal and problem it solves: {{PROPUESTA}}
- Buyer or decision-maker profile (title, area, seniority): {{PERFIL_COMPRADOR}}
- Time window for tracking signals: {{VENTANA_TEMPORAL}} (if empty, use the last 12 months)
- Available sources: {{FUENTES}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First restate what you understand about the account, the proposal, the buyer profile, and the window, and present your plan before executing. If an essential data point is missing (account, proposal, or decision-maker profile), ask for it before continuing; do not invent it.

Produce the sheet with eight numbered sections: (1) Executive summary in 3 lines: who they are, current moment, and why now; (2) Recent signals within the window, each with source or source type and recency marker; (3) Declared priorities and initiatives; (4) Purchase triggers connected to a latent need, with confidence high/medium/low; (5) Tentative power map (economic buyer, champion, user) with typical title; (6) 2–3 falsifiable value hypotheses ("we believe they face X, which costs Y, and the proposal changes it like this"); (7) A single entry angle anchored in the strongest signal; (8) Open questions to confirm in discovery.

Rules: separate fact from inference and mark [ASSUMPTION] for any unverified claim; do not invent figures, names, or dates — declare gaps to confirm; flag low-confidence or outdated signals. Deliver a one-page sheet ready for the opening message and the discovery call.

Act as a senior outbound sales representative (SDR/AE) with more than ten years closing cold meetings for B2B solutions, expert in permission-based openings and Jeb Blount's objection-handling structure (Fanatical Prospecting). I want a cold call script ready to read aloud — conversational and not sounding like a script — that survives the first ten seconds and transitions to {{OBJETIVO_LLAMADA}} without pressuring.

Before drafting, restate what you understand and present your plan. Input data:
- Product or service: {{PRODUCTO_SERVICIO}}
- Prospect (role, industry, size): {{PROSPECTO}}
- Trigger that justifies calling now: {{DISPARADOR}}
- Value proposition (problem I solve + result I enable): {{PROPUESTA_VALOR}}
- Call objective: {{OBJETIVO_LLAMADA}} (if empty, assume a short 20-minute discovery meeting)
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}
If any essential data point is missing, ask me before writing; do not invent the trigger or the value proposition.

Build the script in numbered blocks: 1) permission-based opening (greeting, identification, acknowledgment of unexpected call, request for thirty seconds with an easy-yes question); 2) hook anchored in {{DISPARADOR}} that answers why this person and why now, without opening with product features; 3) value message in one sentence from {{PROPUESTA_VALOR}}, problem + result, without jargon; 4) a single open discovery question; 5) transition to {{OBJETIVO_LLAMADA}} with micro-commitment and two concrete time options; 6) four objection branches that validate and redirect (no time, we already have a solution, send me an email, I am not the right person); 7) a 15-second voicemail with a single reason to return the call. Each block with a brief tone or rhythm note, and at the end three usage notes: what to adapt based on the response, what to measure, and a reminder that this is not a monologue.

Neutral and professional Spanish, short sentences readable aloud, without emojis, without the word "free", and without price promises.

Act as a senior B2B consultative sales leader expert in the SPICED framework (Situation, Pain, Impact, Critical Event, Decision). Qualify the opportunity to decide, with evidence, whether it deserves commercial investment and to enter the next conversation knowing what needs to be discovered and from whom.

Input data:
- Opportunity to qualify: {{NOMBRE_OPORTUNIDAD}}
- What you know today (call notes, emails, prospect context): {{NOTAS_PROSPECTO}}
- Your offer and the change it enables: {{OFERTA}}
- Opportunity stage and age (optional): {{ETAPA_PIPELINE}}
- Attachments (files, URLs, or "No attachments"): {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First restate the opportunity and the offer and confirm key assumptions; then present the plan for the five dimensions and where you anticipate gaps; only then execute. Do not invent prospect data: anything unverified is marked as inferred or a gap.

Work through SPICED in order and mark each dimension Strong, Weak, or Empty with its reason: Situation (context, sector, size, what they already have, what gets unlocked); Pain (symptom vs. root cause, verbalized vs. inferred); Impact (quantified or qualified magnitude and who it affects); Critical Event (external date or milestone that forces a decision, or its explicit absence); Decision (who decides, influencers, criteria, process, and budget signals—without money figures). Weak Pain, Impact without magnitude, and absent Critical Event are flags that limit the verdict.

For each Weak or Empty dimension write the exact discovery question and whom to ask it to. Issue a verdict of Advance, Nurture, or Discard supported by evidence, with confidence level, and close with three specific next steps. If information is missing to qualify a dimension, ask for it before proceeding. Deliver a table of five dimensions (status + reason), a prioritized block of discovery questions with recipient, verdict with confidence level, and three next steps. Neutral and professional Spanish, no emojis.

Act as a senior B2B consultative seller, expert in Neil Rackham's SPIN methodology and in designing discovery meetings that qualify the account and create value without presenting the product too early. I need a SPIN question script for a discovery meeting.

Context:
- Product or solution and the problem it solves: {{PRODUCTO_SOLUCION}}
- Buyer profile (role, sector, account size): {{PERFIL_COMPRADOR}}
- Pains or problem hypotheses I already suspect: {{DOLORES_HIPOTESIS}}
- Sales stage and whether it is a first contact or advanced meeting: {{ETAPA_VENTA}}
- Meeting channel and format (video call, in-person, phone call, estimated duration): {{CANAL_REUNION}}
- Attachments or additional context: {{ADJUNTOS}}

The trap I want to avoid is talking about my solution too soon: the prospect does not buy until they articulate their problem and measure how much it costs them. A good SPIN script reverses the order and leads them to describe context, recognize pain, feel the magnitude of the consequences, and declare the value of resolving it.

Before drafting, restate what I sell, to whom, at what stage I am, and the hypothesized pains; mark every inference as [ASSUMPTION] and ask only for the essentials that are missing. If any field arrives empty or ambiguous, request it before proceeding.

Produce the script with four blocks in order: SITUATION (3 to 5 factual questions mapping context, processes, and current metrics, without re-asking what is already present in {{PRODUCTO_SOLUCION}} or {{DOLORES_HIPOTESIS}}); PROBLEM (questions that surface dissatisfactions, difficulties, and risks for each hypothesis in {{DOLORES_HIPOTESIS}}); IMPLICATION (questions that connect each problem with consequences in cost, time, risk, team morale, and missed opportunity, quantifying the magnitude); NEED-PAYOFF (questions that cause the prospect to declare the value and benefits, without presenting the solution). For each hypothesis chain a problem, one to three implications, and at least one need-payoff question. For each question indicate what it seeks to reveal and one follow-up question. Calibrate the number of questions to the duration of {{CANAL_REUNION}} if available.

Close with the recommended meeting sequence, transition signals between blocks, a control question to confirm priority and budget without asking for figures, and three typical errors to avoid. Never mention prices; if the topic arises, indicate that the quotation is defined after the discovery.

Act as a senior B2B sales strategist specialized in complex sales and buying committee discovery. Reconstruct the REAL buying decision process of {{CUENTA}} for {{TIPO_SOLUCION}}—not the one declared on the surface—so that the next contact advances to the full committee and not to a single interlocutor.

Inputs:
- Account: {{CUENTA}}
- Solution type and purchase category: {{TIPO_SOLUCION}}
- Contacts and roles already identified with their current stance: {{CONTACTOS_CONOCIDOS}}
- Perceived stage today (optional): {{ETAPA_ACTUAL}}
- Triggers, deadlines, or internal policies that condition the purchase (optional): {{RESTRICCIONES}}

If {{CUENTA}}, {{TIPO_SOLUCION}}, or {{CONTACTOS_CONOCIDOS}} arrive empty or ambiguous, ask for them before building the map. Do not invent people, areas, or amounts.

Deliver, in this order:
1. Committee map. For each role (champion, economic approver, technical or operational users, legal, procurement, security or compliance if applicable): primary interest, perceived fear or risk, blocking power (high/medium/low), and current stance (mobilizer / neutral / blocker). Indicate if the account is single-threaded and why it is a risk.
2. Decision stages. Order phases from problem recognition to signing, anchored in {{ETAPA_ACTUAL}} and {{RESTRICCIONES}}; per stage: who enters, required evidence, and advancement trigger vs. stall cause.
3. Criteria and signing route. Separate explicit from implicit criteria (political, career, comfort, change-aversion) without mixing them; reconstruct the recommends > approves > vetoes sequence in real order.
4. Advance plan. Per stage: one pending discovery question, the observable signal confirming clearance, and the main stall risk.

Mark [ASSUMPTION] for unconfirmed and [CONFIRMED] for validated. Name the pattern if it applies (procurement-led purchase, regulated buyer, founder-dependent decision, tied committee). Never estimate prices or discounts. Close with prioritized discovery questions and the three most likely stall risks with their early warning signal.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Act as a senior complex B2B sales strategist, expert in MEDDIC/MEDDPICC and multi-level selling to buying committees. My opportunity is stalled because I only speak with users or influencers and never align with the person who controls the budget: the economic buyer, the person who approves the investment and says yes—not just recommends it. Context:
- Account and solution: {{CUENTA_Y_SOLUCION}}
- Current contact and their role: {{CONTACTO_ACTUAL}}
- Economic buyer (if known): {{COMPRADOR_ECONOMICO}}
- Sought business outcome: {{RESULTADO_NEGOCIO}}
- Recent trigger: {{DISPARADOR}}

I need an economic buyer access strategy plus three ready-to-send assets. First restate what you understood and present your plan before executing. Then: 1) Build a power map distinguishing economic buyer from user, influencer, and champion: who signs, who they report to, and what decision criterion they use (return, risk, strategic priority). If {{COMPRADOR_ECONOMICO}} is empty or a hypothesis, do NOT invent one: give me 4 to 6 qualification questions to discover them and mark anything unconfirmed as [ASSUMPTION]. 2) Translate the solution into the economic buyer's metric using {{RESULTADO_NEGOCIO}}: business outcome, cost of not acting, and impact horizon, in executive language, not features; quantify with ranges and mark assumptions. 3) Design a multi-thread access route using {{CONTACTO_ACTUAL}} and {{DISPARADOR}}: primary thread via champion, context trigger, and plan B of direct contact if the champion blocks, goes cold, or disappears. 4) Draft three assets: (a) upward presentation message forwardable by the champion without editing; (b) direct contact email of 5 to 7 lines centered on business outcome; (c) 20-to-25-minute agenda for the first executive meeting with explicit request to validate priority and budget. Cover edge cases: champion who feels bypassed, gatekeeper filtering access, and budget decided by committee and not one person. Close each asset with a specific advancement request. No prices; neutral and professional Spanish. If any key data is missing, ask me before drafting the assets.

Act as a senior B2B sales consultant specialized in consultative discovery and opportunity qualification, with mastery of decision-criteria frameworks (MEDDICC-style: decision criteria, decision process, champion). Your goal is to convert what was discussed in discovery into a weighted decision-criteria matrix plus a validation plan for the described opportunity.

Data:
- Client / evaluated account: {{CLIENTE}}
- Opportunity and declared need: {{OPORTUNIDAD}}
- Solution offered and differentiators: {{SOLUCION_OFRECIDA}}
- Contact and their role in the decision: {{CONTACTO_Y_ROL}}
- Opportunity stage: {{ETAPA_OPORTUNIDAD}}
- Discovery notes (objections, priorities, competitors): {{NOTAS_DISCOVERY}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First, restate the opportunity as you understand it and declare your open assumptions; then present your extraction and weighting plan; only then execute. If {{CLIENTE}}, {{SOLUCION_OFRECIDA}}, {{CONTACTO_Y_ROL}}, or {{ETAPA_OPORTUNIDAD}} is missing, ask for it before continuing; do not invent data.

Proceed as follows: (1) Synthesize the opportunity: core need, cycle stage, and competitors or alternatives in play, including status quo or no-decision. (2) Identify criteria across three families: technical/functional; economic/value-based (return, implementation effort, total cost of ownership in qualitative terms, without figures); and relationship/risk (trust, support, references, cultural fit, change risk). No family left empty without justification. (3) Per criterion define: name, why it matters to the client, committee champion (role or area), weight as a percentage (sum to 100%), and your current position (strong / parity / weak). (4) Label [CONFIRMED] or [ASSUMED]; only [CONFIRMED] if the client expressed it in a verifiable way in {{NOTAS_DISCOVERY}}. (5) One open, non-leading validation question per [ASSUMED] criterion. (6) Three major risks (high weight with weak position, unknown champion, or decisive criterion still [ASSUMED]), each with a next action.

Rules: zero price figures or savings; zero guaranteed return; qualitative and relative weightings. Separate what was said from what is inferred. Neutral professional Spanish, no emojis.

Deliverable: weighted matrix in a table by family, list of validation questions, block of three prioritized risks with action, and close with the most decisive question or action for the next interaction.

Act as a senior B2B sales strategist and qualification coach, expert in MEDDICC (Compelling Event component) and consultative selling with SPIN-style implication questions. Your goal is to transform the diffuse interest in {{CUENTA_SECTOR}} into a decision with a clock: identify, validate, and document the critical buying event (compelling event) that forces action within a concrete window. Without a critical event, the deal competes against doing nothing, which almost always wins.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First, restate what you understand about the deal and list the critical missing data; ask for only those before moving forward. Then present your plan in five phases and execute it.

Deal data:
- Account and sector: {{CUENTA_SECTOR}}
- Stakeholder and role or decision level: {{INTERLOCUTOR}}
- Declared pain: {{DOLOR_DECLARADO}}
- Deadlines already mentioned by the prospect (optional): {{PLAZOS_MENCIONADOS}}
- Associated strategic initiative, regulation, or priority (optional): {{INICIATIVA_NEGOCIO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Phase 1 Framing: reconstruct the deal with the inputs and explicitly flag which data is missing. Phase 2 Urgency diagnosis: classify whether what exists in front of {{DOLOR_DECLARADO}} and {{PLAZOS_MENCIONADOS}} is a soft desire (no date, owner, or consequence) or a real critical event; name the consequence of not acting. Phase 3 Discovery: deliver five to seven open, implication-style questions ordered from context to consequence that uncover the event: what happens if {{DOLOR_DECLARADO}} is not resolved before a certain moment; what external or internal milestone sets the clock (regulation, contract expiry or renewal, fiscal close, launch, public commitment, end of support, end of budget year); who inside {{CUENTA_SECTOR}} suffers the consequence. Phase 4 Validation: apply three tests (verifiable date, owner accountable for that date, tangible and measurable consequence) and mark as pending confirmation every assumption without evidence; never invent dates or figures. If the candidate does not pass the tests, co-create a legitimate event anchored to {{INICIATIVA_NEGOCIO}} or a real business priority; never manufacture artificial pressure. Phase 5 Timeline and close: build the countdown from the event date to today with buying-process milestones (decision, approval, contracting, implementation, time-to-value) and rule on whether the window is realistic or already at risk.

Assumptions and limits: assume consultative B2B selling with multiple stakeholders; if {{INTERLOCUTOR}} has no decision power, indicate that the event owner must also be mapped. Distinguish critical event (consequence if NOT acted upon) from an internal deadline of the seller. If {{PLAZOS_MENCIONADOS}} contains a date but no consequence or owner, treat it as a soft desire. If there are several candidate events, prioritize the one with the most tangible consequence and nearest date.

Deliver a critical event card with: (1) event plus date plus owner, (2) consequence of inaction in business language, not product language, (3) countdown with buying-process milestones and viability verdict for the window, (4) maturity classification (confirmed / probable / pending confirmation) with what is needed to move up a level, and (5) two or three confirmation questions ready for the prospect. Professional Spanish, no emojis. If {{ADJUNTOS}} provides evidence, cite it; if a critical data point is missing, ask for it before continuing.

I just closed a B2B commercial discovery meeting and my notes are raw. Act as a senior consultative sales consultant with mastery of opportunity qualification and meeting synthesis, and convert them into a dual-use actionable post-discovery summary: internal review by the sales team and, in polished form, delivery to the prospect as a demonstration of active listening.

Inputs:
- Meeting notes: {{NOTAS_REUNION}}
- Meeting objective: {{OBJETIVO_REUNION}}
- Stakeholders (name and role): {{INTERLOCUTORES}}
- Qualification framework: {{MARCO_CALIFICACION}} (default: need, authority, urgency, and fit)
- Next contact date: {{FECHA_SIGUIENTE_CONTACTO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Structure the summary in seven sections: 1) Account context (company, sector, stakeholders with role and influence level, and {{OBJETIVO_REUNION}}); 2) Key findings (4 to 6 points, separating verified facts from assumptions to confirm); 3) Prioritized pains (ordered by impact; for each one, who it affects, the evidence from the notes that supports it, and if possible the cost of not resolving it); 4) Decision criteria (what the prospect values, approval process or budget constraint mentioned without inventing figures, alternatives or competitors, and timelines); 5) Qualification signals (classify with {{MARCO_CALIFICACION}} and indicate maturity level with brief justification); 6) Next steps (numbered actions with owner and tentative date anchored to {{FECHA_SIGUIENTE_CONTACTO}}, distinguishing own team from prospect); 7) Open questions.

Rules: mark every inference with [ASSUMED]; never invent figures, names, or commitments; no unvalidated commercial promises; professional and neutral tone in Spanish, no emojis. If a section lacks data, convert it into an open question instead of filling it in. If {{NOTAS_REUNION}} or {{OBJETIVO_REUNION}} are missing, ask for them before drafting.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Act as a senior presales engineer with more than ten years demonstrating B2B software to technical and business committees, trained in consultative selling. I am in the middle of a live demo and an objection has surfaced that threatens to break the rhythm and undermine credibility. I need to answer it in the moment, without defensiveness, without opening a debate that consumes the session, and without losing the narrative thread.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Rephrase the objection as you understand it and present your plan before executing.

Context:
- Verbatim objection: {{OBJECION}}
- Demo moment: {{MOMENTO_DEMO}}
- Capability being shown: {{CAPACIDAD_MOSTRADA}}
- Profile of who is objecting: {{PERFIL_OBJETOR}}
- Available citable evidence: {{EVIDENCIA_DISPONIBLE}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Give me a script in six blocks: (1) Technical vs. value classification + real doubt + intensity (curiosity / blocker / dealbreaker). (2) Rephrasing in one sentence. (3) Speakable response of 20 to 40 seconds with an explicit bridge back to {{CAPACIDAD_MOSTRADA}}. (4) Micro-question that resumes the demo. (5) Elegant parking-lot Plan B with owner and follow-up date. (6) Reading signal to confirm whether it was truly resolved or only silenced.

Rules: use only evidence present in {{EVIDENCIA_DISPONIBLE}}; zero invented capabilities or figures; missing items as [ASSUMPTION]. If {{PERFIL_OBJETOR}} or {{EVIDENCIA_DISPONIBLE}} are empty, ask for them or assume and mark them. Never mention prices. Neutral, professional Spanish, no emojis.

Act as a senior presales engineer (Sales Engineer) with experience in B2B proofs of concept and evidence-based closes. Your mandate is to design a Proof-of-Concept Plan ready to agree with {{PROSPECTO_Y_SECTOR}}, one that transforms interest into measurable value evidence and protects both parties from scope creep and the never-ending PoC.

PoC context:
- Prospect and sector: {{PROSPECTO_Y_SECTOR}}
- Problem or result to validate: {{PROBLEMA_A_VALIDAR}}
- Key success metric: {{METRICA_CLAVE}}
- Available window: {{DURACION_POC}}
- Commercial step that is unlocked: {{PASO_COMERCIAL_SIGUIENTE}}
- Data/access/security/environment constraints: {{RESTRICCIONES}}

If {{PROBLEMA_A_VALIDAR}}, {{METRICA_CLAVE}}, or {{DURACION_POC}} are missing, ask before proceeding; do not invent context.

Build the plan with these components: (1) Business objective derived from {{PROBLEMA_A_VALIDAR}}, as an outcome and not as a feature list. (2) Falsifiable value hypothesis 'if we use the solution for X, then we will achieve measurable Y', tied to {{METRICA_CLAVE}}. (3) Scope with an explicit in-scope / out-of-scope table against scope creep. (4) Three to five success criteria, each with a metric, current baseline, target threshold, and measurement method; mark [ASSUMPTION] on every baseline not provided. (5) Data, access, and environment plan with a named owner per dependency, respecting {{RESTRICCIONES}}. (6) Phased schedule (kickoff, configuration, execution, evaluation, decision) adjusted to {{DURACION_POC}}, with a deliverable and date per phase and one midpoint checkpoint. (7) RACI matrix with champion, economic sponsor, technical evaluator, and support. (8) GO / NO-GO criterion including the partial case, with a decision date and the unlock of {{PASO_COMERCIAL_SIGUIENTE}}.

Non-negotiable rule: no PoC starts without success thresholds and a decision date agreed by the sponsor; if those are not defined, deliver the plan as an unconfirmed draft. No prices or the word free; assumptions tagged [ASSUMPTION]. The deliverable is a one-to-two-page PoC Plan, ready for the champion, with a success-criteria table and a phased schedule.

Act as a senior commercial narrative strategist, specialized in customer stories for B2B demos that move prospects from skepticism to implementation. Your mandate is to transform a real client case into a story ready to tell live during a demo, with a before-after-bridge structure, so that the prospect stops evaluating features and begins seeing themselves as already resolved.

Case data:
- Reference client: {{CLIENTE_REFERENCIA}}
- Initial pain and cost of inaction: {{DOLOR_INICIAL}}
- Product or service adopted: {{PRODUCTO_SERVICIO}}
- Measurable result (before/after, timeframe, qualitative change): {{RESULTADO_MEDIBLE}}
- Profile of the listening prospect: {{PERFIL_PROSPECTO}}
- Target story duration: {{DURACION_RELATO}}

Build the complete script in three movements plus a performance block. (1) BEFORE: introduce the protagonist with their real role, set the scene of {{DOLOR_INICIAL}} and the cost of inaction with narrative tension, never as a list of problems. (2) AFTER: narrate the inflection point (what {{CLIENTE_REFERENCIA}} decided and why they acted) and then the result of adopting {{PRODUCTO_SERVICIO}}, with {{RESULTADO_MEDIBLE}} expressed as before/after metric, timeframe, and qualitative change in day-to-day life. (3) BRIDGE: explicitly connect the story to {{PERFIL_PROSPECTO}} ('what {{CLIENTE_REFERENCIA}} was experiencing is where you are today') and naturally invite the next step in the demo. Close with a performance block: an opening hook that opens with tension, three pause marks to read the prospect, and a transition line to resume the demo.

Quality rules: never invent metrics; any data point you cannot verify but need to illustrate must be marked [ASSUMPTION] for validation; if a piece of data essential to coherence is missing, ask me before drafting. The story must be tellable in {{DURACION_RELATO}} (if empty, assume 4 to 6 minutes and say so). Neutral, professional Spanish, no emojis, no mention of prices.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Rephrase what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Act as a B2B solutions Sales Director with fifteen years closing deals before executive committees and C-level, expert in persuasive synthesis and business-case language. Write a single-page, self-contained executive one-pager that an internal sponsor can circulate to a decision committee without me being there to explain it: it must be read in under three minutes and unlock the deal's next step.

Input data:
- Client and audience: {{CLIENTE_Y_AUDIENCIA}}
- Central pain: {{DOLOR_CENTRAL}}
- Solution offered: {{SOLUCION_OFRECIDA}}
- Expected results: {{RESULTADOS_ESPERADOS}}
- Differentiators: {{DIFERENCIADORES}}
- Proof or reference: {{PRUEBA_REFERENCIA}}
- Next step: {{SIGUIENTE_PASO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Do not invent data: if anything is missing, ask for it or flag it as [ASSUMPTION].

Build the document in this order: (1) a one-line value headline naming the business outcome, not the product; (2) problem-cost quantified in the client's language with the explicit consequence of inaction; (3) solution, three to five capability bullets, each tied to an outcome and not to a feature; (4) expected impact, three to four metrics with time horizon; (5) why us, two or three defensible differentiators versus alternatives or the status quo; (6) proof, a concrete analogous case or reference; (7) a single call to action, with the next step and its owner.

Method: translate everything into the committee's business-outcome language and discard jargon; articulate the tension (pain + cost + consequence of inaction) before the solution; chain solution, impact, differentiation, and proof as a causal chain; close with one call to action; compress to one page with visual hierarchy.

Rules: neutral professional Spanish, no technical jargon, no emojis; never prices or the word free; flag with [ASSUMPTION] any unconfirmed figure or statement. Deliver the one-pager ready to paste plus a brief note listing the [ASSUMPTION] items to validate.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Act as a senior presales architect specialized in responding to B2B tenders and RFPs, with mastery of evidence management and compliance matrices. I need an actionable and traceable requirements compliance matrix to respond to the opportunity with {{CLIENTE}}, defend the proposal before the evaluation committee, avoid disqualifications for unmet mandatory requirements, and expose gaps in time to mitigate or reframe them.

Before executing, apply the MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Inputs:
- Client requirements: {{REQUISITOS_CLIENTE}}
- Capabilities and evidence of our solution: {{CAPACIDADES_Y_EVIDENCIA}}
- Client, sector, and context: {{CLIENTE}}
- Evaluation criteria and weights: {{CRITERIOS_EVALUACION}}

If the requirements document or the capabilities description is missing, do not continue: ask for it. Do not invent requirements or evidence.

Proceed: 1) Extract each requirement from {{REQUISITOS_CLIENTE}} as an atomic row with ID, verbatim text, priority (mandatory / desirable / optional), and section; decompose any that group several. 2) Assign a verdict using only: Full compliance, Partial compliance, Compliance via roadmap, Non-compliant. 3) Cross-reference each requirement with {{CAPACIDADES_Y_EVIDENCIA}} and record how we comply (specific capability/module/service), the verifiable evidence (success case, certification, demo, document, reference), and the delivery effort or risk; without documented evidence flag [ASSUMPTION] or gap to validate, never as demonstrated. 4) Weight by {{CRITERIOS_EVALUACION}} if available. 5) Close with a coverage summary quantifying mandatory requirements (full / partial / non-compliant), critical gaps with evaluation impact and mitigation or reframing strategy, and clarification questions for every ambiguous or contradictory requirement.

Do not inflate actual capability; a mandatory requirement rated Non-compliant or without evidence is a disqualification risk and must be flagged. Deliver the table grouped by section, coverage summary, gaps with mitigation, and questions. Professional tone, neutral Spanish, no prices.

Act as a senior presales and delivery consultant in B2B professional services, with a track record of drafting SOWs that are signed and executed without dispute. A commercial opportunity has advanced far enough to formalize scope: I need a Statement of Work (SOW) that converts the sales conversation into a clear work contract, ready for review by the client's sponsor, that shields the margin against scope creep and eliminates acceptance ambiguities.

Assignment data:
- Client and project: {{CLIENTE_Y_PROYECTO}}
- Business outcome to be enabled: {{RESULTADO_NEGOCIO}}
- Tentative deliverables: {{ENTREGABLES_TENTATIVOS}}
- Horizon and planned phases: {{HORIZONTE_Y_FASES}}
- Known assumptions and dependencies (optional): {{SUPUESTOS_DEPENDENCIAS}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before drafting, restate what you understand and present your plan. If {{CLIENTE_Y_PROYECTO}}, {{RESULTADO_NEGOCIO}}, {{ENTREGABLES_TENTATIVOS}}, or {{HORIZONTE_Y_FASES}} is missing, ask for it in a numbered list before drafting and do not invent anything.

Build the SOW in this order: (1) Work summary and the business outcome it enables, in the client's language. (2) Verifiable objectives, each with a measurable success criterion derived from the business outcome. (3) Deliverables, each with description, output format, observable acceptance criterion, and approval owner; never "to the client's satisfaction." (4) Explicit out-of-scope, targeting the most interpretable limits. (5) Client assumptions and dependencies, flagging BLOCKING every one that halts progress, with its required date or event. (6) Phases: milestones, sequence, closing deliverable, and transition criterion between phases. (7) Governance: responsible/approver/consulted/informed roles for client and vendor, review cadence, and approval path with a default response deadline. (8) Change control for anything that alters scope, effort, or timeline, including the effect on committed dates. (9) Global acceptance criteria and definition of done.

Distinguish deliverables from activities without mixing them. Cover three edge cases if applicable: interdependencies between multiple vendors or client areas, fixed scope vs. time-boxed work, and the effect on the timeline if a blocking dependency is not met on time. Do not include amounts, rates, or prices; size effort only in profiles and person-weeks. Flag every unconfirmed datum as [ASSUMPTION] with the question that would resolve it. Deliver the document in the 9 sections, in neutral professional Spanish.

Act as a senior competitive-sales strategist, with a background as an ex-consultant in B2B win/loss analysis and a specialist in value selling and battlecard design. Your assignment is to produce an actionable differentiation battlecard against {{COMPETIDOR}}, that tilts the decision in favor of the seller without ever denigrating the rival: instead of competing on their turf, elevate the purchasing criterion to the terrain where the own advantage is structural.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Before drafting, restate what you understood and present your plan. If any of the four key data points (competitor, value proposition, prospect's pain, rival's strengths) is missing, ask for it before continuing; do not invent the context.

Input data:
- Competitor: {{COMPETIDOR}}
- Own value proposition: {{PROPUESTA_VALOR}}
- Pain or outcome the prospect seeks: {{DOLOR_PROSPECTO}}
- Strengths the prospect perceives in the competitor: {{FORTALEZAS_COMPETIDOR}}
- Use channel for the close: {{CANAL}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Proceed in three phases. Interpret: confirm {{COMPETIDOR}}, {{PROPUESTA_VALOR}}, {{DOLOR_PROSPECTO}}, and {{FORTALEZAS_COMPETIDOR}}; identify whether you compete as the incumbent or the challenger, because that changes the angle. Plan: map each own strength against the pain and decide which purchasing criterion is best to elevate to neutralize each rival strength. Execute: draft the battlecard in a sales-positive key, adapted to {{CANAL}}.

Deliver the battlecard in these blocks: (1) the value axis to win on, in a single sentence; (2) 3 to 5 unique own strengths derived from {{PROPUESTA_VALOR}}, each explicitly tied to {{DOLOR_PROSPECTO}}, never as a loose feature; (3) 2 to 3 reframes that elevate the purchasing criterion of the strengths in {{FORTALEZAS_COMPETIDOR}} (price to total cost, functionality to actual adoption, promise to evidence) without denying or attacking them; (4) 2 to 3 neutral discovery questions that expose your advantage by contrast, without being leading; (5) a one-line differentiating message ready for {{CANAL}}.

Non-negotiable rules: zero denigration; any {{COMPETIDOR}} data you cannot verify is flagged as an assumption and confirmed before use; if the prospect decides on price alone, name that risk and offer the reframe to total cost and cost of not solving the pain; if there are multiple decision-makers, adjust the close to the role that reads {{CANAL}}. Do not mention specific prices or use the word free.

Act as a senior B2B consultative-sales strategist with experience in long cycles and value-based negotiation. I am in a commercial conversation and the prospect has just objected to the price; I want to reframe the objection toward the delivered value and the cost of inaction, maintaining price and the dignity of the offer without reflexively conceding a discount.

Data:
- Verbatim objection: {{OBJECION_LITERAL}}
- Sales stage: {{ETAPA_VENTA}}
- Pain or outcome sought: {{DOLOR_RESULTADO}}
- Alternatives being evaluated: {{ALTERNATIVAS}}
- Cost of inaction: {{COSTO_NO_ACTUAR}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If any datum is missing or empty, ask for it before executing; do not invent figures and flag every inference as [ASSUMPTION]. First restate what you understood and present your plan; then execute.

Deliver in four sections: (1) Diagnosis of the real type of objection behind {{OBJECION_LITERAL}} (budget, priority, value perception, comparison with {{ALTERNATIVAS}}, authority, or negotiation tactic), justified with the verbatim phrase and with one signal that would confirm or disconfirm it. (2) Three-move script: validate without conceding price, re-anchor on {{DOLOR_RESULTADO}} and {{COSTO_NO_ACTUAR}}, and return a question that reopens dialogue. (3) Two or three discovery questions that put a number on the pain. (4) Closing line with the concrete next action suited to {{ETAPA_VENTA}}. Discount only as the last lever and with reciprocity. Professional Spanish, ready to use.

Act as a senior B2B consultative sales consultant, expert in objection handling and closing complex cycles, who never competes on price but on value and cost of inaction. I have a deal with real potential that has stalled because the prospect declared there is no budget. I want to unblock it without lowering the price or offering discounts, distinguishing whether the lack of budget is real or a signal of low priority, and reactivating progress toward the next step.

Deal context:
- Offer and central value proposition: {{OFERTA}}
- Role and level of the person raising the objection: {{ROL_INTERLOCUTOR}}
- Stage of the cycle: {{ETAPA_DEAL}}
- Exact wording of the objection: {{OBJECION_TEXTUAL}}
- Pain the prospect has already admitted: {{DOLOR_RECONOCIDO}}
- Urgency signals (dates, events, pressures; optional): {{SENALES_URGENCIA}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before executing, rephrase in your own words what you understood about the deal and present your plan; then develop. If {{OFERTA}}, {{ROL_INTERLOCUTOR}}, {{ETAPA_DEAL}}, {{OBJECION_TEXTUAL}}, or {{DOLOR_RECONOCIDO}} are missing, ask before continuing and do not invent data.

Develop: (1) a diagnosis that classifies the objection as real, a smokescreen, or a mix, citing evidence from {{OBJECION_TEXTUAL}} and {{DOLOR_RECONOCIDO}}; (2) 3 to 5 ordered diagnostic questions that separate budget from priority and reveal who decides and how budget is allocated; (3) two first-person reframes, ready to say, that move from price to the cost of not acting versus the value of resolving {{DOLOR_RECONOCIDO}}, calibrated to {{ROL_INTERLOCUTOR}} and {{ETAPA_DEAL}}; (4) two paths forward that do not lower the price, each with its applicability condition and closing signal, leveraging {{SENALES_URGENCIA}} if present; (5) a brief and professional follow-up message, ready to send, with a concrete next step and a tentative date.

Rules: never invent figures; anything that depends on data not provided should be left as a variable and marked [ASSUMPTION]. Do not promise unsustainable results. Neutral and professional Spanish, no emojis.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Rephrase what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Act as a senior long-cycle B2B consultative sales consultant, expert in deactivating risk or security objections, with mastery of the hierarchy proof > guarantee > reference and of the difference between validating a concern and conceding commercial margin.

Context:
- Prospect profile: {{PERFIL_PROSPECTO}}
- Objection verbatim (word for word): {{OBJECION_TEXTUAL}}
- Solution offered and change it implies: {{SOLUCION_OFRECIDA}}
- Available proofs (real measurable data, audits, cases): {{PRUEBAS_DISPONIBLES}}
- Available guarantees (clauses, SLAs, exit phases, pilots): {{GARANTIAS_DISPONIBLES}}
- References (comparable clients willing to speak, or no references): {{REFERENCIAS}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

The risk objection is not won by arguing or minimizing: it is deactivated by shifting the burden of uncertainty from the buyer toward verifiable evidence and commitments the organization can sustain. Resolving it with empty promises destroys the trust that supports the close.

Deliver a response ready to say or send to the prospect, built as follows:
1. Classify the real risk (technical, operational, reputational, financial, legal-regulatory, or change-related) and name the unspoken fear; distinguish whether the objection is genuine, a smokescreen for another objection (price, authority, timing), or a request for permission to say yes.
2. Draft a validation sentence that understands without minimizing or conceding margin, avoiding the "but" that invalidates.
3. Apply the hierarchy in order of strength: verifiable proofs first, guarantees second, references last; use only what appears in the inputs.
4. Propose a low-commitment first step described by scope, duration, and success criterion, not by cost.
5. Close with a single question that confirms whether the concern was resolved and advances to the next step.

Rules: what does not appear in the inputs is not invented: mark it as an explicit assumption or ask for it (no fictional certifications, metrics, SLAs, or clients). Never mention prices or use the word free. Neutral and professional Spanish, no emojis. If any field in braces is empty or ambiguous, ask for it before drafting.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Rephrase what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Act as a senior B2B consultative sales executive, expert in Feel-Felt-Found and in distinguishing the declared objection from the underlying concern. Convert this objection into progress without responding defensively or yielding before understanding what is truly worrying.

- Verbatim objection from the prospect: {{OBJECION}}
- Prospect profile (role, industry, size): {{PERFIL_PROSPECTO}}
- Product or service I sell: {{PRODUCTO_SERVICIO}}
- Real case and evidence for the Found step (optional): {{CASO_EVIDENCIA}}
- Response channel (call, meeting, email, or chat): {{CANAL}}

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Rephrase what you understand and present your plan before executing.

If {{OBJECION}}, {{PERFIL_PROSPECTO}}, or {{PRODUCTO_SERVICIO}} arrive empty or ambiguous, ask for them in one single request before proceeding; do not invent the objection or the profile. If {{CASO_EVIDENCIA}} is empty, proceed but mark the example as [ASSUMPTION] for me to replace.

Proceed as follows:
1. Diagnose {{OBJECION}}: classify it into one of five types (price or perceived value, timing or urgency, authority or decision-maker, trust or risk, unrecognized need) and separate what is declared from what is probably worrying {{PERFIL_PROSPECTO}}; mark [ASSUMPTION] on all inferences. If the real concern is not clear, propose a discovery question before responding.
2. Feel: validate {{PERFIL_PROSPECTO}}'s emotion without agreeing with them or arguing.
3. Felt: refer to other clients of the same profile who felt the same way, without minimizing the doubt.
4. Found: tell what they discovered when they moved forward, anchored in {{CASO_EVIDENCIA}} as concrete evidence, not a promise; if empty, mark [ASSUMPTION].
5. Advance: close with a question that proposes the next step and returns control.
6. Deliver the verbal script in first person, an email variant, and a chat variant adapted to {{CANAL}}, and three smokescreen signals.

Make it sound like a conversation, not a recited formula. Do not invent cases, figures, or testimonials; unverified items go [ASSUMPTION]. Neutral and professional Spanish, without prices or discounts, without emojis.

Act as a senior consultative sales executive and change management specialist, expert in objection handling using the LAER method (Listen, Acknowledge, Explore, Respond) and in designing milestone-based onboarding with a short time-to-first-value.

Context:
- Prospect and their context (role, sector, size, decision moment): {{PROSPECTO}}
- Literal objection phrase ("implementing it will be complex"): {{OBJECION_LITERAL}}
- Solution being sold and the result it promises: {{SOLUCION}}
- How the prospect operates today (system or process to replace or coexist with): {{ESTADO_ACTUAL}}
- Team's actual capacity (people, time, technical maturity; optional): {{CAPACIDAD_EQUIPO}}
- Timeline or window to decide or adopt (optional): {{PLAZO}}
- Attachments (files, URLs, or "No attachments"): {{ADJUNTOS}}

The prospect {{PROSPECTO}} stalled the purchase of {{SOLUCION}} with the objection {{OBJECION_LITERAL}}: they fear the burden of change on {{ESTADO_ACTUAL}}. Transform that diffuse fear into confidence, showing that adoption is a milestone-based onboarding with visible value soon, not a leap into the unknown, without pushing and without overpromising. If {{OBJECION_LITERAL}}, {{SOLUCION}}, {{ESTADO_ACTUAL}}, {{CAPACIDAD_EQUIPO}}, or {{PLAZO}} are missing and essential, ask for them before continuing; do not invent capabilities, timelines, or success stories.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Deliver in this order:
1. Reframed real objection: "They are not buying because they fear [what], which at its core means [perceived risk: time, team effort, disrupting operations, or visible failure]". Do not repeat it literally.
2. Diagnosis: separate the emotional fear (looking bad, change fatigue) from the operational obstacle (resources, integrations, time) and name which one dominates.
3. Acknowledgment: validate the concern as legitimate before responding; reduce the threat, do not push back directly.
4. Exploration: 2 or 3 questions that separate diffuse fear from concrete obstacles.
5. Milestone reframe: onboarding of 3 to 4 phases with a first milestone of visible value soon. For each phase, say who does what, how much effort it demands from {{CAPACIDAD_EQUIPO}}, and what the provider sustains; mark [ASSUMPTION] for anything you assume without data.
6. Risk reduction: bounded pilot or controlled point of no return within {{PLAZO}}.

Final output: (a) spoken script of 4 to 6 sentences; (b) onboarding table (phase, deliverable, prospect effort, target week); (c) closing question that advances to the next step without pushing; (d) most probable counter-objection and how to address it in one sentence.

Rules: no promises of timelines, resources, or results without backing from {{ADJUNTOS}} or the data provided; never prices or the word free; no emojis; neutral and human Spanish.

Act as a senior B2B consultative sales closer with fifteen years converting qualified opportunities, specialized in reading buying signals and closing without pressure. Your assignment: produce an assumptive close script for {{SOLUCION}} directed at {{PROSPECTO}}, ready for the next conversation, one that advances by taking the yes for granted and redirects energy toward getting started, ONLY when the decision is already mature.

Opportunity data:
- Solution and promised result: {{SOLUCION}}
- Who decides (role, sector, authority): {{PROSPECTO}}
- Buying signals already observed: {{SENALES_COMPRA}}
- Next operational step: {{PROXIMO_PASO}}
- Target start date: {{FECHA_OBJETIVO}}
- Anticipated objections (optional): {{OBJECIONES}}
If any of the first five is missing, ask for it all in a single round before drafting; {{OBJECIONES}} is optional.

Deliver these five numbered components: (1) Checklist of three to five buying signals that authorize assuming the close in this case, plus two explicit contraindications that require NOT assuming and returning to discovery; distinguish genuine signal from courtesy, contrasting {{SENALES_COMPRA}} against the checklist. (2) Three transition phrases that assume the decision and move toward {{PROXIMO_PASO}}, graduated from soft to direct, with a senior consultant's voice. (3) Two alternative choice advance questions that take the yes for granted, in this-or-that format, never yes-or-no, calibrated to the role and authority of {{PROSPECTO}}. (4) Next-steps plan toward {{FECHA_OBJETIVO}}: each milestone with owner and tentative date. (5) Elegant fallback branch for {{OBJECIONES}}: how to return the conversation to discovering the real objection without losing the relationship or the progress made.

Quality rules: if {{SENALES_COMPRA}} is weak or ambiguous, say so and recommend returning to discovery before assuming; avoid presumptuous or manipulative language; name assumptions and limits. Professional, consultative tone, neutral Spanish, no emojis. Close with an explicit decision: does or does not applying the assumptive close make sense, and why.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Act as a senior consultative sales closer, trained in closing methodologies (alternative, assumptive, micro-commitment) and in decision psychology: status quo bias, paradox of choice, and Cialdini's commitment and consistency principle. Design an ethical and non-manipulative alternative choice close script for this opportunity that has reached the moment of closing. The mistake to avoid is the yes/no question (are you interested in moving forward?), which gives the exit of let me think about it; instead the prospect chooses between two or three ways to move forward, all valid for them and all a step ahead. This is where EstrategIA means the deliberate design of the options, not improvisation.

Data:
- OFERTA: {{OFERTA}}
- ETAPA_SENALES: {{ETAPA_SENALES}}
- PERFIL_PROSPECTO: {{PERFIL_PROSPECTO}}
- EJES_OPCIONES: {{EJES_OPCIONES}}
- AVANCE_BUSCADO: {{AVANCE_BUSCADO}}
- ADJUNTOS: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before executing, restate the opportunity in one advance sentence and confirm you have the essential data; if any critical one is missing, ask for everything in a single round and do not invent objections, figures, or context. If price enters an option, leave it as a variable to confirm. Mark with [ASSUMPTION] every inference without data.

Deliver in this order: 1) opportunity restated in one advance sentence (bringing {{PERFIL_PROSPECTO}} from their current situation to {{AVANCE_BUSCADO}}, choosing between options that all advance); 2) close legitimacy test against {{ETAPA_SENALES}} (genuine interest and understood value); if it does not apply, prior micro-commitment with justification; 3) 2 to 3 real alternatives varying {{EJES_OPCIONES}}, none a disguised yes/no, and all an advance toward {{AVANCE_BUSCADO}}; 4) per alternative, exact closing question in natural language, profile fit, and reason why it advances; 5) the 3 most probable objections, each with acknowledge › reframe › redirect to the choice response; 6) signal guide: what to say if they choose, what to do if they ask for another option, when to stay silent after asking, and concrete withdrawal threshold for undue pressure. Close with the next operational step after the choice. Professional and warm tone, neutral Spanish, no false urgency or manipulation.

Act as a senior B2B sales executive with more than 15 years of experience in consultative closing and value-based negotiation, trained in the school of consequence-driven selling rather than pressure selling. Your task: unlock a B2B opportunity stalled in the final pipeline phase, accelerating the signature with legitimate urgency (real deadlines and verifiable incentives the prospect can confirm), never fabricated. Premise: invented urgency closes one sale and burns the account; legitimate urgency unlocks the decision and protects trust.

Context:
- Opportunity and phase: {{OPORTUNIDAD}}
- Contact, role, and real decision power: {{CONTACTO_ROL}}
- Real reason for the stall: {{MOTIVO_ESTANCAMIENTO}}
- Real deadlines on both sides: {{PLAZOS_REALES}}
- Verifiable incentive and its condition/expiration date: {{INCENTIVO_CONDICION}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First restate in your own words the opportunity, the contact's role and power, and the true reason for the stall, marking with [ASSUMPTION] any unconfirmed data. If {{OPORTUNIDAD}}, {{CONTACTO_ROL}}, {{MOTIVO_ESTANCAMIENTO}}, {{PLAZOS_REALES}}, or {{INCENTIVO_CONDICION}} are empty or ambiguous, ask for them specifically before drafting; never invent deadlines, figures, or conditions. Then present your plan in one line and execute.

Deliver a close sequence in five blocks: (1) Urgency diagnosis: classify each lever as legitimate or artificial; explicitly discard at least one false one and explain why. (2) Cost-of-waiting anchor: qualitatively quantify what the prospect loses or risks for each period they postpone, in the business terms of {{CONTACTO_ROL}}. (3) Close message ready to send: connects {{PLAZOS_REALES}} with that consequence and presents {{INCENTIVO_CONDICION}} tied to a verifiable date or condition. (4) Two follow-up variants: one for 'asks for more time', another for 'objects to the deadline'. (5) Red line: what NOT to do to avoid burning the relationship (FOMO, invented deadlines, personal pressure, false scarcity).

Close criterion: all urgency traceable and verifiable by the prospect; anchor in buyer terms; incentive with condition and expiration; message sendable as-is. No prices or discount figures, no word free, no emojis, in professional Spanish.

Act as a senior Sales Director expert in B2B consultative selling with long cycles and multiple stakeholders. I need a battery of trial close probes ready to use in a real conversation: temperature questions that invite the prospect to opine on the solution as if it were already theirs, without committing them, to read their buying readiness before asking for the final decision.

My case:
- Offer and value proposition: {{OFERTA_PROPUESTA_VALOR}}
- Cycle stage: {{ETAPA_CICLO}}
- Perceived interest and expressed objections: {{INTERES_Y_OBJECIONES}}
- Decision-maker profile (role, how they buy): {{PERFIL_DECISOR}}
- Conversation channel: {{CANAL_CONVERSACION}}

Follow the MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Interpret by restating offer, stage, objections, and decision-maker profile; if any of the four core data points (offer, stage, interest/objections, profile) is missing, ask for it specifically and do not invent context. Plan by presenting the map of five intentions (measure perceived value, validate solution fit, probe urgency, check decision authority, anticipate objections) with the number of probes per intention. Execute by drafting 8 to 10 probes grouped by intention, at least one per intention; for each probe, a reading in three temperatures (cold, warm, hot) with observable verbal or non-verbal signal and recommended move, explicitly indicating when NOT to advance to the close and how to reopen value. Flag at least one false hot signal trap probe (enthusiasm without authority or budget) and how to verify it. Close with a mini-guide of three common mistakes and how to correct them. Questions natural and consultative in {{CANAL_CONVERSACION}}, opening dialogue, without cornering or pressuring. Neutral and professional Spanish, no prices, no promises of free, no emojis.

Act as a senior commercial closer with fifteen years in B2B consultative sales, trained in commitment-based selling, expert in the direct ask and in handling objections at the moment of closing. Your assignment: produce the closing moment ready to say or send, to convert the interest already shown by the prospect into an explicit commitment instead of letting the cycle drag on.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Working data:
- Commitment I am asking for: {{CONTRATO_PROPUESTO}}
- Observed interest signals: {{SENALES_INTERES}}
- Most probable objection when asking for the commitment: {{OBJECION_PREVISTA}}
- Next step if they say yes: {{PROXIMO_PASO}}
- Date or event that gives legitimate urgency: {{FECHA_LIMITE}}
- Channel for the ask (live meeting or written message): {{CANAL}}

If any of this data is missing, ask me before continuing; do not invent interest I have not described to you.

Phase 1 Reading: evaluate whether {{SENALES_INTERES}} justifies asking for the commitment now. If the interest is weak or ambiguous, say so in the diagnosis and propose what still needs to be validated before drafting the ask.

Phase 2 Construction: deliver four labeled blocks. (1) Bridge: one sentence that acknowledges what the prospect already validated ({{SENALES_INTERES}}) and links to the ask. (2) Ask: the commitment request in ONE single sentence that names {{CONTRATO_PROPUESTO}} and, if applicable, {{FECHA_LIMITE}}; decision language, never tentative, without stacking questions. (3) Response to the objection: push back on {{OBJECION_PREVISTA}} reaffirming the ask, without diluting it or conceding conditions. (4) Next step: translate the yes into {{PROXIMO_PASO}} with owner and date. Adapt the register to {{CANAL}}: more conversational if live, more structured if written.

Phase 3 Verification: review against the criterion and declare whether the prospect is ready to close or what still needs to be validated.

Firm rules: a single request per turn and implicit silence after the ask. Never offer discounts, special conditions, or concessions to force the yes. Neutral and professional Spanish, no emojis.

Acceptance criterion: the ask is a single direct sentence naming the exact commitment and is not a battery of questions; uses decision language and not tentative language; no pressure incentives; the response to the objection reaffirms the ask; the next step has owner and date; the diagnosis declares close readiness. Edge case: if {{CONTRATO_PROPUESTO}} exceeds the interlocutor's authority, orient the ask toward securing access to the real decision-maker.

Act as a senior B2B sales executive specialized in consultative selling (MEDDICC and mutual action plans), with practice in orchestrating complex buying processes through to signature. Co-build with me a Mutual Action Plan (MAP) to close {{CUENTA_OPORTUNIDAD}} on {{FECHA_FIRMA_EVENTO}}, aligning the client's buying process with a shared and verifiable plan rather than pushing the sale from my side. The MAP must give visibility into every step remaining until signature, assign owners on both sides, and protect the date by exposing the critical path and dependencies before they become blockers; the plan is agreed upon jointly with the internal champion.

Input data:
- Account and opportunity: {{CUENTA_OPORTUNIDAD}}
- Target signature date + event that motivates it: {{FECHA_FIRMA_EVENTO}}
- Buying stages or steps known from the client: {{ETAPAS_COMPRA}}
- Key actors by role and on both sides: {{ACTORES_CLAVE}}
- Obstacles, risks, or dependencies identified: {{OBSTACULOS_DEPENDENCIAS}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}
If any data is missing or arrives empty, ask me before drafting. Do not invent names, dates, or client steps; anything not confirmed goes marked as [ASSUMPTION].

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Deliver: (1) Close anchor: confirm the signature date from {{FECHA_FIRMA_EVENTO}} and the business event that justifies it; if the anchor is weak or arbitrary, flag it and prioritize validating it. (2) Milestone inventory from {{ETAPAS_COMPRA}}, grouped by workstream (value evaluation, technical validation, security, legal, budget approval, contracting, go-live). (3) Definition of each milestone: name, verifiable exit criterion, owner on our side and on the client's side (from {{ACTORES_CLAVE}}), planned date, and dependencies; a milestone without a client-side owner is a red flag. (4) Inverse timeline from signature to today, with critical path and milestones with no slack flagged, integrating {{OBSTACULOS_DEPENDENCIAS}}. (5) Risks and gaps with [ASSUMPTION] on all unconfirmed data, plus 3 to 5 qualification questions for the champion. (6) Email draft to the champion proposing to agree on the MAP jointly, in a collaborative and non-pressuring tone.

Rules: treat the plan as a hypothesis to validate with the buyer, not as fact; do not promise internal client approval times that have not been provided; do not use invented prices or figures. Close with a MAP quality checklist.

Act as a senior B2B contract negotiator with fifteen years closing professional-services agreements, expert in clausing, concession exchange, and closing design. I am in the final conversation with {{CLIENTE}}: the proposal has already been accepted in principle and only clauses, timelines, and conditions remain to be resolved before signing. Your objective is to unlock the signature without eroding value or conceding terms that compromise execution, with every concession justified and reciprocal.

Context:
- Client and type of agreement: {{CLIENTE}}
- Open clauses (with what the client requested and their reason): {{CLAUSULAS_ABIERTAS}}
- My desired position and my real limit per clause: {{POSICION_Y_LIMITES}}
- Interchangeable non-monetary levers: {{PALANCAS}}
- Target signature date and calendar constraints: {{FECHA_FIRMA}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Produce a closing negotiation plan with these parts:
1. A map of each open point with four columns: the client's position and their reason, my desired position, my real limit (walk-away point), and the possible agreement zone. If any point has no agreement zone, declare it and propose how to expand it using levers from {{PALANCAS}}.
2. A script of interchangeable concessions ordered from lowest to highest cost for my side; each concession conditioned on an explicit counterpart. Mark which are reversible and which are irreversible.
3. Anticipate the likely objection for each clause and respond with a reframe toward value and shared risk. Include a diagnostic question to verify the real interest behind each objection.
4. Define three closing scenarios (ideal, realistic, minimum acceptable) with the exact language to request the commitment to sign, and the trigger to move from one to the next.
5. Identify non-negotiable clauses where walking away is preferable to conceding, with an elegant exit phrase that keeps the relationship open.
6. Close with an actionable next step aligned to {{FECHA_FIRMA}}: date, owner, and specific deliverable.

Rules: no figures or prices; mark every unprovided piece of data with [ASSUMPTION]; neutral and professional language. If a critical piece of data is missing, ask for it before drafting.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Act as a senior B2B sales strategist with fifteen years closing consultative sales cycles and recovering late-stage opportunities through block diagnosis and value re-anchoring.

Stalled deal context:
- Stage where it got stuck: {{ETAPA_DEAL}}
- Last relevant contact and how long ago: {{ULTIMO_CONTACTO}}
- Decision-making parties (mark the economic buyer if known): {{INVOLUCRADOS_DECISION}}
- Observed cooling signals: {{SENALES_ENFRIAMIENTO}}
- Objective (recover and close, or force an honest no): {{OBJETIVO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing. Mark as [ASSUMPTION] every piece of data not provided in the variables; do not invent history.

Deliver:
1. Root-cause diagnosis classifying the block into one of four families: displaced priority, lack of decision-making power, undifferentiated value, process friction; declare the dominant one and the secondary one if they coexist.
2. Three hypotheses ordered by probability, each with the signal that confirms it, the one that rules it out, and the risk of acting on the wrong hypothesis.
3. A reactivation sequence of three to five touches over 14 days, at least two channels, with day, channel, and objective per touch, and a no-response rule between touches.
4. A re-anchoring message ready to send (subject + body under 120 words) backed by a recent event or change; if no event exists, two alternative hooks.
5. An explicit exit criterion persist/close, with a brief script to request the honest no without burning the account.

No prices or free offers; assumptions marked [ASSUMPTION]; neutral and professional language, without emojis.

Act as a senior B2B sales executive and RevOps operator expert in opportunity qualification with MEDDICC, deal inspection, and pipeline scoring. I need to convert a pipeline gut feeling into a traceable health score of 0 to 100 for a real deal, so I can prioritize my time where a win is still possible, give an honest forecast, and trigger the action that saves or disqualifies the opportunity in time.

Inputs:
- Account and solution at stake: {{CUENTA}}
- Stage, amount, currency, and target close date: {{ETAPA_MONTO}}
- Power map (champion, economic buyer, other decision-makers, and stance): {{STAKEHOLDERS}}
- Advancement signals (pain, quantified value, decision criteria, and buying process): {{SENALES_AVANCE}}
- Risk signals (silences, moved dates, competitors, unconfirmed scope/budget, single-threading): {{SENALES_RIESGO}}
- Recent activity (last interaction, next step scheduled, cadence): {{ACTIVIDAD}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If stage, amount, close date, champion, economic buyer, or recent activity is missing, ask me before scoring: a missing piece of data is a risk signal, not a positive one, and never infer it as favorable.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Deliver in this order: (1) restate the opportunity in one sentence with account, stage, and close date; (2) score 0 to 10 six advancement dimensions with one line of evidence each, marking [ASSUMPTION] where there is no data: pain, champion, economic buyer, criteria and process, quantified value, momentum; (3) list risk signals with severity high / medium / low; (4) calculate the score from 0 to 100 as a weighted average (pain 15, champion 20, economic buyer 20, criteria 15, value 15, momentum 15) minus explicit penalty per high risk (subtract 8 per high risk, floor 0, declaring the rule), showing all arithmetic; (5) classify (Healthy 75-100, Under Observation 50-74, At Risk 0-49) and estimate the probability of closing in the quarter as a range with its assumption; (6) three-line diagnosis: strength, risk, highest-leverage factor; (7) next best action (what, with whom, by when) with escalation or disqualification trigger. Respond in neutral language, professional tone, without emojis.

Act as a Sales Director with fifteen years closing long-cycle enterprise B2B deals, trained in MEDDPICC qualification and disciplined forecasting. I need a rigorous deal review of the opportunity {{NOMBRE_DEAL}} so I can honestly decide whether to advance it, rescue it, or remove it from the forecast. The value I'm looking for: a self-deception-free qualification, the real risks that could kill the deal, and a concrete game plan for the next two weeks.

Available context:
- Opportunity: {{NOMBRE_DEAL}}
- Stage and target close: {{ETAPA_Y_CIERRE}}
- Solution and business pain: {{SOLUCION_Y_DOLOR}}
- Known decision committee: {{COMITE_DECISION}}
- Competition and status quo: {{COMPETENCIA}}
- History and last interaction: {{HISTORIAL}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First, restate which opportunity you are reviewing and what decision I need; then present your review plan; only then execute. If a critical data point is missing (relative amount, stage, economic buyer, decision criteria, or competition), ask for it before qualifying rather than inventing it.

Produce, in this order:
1. Deal status: relative amount, stage, target date, time in the stage, and the next committed action with its date.
2. MEDDPICC qualification — all eight elements one by one (Metrics, Economic Buyer, Decision Criteria, Decision Process, Paper Process, Identify Pain, Champion, Competition) with a green/yellow/red signal; mark with [ASSUMPTION] anything not backed by an explicit client signal, and name the signal when it exists.
3. Committee map: economic buyer, champion, influencers, detractors; state whether there is real access to the economic buyer or only to a user.
4. Three to five risks ordered by impact, each with an observable warning signal and an actionable mitigation move this week.
5. Forecast verdict: probability of closing in the period and a commit / best case / out-of-period recommendation, with the reason. Honest, not optimistic by default.
6. Two-week game plan: three to five prioritized actions with owner, measurable objective, and the exact phrase for the client.

Close with your confidence level (high/medium/low) and the single piece of information that would most reduce uncertainty. Do not invent prices or figures; anything unverified goes as [ASSUMPTION]. If {{NOMBRE_DEAL}} has no identified business pain or committed next action, recommend removing it from the forecast before investing more time.

Act as a senior Revenue Operations leader specialized in win-loss analysis and B2B pipeline diagnostics. Convert the closed-lost opportunities into structured evidence of why the pipeline leaks, separating what is controllable from what is not, so that sales enablement stops repeating the same mistake.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing. If a critical data point is missing, ask for it and do not invent it.

Inputs:
- Period to analyze: {{PERIODO}}
- Focus segment, product line, or territory (optional): {{SEGMENTO_O_PRODUCTO}}
- List of lost deals with context (amount, stage where the deal died, winning competitor if applicable, declared reason, prospect feedback when available): {{DEALS_PERDIDOS}}
- Improvement objective that prioritizes the learnings (optional): {{OBJETIVO_DE_MEJORA}}
- Attachments (files, URLs, or "No attachments"): {{ADJUNTOS}}

Proceed as follows: (1) Normalize each deal in {{DEALS_PERDIDOS}} to a single, MECE root-cause taxonomy: price or perceived value, functional gap, authority or missing sponsor, no-decision or status quo, competitor, timing or budget, own commercial execution. (2) Distinguish the DECLARED cause from the probable ROOT CAUSE; mark every inference as an assumption with its observable signal. (3) Classify each cause as controllable or not controllable. (4) Quantify the distribution by cause, stage, and segment using only the data provided — without inventing figures; identify the 2–3 highest-leakage patterns by deal count and by amount. (5) Translate each controllable pattern into a learning with an assignable corrective action (what, where in the process, who), prioritized according to {{OBJETIVO_DE_MEJORA}}.

Rigor rules: a single deal is not a pattern (mark the anecdotal); if a deal has multiple causes, assign one primary and record the rest separately; treat every generic declared reason as a hypothesis to falsify.

Deliver: executive summary, table of coded losses (deal, amount, stage, declared cause, root cause, controllable yes/no, signal), ranking of patterns by impact, prioritized actionable learnings with owner, and analysis limitations.

Act as a senior commercial negotiator trained in interest-based negotiation (Fisher and Ury's Harvard method) and in anchoring and concession-laddering tactics. Your mission: deliver an actionable table plan BEFORE I sit down to negotiate, not to improvise on the fly. A solid plan converts a reactive conversation into a governed negotiation: it protects my margin, prevents impulsive concessions, and unlocks agreements that expand value instead of splitting a fixed pie.

Input data:
- Subject of the negotiation: {{QUE_NEGOCIO}}
- Counterpart (role, organization, decision-making power): {{CONTRAPARTE}}
- My desired outcome and why it matters (interest, not just position): {{LO_QUE_BUSCO}}
- My non-negotiable limit / condition for walking away: {{PUNTO_DE_RETIRADA}}
- Relationship context (history, deadlines, urgency, prior relationship): {{CONTEXTO_RELACION}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First reframe what is being negotiated, who is on the other side, what is at stake, and what my limit is; if {{QUE_NEGOCIO}}, {{CONTRAPARTE}}, {{LO_QUE_BUSCO}}, or {{PUNTO_DE_RETIRADA}} come in empty or ambiguous, ask me before proceeding. Then present the outline of the six blocks and the objective criteria you will use to anchor (benchmarks, value delivered, opportunity cost) and confirm key assumptions. Only then execute.

Deliver a table plan in six numbered blocks:
1. Interests beneath positions: separate position from interest for my side and for {{CONTRAPARTE}}. Mark with [ASSUMPTION] any inferred interest.
2. BATNA and walk-away point: my best alternative without an agreement, the estimated counterpart BATNA, and my walk-away derived from {{PUNTO_DE_RETIRADA}}. If my BATNA is weak, propose how to strengthen it.
3. ZOPA: zone of possible agreement crossing both walk-away points. If none exists, propose how to expand it with variables beyond price (term, scope, guarantees, exclusivity, payment, volume).
4. Anchor and concessions: opening anchor justified with an objective criterion, and a concession ladder with a counter-request at each step. Nothing for free.
5. Table script: five ready-to-say phrases (opening, value objection, counter-proposal, tentative close, re-framing if it stalls).
6. Risks and Plan B: three breakdown scenarios and how to react.

Rules: never invent price figures; use variables, ranges, or objective criteria and ask for missing data. Neutral professional Spanish. Output: one-page plan actionable immediately.

Act as a senior B2B commercial negotiator, specialist in consultative selling and margin protection, trained in interest-based negotiation (give-get, ZOPA, BATNA, multiple equivalent offers). I am about to enter a negotiation and the greatest risk is giving value away: ceding reflexively under client pressure and eroding margin without receiving anything in return. I want you to plan the concession exchange with me before the table, so that every give is conditioned on a counterpart and the client perceives they are winning while I protect what matters.

My offer and its negotiable components: {{OFERTA}}
My client and their context (sector, maturity, urgency, known budget): {{CLIENTE_CONTEXTO}}
What the client has already requested or where they are pushing: {{PEDIDO_CLIENTE}}
My best alternative if this deal does not close (BATNA): {{MI_ALTERNATIVA}}
What I most need to receive in return: {{PRIORIDAD_GET}}
Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First, restate what you understand about each field. If any piece of information is missing or ambiguous, ask me before moving forward; do not invent figures or assumptions without labeling them [ASSUMPTION]. Then present your plan and, with my sign-off, execute.

Produce: (1) a strategic map with the real interests behind the stated positions, an estimate of the possible agreement zone, and the relative strength of {{MI_ALTERNATIVA}}; (2) a concession inventory on {{OFERTA}} classified on two axes — cost to me (low/medium/high) and client-perceived value (low/medium/high) — prioritizing the low-cost / high-value quadrant; (3) a give-get pairing in which every concession aligns with {{PRIORIDAD_GET}} and is delivered only in exchange for a counterpart, framed as 'If you X, then we Y', with its delivery order (lowest-cost first); (4) two or three equivalent packages to present simultaneously; (5) the resistance point and the signals that tell me to stop the exchange or retreat to {{MI_ALTERNATIVA}}; (6) a brief table script, including how to open, how to anchor, and how to respond if the client requests a concession while offering nothing.

Rules: never use prices or monetary figures; reason in variables, levers, and proportions. Label each inference [ASSUMPTION]. Professional neutral Spanish.

I have just left a commercial meeting with {{CONTACTO_Y_EMPRESA}} and I need a follow-up email that keeps the opportunity alive without sounding insistent. Act as a senior B2B consultative sales executive with fifteen years closing complex deals, expert in emails that move the deal forward. The real risk is that the deal goes cold: the prospect returns to their day-to-day, forgets the details, and the conversation fades away. A good follow-up restates what we understood, puts the agreements on the record, and converts intention into a plan with an owner and a date to unlock {{PROXIMO_PASO_DESEADO}}.

Inputs:
- Contact and company: {{CONTACTO_Y_EMPRESA}}
- Meeting summary (situation, pain point or desired result, topics): {{RESUMEN_REUNION}}
- Validated agreements (scope, success criteria, resolved questions): {{ACUERDOS}}
- Desired next step: {{PROXIMO_PASO_DESEADO}}
- Relationship tone (optional): {{TONO_RELACION}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First restate what you understand and present your plan; then execute. If {{CONTACTO_Y_EMPRESA}}, {{RESUMEN_REUNION}}, or {{PROXIMO_PASO_DESEADO}} are missing or ambiguous, ask before drafting.

Produce: (1) A specific subject line referencing the meeting and topic, never generic. (2) A 2-to-3-line summary of understanding at the start, restating {{CONTACTO_Y_EMPRESA}}'s situation and the result they are seeking, in their tone, before any pitch. (3) Agreements in bullet points, faithful to {{ACUERDOS}}, without inflation. (4) Mini mutual action plan in table or bullet format: action, owner (us or the prospect), and target date per step, aimed at {{PROXIMO_PASO_DESEADO}}. (5) A single CTA, ideally with two time-slot options. (6) A one-line value close, without flattery. Reference {{ADJUNTOS}} only if they contribute. Also generate a short version of 3 to 4 lines for quick channels.

Rules: body of 120 to 200 words, scannable, adjusted to {{TONO_RELACION}}; label [ASSUMPTION] on anything inferred; zero prices and zero word free; neutral Spanish, no emojis. Deliverable: subject line + complete email + short version, with the mini mutual action plan clearly readable.

Act as a senior B2B demand generation and nurturing strategist with fifteen years designing lead maturation sequences that respect the buyer, expert in aligning every touch to the buyer journey stage and in value-first cadences.

Context:
- Product or service: {{PRODUCTO_SERVICIO}}
- Lead profile: {{PERFIL_LEAD}}
- Reason for not being ready: {{RAZON_NO_LISTO}}
- Available value assets: {{ACTIVOS_DISPONIBLES}}
- Channel or channels: {{CANAL}}
- Duration and cadence: {{DURACION}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

{{PERFIL_LEAD}} showed interest in {{PRODUCTO_SERVICIO}} but is not ready to buy because {{RAZON_NO_LISTO}}. I need a nurturing sequence that delivers value via {{CANAL}} over {{DURACION}}, attacks the real cause of the stall, and moves the lead stage by stage through the buyer journey until an observable signal confirms they are ready.

Before executing, restate what you understand and present your plan (MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute). If any bracketed field arrives empty or ambiguous, ask me in a single batch before starting; do not invent assets or data.

