1. Interpret: restate the problem; identify entity, temporal order, and when two rows belong to the same streak according to {{GRANULARIDAD}}. Mark every assumption with [ASSUMPTION] (time zone, duplicates on the same step, ties in the order, strictly uniform step, out-of-calendar events, open edges at the start or end of the series).
2. Plan: present the plan of four CTEs and the signature of each window function before writing the complete SQL.
3. Execute and deliver:
   - CTE 1 normalization: order by {{COLUMNA_ENTIDAD}} and {{COLUMNA_TIEMPO}}, deduplicate repeated events on the same step, and leave one row per {{GRANULARIDAD}} unit.
   - CTE 2 island key: difference between ROW_NUMBER OVER (PARTITION BY {{COLUMNA_ENTIDAD}} ORDER BY {{COLUMNA_TIEMPO}}) and the temporal index normalized to {{GRANULARIDAD}}; document the LAG plus cumulative sum alternative and when to prefer each technique.
   - CTE 3 islands: group by entity and island key; start, end, duration, and count per streak.
   - CTE 4 gaps: with LEAD over the islands, gap between end and next start per entity, duration in the {{GRANULARIDAD}} unit.
   - Final commented SQL, explanation of why the sequence difference is constant within an island, assumptions, and a validation query that reconstructs the total range per entity.

Closing criterion: SQL executable in {{MOTOR_SQL}} without syntax from another engine; island key built and explained; each CTE commented; duplicates and ties resolved; gaps with LEAD and correct duration; validation query included; assumptions with [ASSUMPTION]; zero columns outside {{TABLA_FUENTE}}.

Act as a senior analytics engineer expert in Kimball methodology and model optimization on columnar MPP engines. I need a comparative evaluation between the One Big Table (OBT) denormalized pattern and a dimensional star model for the consumption layer of my columnar store, because this choice conditions query cost, storage cost, pipeline maintainability, and consumer experience for years, and choosing wrong forces re-modeling productive marts and reprocessing history.

My context:
- Target columnar engine: {{MOTOR_COLUMNAR}}
- Analytical use case: {{CASO_DE_USO}}
- Fact table volume: {{VOLUMETRIA_HECHOS}}
- Dimensions (cardinality and SCD type): {{DIMENSIONES}}
- Refresh frequency and tolerable latency: {{FRECUENCIA_REFRESCO}}
- BI tools and consumers: {{HERRAMIENTAS_BI}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First, restate my case in your own words and declare each missing piece of data as [ASSUMPTION]; present your plan before executing. Do not invent figures: if the engine, volume, dimensions, refresh, or consumers are missing, ask before evaluating.

Then deliver: (1) a comparative table with six axes, one per row, each with a verdict OBT/Star/Depends and evidence: query performance and cost under column pruning and partition pruning; storage cost and compression (OBT redundancy vs. normalization); handling of SCD type 1 and type 2 and impact on backfills; freshness, latency, and pipeline complexity; BI and ad hoc SQL experience; governance, lineage, and reuse of conformed dimensions. (2) Quantified decision thresholds (cardinality, fact volume, dimension change ratio, join frequency). (3) Assessment of the hybrid pattern (intermediate star + OBT materialized at consumption) and when you recommend it. (4) Prioritized recommendation of one to three lines tied to {{MOTOR_COLUMNAR}} and {{CASO_DE_USO}}, with justification. Distinguish query cost from storage cost. Mark [INFERENCE] and [ASSUMPTION]. Neutral language, no emojis.

Act as a senior data architect expert in dimensional modeling (Kimball) and relational OLTP/OLAP design, with strong judgment on referential integrity and join performance. Produce an entity-by-entity key strategy for this model.

Entities and candidate natural keys: {{ENTIDADES_Y_CLAVES_NATURALES}}
Engine and version: {{MOTOR_BD}}
Load type: {{TIPO_CARGA}}
History requirement (SCD2 vs. SCD1 per entity): {{REQUISITO_HISTORIA}}
Critical joins and expected cardinality: {{JOINS_CRITICOS}}
Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret, Plan, Execute. First, restate the received model and present your plan (factor matrix and entity order); only then decide. Mark as [ASSUMPTION] all absent data (engine, volume, mutability of each natural key) instead of inventing it, and ask for the minimum missing data before committing to an irreversible decision.

For each entity in {{ENTIDADES_Y_CLAVES_NATURALES}} evaluate five factors: stability and immutability of the natural key; need for SCD2 per {{REQUISITO_HISTORIA}}; cardinality and join cost per {{JOINS_CRITICOS}}; exposure in APIs/URLs and privacy (the natural key is often PII); single or multi-source origin and collision risk when integrating.

Recommend per entity surrogate (integer identity/sequence; UUID only if justified by distributed generation or not exposing cardinality), natural, or hybrid (surrogate PK + natural UNIQUE, default pattern when in doubt), with justification tied to the factors. Define the referential integrity pattern (FK to the surrogate, never to a mutable natural key; natural as indexed UNIQUE). Specify SCD2 (surrogate per version as PK, durable business key, validity from/to, current-version flag, attribute hash). Analyze performance (FK and index width, integer vs. composite/string joins, write cost). Deliver illustrative DDL valid for {{MOTOR_BD}} with surrogate PK, natural UNIQUE, and FK.

Close with per-entity decision table, referential integrity and SCD2 pattern, performance analysis, DDL, and list of assumptions and pending items. If any critical input is missing, ask for it before proceeding.

Act as a senior data engineer specializing in columnar data warehouse optimization, with measurable experience reducing bytes scanned. I want an actionable partitioning and clustering strategy for a growing fact table that scans more data than it needs, raising cost and latency.

