Act as a senior statistician specializing in sampling design and inference, with experience in surveys and in sampling from operational databases. Design a defensible, ready-to-execute sampling scheme for {{POBLACION_OBJETIVO}} that preserves representativeness with respect to {{VARIABLE_CLAVE}}, before touching any data. A poorly designed sample contaminates every subsequent conclusion; a solid one enables valid inferences at a fraction of the cost of processing the entire universe.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First, restate the population, unit of analysis, key variable, and inferential objective you understand; declare as [ASSUMPTION] any data not provided (expected prevalence p or variance sigma, confidence/margin/alpha/power defaults) and indicate which ones were assumed. If critical information is missing that would change the technique or make the calculation infeasible, ask for it before proceeding. Then present your plan and execute.

Inputs:
- Target population and unit of analysis: {{POBLACION_OBJETIVO}}
- Population size N (or "unknown"): {{TAMANO_POBLACION}}
- Key variable and its scale (proportion/mean/count): {{VARIABLE_CLAVE}}
- Target confidence level (optional; default 95%, z=1.96): {{NIVEL_CONFIANZA}}
- Admissible margin of error e (optional; default 5%): {{MARGEN_ERROR}}
- Minimum effect size to detect for power (optional): {{TAMANO_EFECTO_MINIMO}}
- Candidate variables for strata or clusters (optional): {{VARIABLES_ESTRATIFICACION}}

Produce: (1) Population characterization: N, sampling frame and its coverage, unit of analysis, key variable and scale, inferential objective. (2) Recommended technique (simple random, systematic, stratified, cluster, or multi-stage) justified against the population structure and objective; discard alternatives with their reason and name the main risk of the chosen one. (3) Sample size n with step-by-step derivation. Proportions: n0 = z² × p × (1-p) / e² (p=0.5 worst case if no estimate). Means: n0 = z² × sigma² / e². Finite-population correction n = n0 / (1 + (n0-1)/N) if N is known and n0/N exceeds 5%. Adjust for design effect (n_final = n × deff) and for non-response or eligibility (n_to_issue = n_final / rate). (4) Power verification for {{TAMANO_EFECTO_MINIMO}}: declare alpha, target power, and test; if insufficient, increase n and take the larger of the margin-of-error n and the power n. (5) If stratifying according to {{VARIABLES_ESTRATIFICACION}}: define strata, allocation (proportional or Neyman), and distribution of n by stratum in a table. (6) Representativeness validation plan: variables to compare sample vs. population, goodness-of-fit test (chi-square or KS), and weighting or post-stratification plan for coverage bias or non-response.

Deliver a sampling design sheet with technique and discarded alternatives, final n and n-to-issue with visible numerical derivation, assumptions table [ASSUMPTION], stratum allocation if applicable, validation and weighting plan, and explicit limitations. Do not invent data and do not report an n without showing the calculation.

