- 1:1 frequency: {{FRECUENCIA_1A1}}
- Horizon for building solid trust: {{HORIZONTE_TEMPORAL}}
- Current bond status or relevant background: {{CONTEXTO_RELACION}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Premise: the first dysfunction of a team is the absence of vulnerability-based trust — the willingness to expose mistakes, doubts, and limits without fear of reprisal. That trust is built through reciprocal, graduated self-disclosure: escalating too quickly generates resistance, never escalating stalls the relationship.

Deliver a sequence of 1:1 questions in four levels of increasing vulnerability, ready to use:
1. Level 1, Work context and preferences (low emotional risk): 3 to 5 open questions about work style, communication, and rhythms.
2. Level 2, Motivations and aspirations: 3 to 5 questions about what energizes the collaborator and where they want to grow.
3. Level 3, Challenges, fears, and friction moments: 3 to 5 questions about obstacles, recent mistakes, and areas of insecurity.
4. Level 4, Mutual feedback and reciprocal expectations: 3 to 5 questions that open bidirectional feedback and relationship agreements.

For each level indicate: the introduction 1:1 number consistent with {{HORIZONTE_TEMPORAL}} and {{FRECUENCIA_1A1}}, the leader's opener (what the leader reveals first, in one or two sentences, before asking) and an observable signal of readiness to move to the next level. Adjust escalation pace: higher {{SENIORITY}} and more tension in {{CONTEXTO_RELACION}} tolerate less speed. Close with 3 premature-escalation signals and a concrete script to step back a level without blame.

Rules: open questions, not yes/no, not leading; nothing that sounds like a performance interrogation; neutral professional language, no empty jargon. If a critical piece of data is missing, ask for it before designing. First restate what you understood and present your plan; then execute.

Act as a senior executive coach specializing in psychological safety and upward feedback, with mastery of the start-stop-continue framework and Amy Edmondson's research on high-performing teams. I need the complete script for a 1:1 where my direct report gives me feedback on me as their leader, using inverted start-stop-continue (having them tell me what I should START, STOP, and KEEP doing). Upward feedback almost never flows on its own because the power imbalance makes staying silent rational for the person who reports to me; the script must invert that equation and place the risk of the conversation on me.

Context:
- My role and relationship with the person: {{TU_ROL_Y_RELACION}}
- Direct report's name or alias: {{NOMBRE_REPORTE}}
- Team context and prior upward feedback history: {{CONTEXTO_EQUIPO}}
- Recent trigger or sensitive topic (optional): {{ANTECEDENTE_FEEDBACK}}
- Date or timing of the next 1:1: {{PROXIMO_1A1}}
- Leadership behavior to prioritize (optional): {{FOCO_OPCIONAL}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing. If an essential datum is missing, ask in a single batch before drafting; do not invent sensitive data. Flag every inference as [ASSUMPTION].

Deliver the script in five sections: (1) Opening frame of 60–90 seconds that declares my intention, names the power imbalance, and promises no reprisals, calibrated to the actual starting level of psychological safety. (2) Three START / STOP / KEEP blocks, each with one main open question and two probing follow-ups that lower the social cost of criticizing me (nothing binary, nothing fishing for praise, nothing defensive). (3) Reception script: phrases to validate, paraphrase, and ask for examples without justifying or defending myself, including what to do if I disagree. (4) Close: summary, 1–2 of my commitments with a date, and opening of the follow-up loop. (5) Three self-censorship signals with their in-the-moment recovery move. Neutral professional language, no emojis, ready to read aloud.

Act as a senior executive coach and difficult-conversations facilitator, trained in the SBI (Situation-Behavior-Impact) model from the Center for Creative Leadership. Prepare a one-on-one feedback conversation with someone with whom I have the relationship {{RELACION_RECEPTOR}}, in tone {{TONO}}, with the purpose {{RESULTADO_ESPERADO}} (reinforcement or improvement).

Fact data:
- Observed situation (when and where): {{SITUACION_OBSERVADA}}
- Behavior (conduct as a camera would record it): {{COMPORTAMIENTO_HECHO}}
- Concrete impact on me, the team, the client, or the outcome: {{IMPACTO_OBSERVADO}}
- Attachments or context: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Deliverable: a script ready to read in the conversation, in labeled blocks S | Situation, B | Behavior, I | Impact, plus three variants of the opening phrase according to {{TONO}}.

Method: first restate what you understand and separate fact from interpretation; mark as [ASSUMPTION] any datum not provided. If the exact moment, observable conduct, or impact is missing, ask before drafting. Present the S-B-I skeleton one line per block for validation and only then draft the full script.

Rules: Situation names a specific moment and place, without always/never; Behavior is observable and without personality adjectives or attributed intention; Impact is in the first person, not a reproach; close, if improvement, includes an open question plus verifiable follow-up agreement, and if reinforcement, names the behavior to repeat and why. If a {{DATUM}} is missing, ask me one at a time before moving forward.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Act as a senior leadership coach specialized in high-stakes conversations, trained in the Crucial Conversations model (Patterson, Grenny, McMillan, Switzler) and in facilitating psychological safety. We are going to prepare a crucial conversation: an exchange with opposing opinions, intense emotions, and a result that matters, where improvising leads to silence or verbal violence. The deliverable is a one-page preparation script, in neutral, professional Spanish, without judgments about the other person.

Case data:
- Difficult topic and why it was postponed: {{TEMA_DIFICIL}}
- Person and relationship/hierarchy: {{PERSONA_Y_RELACION}}
- What is at stake: {{LO_QUE_ESTA_EN_JUEGO}}
- My pattern under pressure (silence or verbal violence): {{MI_PATRON_BAJO_PRESION}}
- Desired outcome (for myself, the other, and the relationship): {{RESULTADO_DESEADO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}
If a critical piece of data is missing, ask for it before moving forward; do not invent context or data about the other person.

Work through these steps, one at a time, waiting for my response before moving to the next; return the draft in my own words, not theory:
1. Start with the heart: separate what I say I want from what I really want about {{TEMA_DIFICIL}}; define goal for myself, for the other, and for the relationship; name and discard one hidden goal.
2. Master the story: collect only observable facts about {{TEMA_DIFICIL}}, separate them from interpretation, identify the villain/victim/helpless story and reframe it.
3. Draft the STATE script in first person, ready to say: S facts, T story with tentativeness, A ask for the other's view, T firm tact, E encourage testing.
4. Design safety: Contrasting statement (NOT / YES) and CRIB plan aligned with {{RESULTADO_DESEADO}}, with a concrete mutual goal.
5. Learn to watch: signals that {{MI_PATRON_BAJO_PRESION}} is activating or that the other person is entering silence/violence, each with its action to restore dialogue.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate the case and present the plan before executing. At close, consolidate the one-page script and verify: real vs. declared intention with one hidden goal discarded; facts and story separated; STATE in first person ready to say; Contrasting with NOT and YES; CRIB with concrete mutual goal; each signal with actionable response; professional tone without judgments.

Act as a senior executive coach in difficult conversations and feedback reception, trained in the Douglas Stone and Sheila Heen framework (Thanks for the Feedback) and in emotional regulation under stress. I am going to receive, or have already received, critical feedback that activates my defensive reaction, and I need to process it without reacting impulsively so I don't discard what's valuable or damage the relationship.

Data about my case:
- Feedback received: {{FEEDBACK_RECIBIDO}}
- Who is giving it and my relationship with them: {{QUIEN_Y_RELACION}}
- Context of the conversation: {{CONTEXTO}}
- My reaction (felt or feared): {{MI_REACCION}}
- Desired outcome (optional): {{RESULTADO_DESEADO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Receiving feedback well is a trainable skill: the defensive reaction springs from three triggers (truth, relationship, identity) which, once named, stop hijacking the conversation. Before executing, restate in one sentence what you understand about my situation and present your plan; if {{FEEDBACK_RECIBIDO}}, {{QUIEN_Y_RELACION}}, {{CONTEXTO}}, or {{MI_REACCION}} are empty or ambiguous, ask for them in a brief list of at most four questions before moving forward.

Produce a personal script with: (1) diagnosis of the dominant trigger (truth/relationship/identity) with evidence, acknowledging the blend; (2) one sentence that separates the merit of the feedback from my feelings toward who is giving it; (3) three exact comprehension questions that probe, not defend; (4) the '10% probably true' without self-flagellation, with the data that supports it; (5) identity reframe in one sentence, from verdict to specific behavior; (6) script with exact phrases for receiving, probing, and closing with dignity while requesting time if needed, aligned with {{RESULTADO_DESEADO}} when provided; (7) micro-experiment in improvement with an observable success criterion at 2–4 weeks.

Mark as [ASSUMPTION] every inference about others' intentions. If {{FEEDBACK_RECIBIDO}} describes seriously harmful conduct (harassment, discrimination, illegality) or an abusive pattern, say so candidly and do not reframe it as a mere growth opportunity. Neutral and professional Spanish, no condescension or empty self-help; phrases ready to say aloud.

Act as a senior executive coach specialized in performance feedback and difficult conversations, with command of the SBI model (Situation-Behavior-Impact). I have an attitude complaint about someone on my team and I need to convert it into a useful feedback conversation, translating the label into observable behaviors with measurable impact.

Data:
- Attitude complaint as currently expressed: {{QUEJA_ACTITUD}}
- Observed examples (what happened, when, in what situation, frequency): {{EJEMPLOS_OBSERVADOS}}
- Team context (composition, moment, how it affects work): {{CONTEXTO_EQUIPO}}
- Hierarchical relationship and conversation channel: {{RELACION_Y_CANAL}}
- Desired conversation outcome: {{RESULTADO_DESEADO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Give me a package with three parts: (1) translation table that converts {{QUEJA_ACTITUD}} into 2 to 4 observable, verifiable, judgment-free behaviors, each with its concrete impact according to {{CONTEXTO_EQUIPO}}; (2) ready-to-use SBI conversation script with an opening that protects the relationship, factual Situation + Behavior, Impact statement, and open question, adjusted to {{RELACION_Y_CANAL}}; (3) close with a verifiable future-behavior agreement and follow-up method, aligned with {{RESULTADO_DESEADO}}. Rules: no behavior with identity labels or intention assumptions; do not invent examples or figures; if {{EJEMPLOS_OBSERVADOS}} doesn't provide enough facts, ask me before drafting and mark [PENDING CONFIRMATION] on any data without observable backing; respectful and non-accusatory tone that separates conduct from person. If any essential data is missing, ask me before starting. >> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Act as a senior leadership coach specialized in peer feedback and influence without hierarchical authority, with command of the SBI model (Situation-Behavior-Impact) and difficult conversation techniques. I am going to prepare a feedback conversation toward {{PAR}}, a colleague at my same level over whom I have no formal authority. The risk is not the content but the reading: without hierarchy to back it up, a poorly framed feedback reads as territorial invasion or personal judgment and erodes a relationship I depend on for collaboration.

Case data:
- Observable behavior: {{COMPORTAMIENTO}}
- Concrete impact: {{IMPACTO}}
- Shared goal: {{OBJETIVO}}
- Relationship status: {{RELACION}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before drafting, confirm that {{COMPORTAMIENTO}}, {{IMPACTO}}, and {{OBJETIVO}} are complete and concrete; if any is missing or ambiguous, ask me before continuing and do not invent the case.

Then deliver a ready-to-rehearse script: (1) opening that asks for permission and lowers the defense; (2) core message in SBI separating fact from interpretation, without attributing intentions, with the impact linked to {{OBJETIVO}}; (3) explicit anchor to the shared goal; (4) two probable defensive reactions calibrated to {{RELACION}}, each with a calm response; (5) close with a future request as a proposal to validate; (6) three phrases to avoid with their reason. Apply the lateral symmetry test: if you wouldn't say a phrase the same way to your own boss, reframe it. If the case reveals the right channel is not direct feedback, say so with justification.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Act as a senior executive coach specialized in boss-employee dynamics, upward communication, and difficult conversations, with the judgment to protect the relationship without diluting the message. I need to give upward feedback to my boss and I want a complete, calm, results-oriented script for a 1:1.

Data:
- Situation or behavior: {{SITUACION_O_COMPORTAMIENTO}}
- Impact on team and objectives: {{IMPACTO_EN_EQUIPO_Y_OBJETIVOS}}
- Desired outcome: {{RESULTADO_DESEADO}}
- Relationship and trust: {{RELACION_Y_CONFIANZA}}
- Urgency: {{URGENCIA}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First restate in your own words the behavior, its impact, the relationship, and the desired outcome; if {{SITUACION_O_COMPORTAMIENTO}} or {{IMPACTO_EN_EQUIPO_Y_OBJETIVOS}} are empty or ambiguous, ask for them before drafting (do not invent data or assume my boss's intentions). Then present the 5-step outline and the observation-impact-request framing for validation. Only then draft.

Deliver a script in 5 sections: (1) Outcome: translate {{RESULTADO_DESEADO}} into a concrete, observable, verifiable change, and make explicit why it benefits my boss's and the team's success. (2) Permission and timing: phrase to ask authorization plus recommendation for when and where (1:1, never in public or in crisis), calibrated by {{URGENCIA}} and {{RELACION_Y_CONFIANZA}}. (3) Core observation-impact-request message: observable fact from {{SITUACION_O_COMPORTAMIENTO}}, impact from {{IMPACTO_EN_EQUIPO_Y_OBJETIVOS}}, and a specific and actionable request; no character judgments or generalizations (always, never). (4) Handling reactions: up to three probable reactions (defensiveness, minimization, surprise) with a calm response each that reframes toward shared goals. (5) Close: confirm concrete agreements and propose follow-up with a date.

Also include: a plan B if my boss rejects the feedback or reacts badly (withdraw with dignity without burning the relationship) and a warning signal to abort or reschedule (boss overwhelmed, active crisis, real risk of retaliation). Consider {{ADJUNTOS}} if they add context. Tone of partner: professional, without complaint or submission. No emojis.

Act as a senior executive coach specialized in performance conversations and communication under pressure, with the judgment to distinguish real urgency from the leader's own discomfort. My problem: I'm torn between giving feedback in the heat of the moment or waiting for a better time. Deciding on impulse burns trust; deciding through avoidance lets the pattern repeat. I want you to convert that doubt into a timing decision with explicit, defensible, and replicable criteria.

Case data:
- Situation (observable fact, not judgment): {{SITUACION}}
- Severity (safety/ethical/legal risk or third-party/client harm, or only performance improvement): {{SEVERIDAD}}
- Recipient's state (emotional and workload right now): {{ESTADO_RECEPTOR}}
- Event recurrence (upcoming moment where the behavior repeats, when): {{RECURRENCIA_EVENTO}}
- Relationship (trust and track record between sender and recipient, optional): {{RELACION}}

Proceed as follows: first classify {{SEVERIDAD}}. If there is a safety, ethical, legal, or third-party/client harm risk, the feedback is almost always NOW: contain before polishing the message. Second, evaluate the learning opportunity: how much is lost by delay versus the cost of interrupting the context; weigh {{RECURRENCIA_EVENTO}} (if it repeats soon, urgency increases). Third, evaluate receptiveness using {{ESTADO_RECEPTOR}}, available privacy, and the sender's own regulation; calibrate with {{RELACION}}.

Deliver: single timing recommendation (NOW / WITHIN 24-72H / IN A SCHEDULED SPACE) naming the dominant axis; risks of the opposite option; and the operational piece: if waiting, a concrete trigger for the best moment plus a two-sentence opening script; if now, an opening that lowers the guard without diluting the message. Do not answer "it depends": commit to one option. If real severity, recipient's state, recurrence, or relationship trust is missing, ask me before recommending; do not fill in with untagged assumptions.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Act as a senior people leader and Human Resources partner, expert in corrective feedback, labor documentation, and the SBI model (Situation-Behavior-Impact) from the Center for Creative Leadership. Draft a corrective feedback memo ready for the record for {{COLABORADOR}}. Before writing, restate in one line what you understood and present your plan; if {{COLABORADOR}}, {{COMPORTAMIENTO_HECHOS}}, {{IMPACTO}}, or {{EXPECTATIVA}} are missing or empty, ask for them and do not proceed with those gaps. Do not invent data: every unconfirmed inference goes tagged as [ASSUMPTION].

Data:
- Person and role: {{COLABORADOR}}
- Observable behavior and facts (with date and context): {{COMPORTAMIENTO_HECHOS}}
- Concrete impact: {{IMPACTO}}
- Expected behavior: {{EXPECTATIVA}}
- Change deadline and follow-up date: {{PLAZO_SEGUIMIENTO}}
- Who receives or signs the record: {{DESTINATARIO_REGISTRO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

The memo must have: header (person, date, subject, record type, and whether corrective or disciplinary); SBI facts block where Situation locates when and where, Behavior from {{COMPORTAMIENTO_HECHOS}} describes observable conduct without adjectives or intention judgments, and Impact from {{IMPACTO}} is concrete and measurable where possible; expectation derived from {{EXPECTATIVA}} in observable behaviors with agreements, available support, and deadline from {{PLAZO_SEGUIMIENTO}}; follow-up close with review date, consequences for not changing only if applicable, space for the person's response, and signature lines for {{DESTINATARIO_REGISTRO}}. Remove generalizations like "always" or "never" and intention assumptions. Deliver: (a) the complete memo ready to paste and record; (b) an evidence-control note listing which statements are verifiable facts and which remained as [ASSUMPTION]. Professional, respectful, and non-accusatory tone, second person, neutral Spanish, no emojis.

Act as a senior leadership coach specializing in restoring trust after management errors, grounded in Aaron Lazare's 4 R apology model: recognition, remorse, responsibility, and repair. Produce an apology from a leader to their team that the affected people recognize as genuine, not as image management.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret, Plan, Execute. First, rephrase in your own words the error, its impact, and the goal; mark with [ASSUMPTION] what you infer. Declare the structure, length, and register according to the channel. If ERROR_COMMITTED, IMPACT_ON_PEOPLE, CHANNEL, or POSSIBLE_REPAIR is missing, ask for it before drafting.

Data:
- Error committed: {{ERROR_COMETIDO}}
- Impact on people: {{IMPACTO_EN_PERSONAS}}
- Delivery channel: {{CANAL}}
- Possible repair: {{REPARACION_POSIBLE}}
- Relationship and tone (optional): {{RELACION_Y_TONO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Deliver three pieces. First, an impact map of 3 to 5 bullets written from the perspective of the person who lived it, translating {{ERROR_COMETIDO}} and {{IMPACTO_EN_PERSONAS}}. Second, the apology ready to deliver, adjusted to {{CANAL}} and {{RELACION_Y_TONO}}, following this sequence: name the specific fact without euphemisms or passive voice; name the impact on the people; assume responsibility in the first person without 'but' or conditionals; verifiable repair derived from {{REPARACION_POSIBLE}} with what and when; observable commitment to change. Third, a table of three prohibited phrases (conditional 'if anyone felt', justification, blame-shifting) with their responsible rewrite.

Rules: first person, short sentences, action verbs; zero empty corporate language; professional neutral English; no emojis. Do not invent facts or promises; the repair derives only from {{REPARACION_POSIBLE}}. If the error affects a single person, the private apology precedes any public gesture.

Act as a senior OKR coach and team strategy expert, with experience facilitating bidirectional alignment cycles in product and operations organizations. Your assignment is to produce an OKR alignment report connecting the objectives of {{NOMBRE_EQUIPO}} to the corporate strategy of cycle {{HORIZONTE_CICLO}}, avoiding mechanical cascade. Before executing, rephrase what you understand and present your plan; if essential data is missing, ask before continuing.

Inputs:
- Corporate OKRs: {{OKRS_CORPORATIVOS}}
- Team-proposed OKRs: {{OKRS_EQUIPO}}
- Team: {{NOMBRE_EQUIPO}}
- Cycle horizon: {{HORIZONTE_CICLO}}
- Neighboring teams with dependencies: {{EQUIPOS_VECINOS}}
- Already-known dependencies or tensions: {{DEPENDENCIAS_CONOCIDAS}}

Deliver: (1) Vertical map objective-by-objective with classification — direct contribution / enables / not related — and justification. (2) Traceability of each team KR toward its superior KR or goal with explicit causal logic. (3) Gaps: corporate objectives with no contribution and team KRs that do not roll up to anything (operational health vs cut). (4) Horizontal map per team in {{EQUIPOS_VECINOS}}: what I give, what I receive, concrete commitment, confirmation date. (5) Resource or contradiction conflicts with resolution or escalation path. (6) Counterproposals to leadership with argument. Mark [ASSUMPTION] on all inferred links; distinguish ambitious-achievable KR from KR with no line of sight. Do not invent OKRs absent from the inputs.

Act as a senior OKR facilitator with experience conducting weekly confidence check-ins in product and operations teams, trained in the practice of confidence-based check-ins (Wodtke, Doerr). We are going to conduct the team's weekly OKR check-in in {{SEMANA_DEL_TRIMESTRE}}. The purpose is not to report percentage progress, but to update the achievement confidence level per Key Result, detect drifts while they can still be corrected, and leave with agreements that unblock what is in red — before a KR goes from recoverable to lost.

Input data:
- Quarterly OKRs, ideally with target metric per KR: {{OKRS_TRIMESTRE}}
- Confidence per KR recorded last week (scale 1 to 10, optional for the first week): {{CONFIANZA_SEMANA_ANTERIOR}}
- Moment in the quarter: {{SEMANA_DEL_TRIMESTRE}}
- Already-identified blockers, risks, or dependencies: {{BLOQUEOS_CONOCIDOS}}
- Time available for the ritual: {{DURACION_RITUAL}}

First rephrase the OKRs, the moment in the quarter, and the goal of the ritual, and present your facilitation plan. If a critical data point is missing, ask before proceeding — do not invent it. Then deliver: (1) a status board per KR with current confidence 1 to 10, trend vs {{CONFIANZA_SEMANA_ANTERIOR}}, and color (green 7–10, yellow 4–6, red 1–3), ordered by risk; (2) the separation between green KRs (only reported as a block) and yellow or red ones (discussed within {{DURACION_RITUAL}}); (3) for each yellow or red KR: root blocker, concrete impact if not resolved this week, and an open unblocking question — never an imposed solution; (4) agreements, each with a single owner and a date; (5) a three-line summary for leadership with overall confidence, at-risk KR, and key decision. Always distinguish achievement confidence from percentage of progress. Mark what is not provided as [ASSUMPTION]. Flag every KR in red for two or more weeks as a candidate for escalation or re-scoping.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Rephrase what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Act as a senior OKR coach with years operating quarterly cycles in product and operations teams, trained in the 0.0–1.0 scoring discipline in the style of Doerr/Grove. You will produce an OKR scoring retrospective ready to review at close, understanding that scoring is not an exam but the input that decides where to bet focus for the next cycle: it calibrates whether targets were too easy or impossible, separates valid learning from underperformance so as not to penalize honest ambition, and converts numbers into portfolio decisions.

Input data:
- Quarter closing: {{TRIMESTRE}}
- Team or area owning the OKRs: {{EQUIPO}}
- Objectives with their Key Results (per KR: start, target, real value at close, and whether committed or aspirational): {{OKRS_Y_RESULTADOS}}
- Next cycle the decisions will feed: {{SIGUIENTE_CICLO}}
- Context of the quarter that affected execution (reorganizations, market, dependencies): {{CONTEXTO}}

If any of the three data points for a KR (start, target, close) is missing or committed vs aspirational is not indicated, ask before scoring; never invent values or mark them as real (if you assume something, label it [ASSUMPTION]).

Proceed as follows: 1) For each Key Result assign a score 0.0–1.0 with the visible evidence (start → target → real value); convention: for an aspirational KR, 0.7 = achieved, not the ceiling; committed expected close to 1.0. 2) Classify each KR as green 0.7–1.0, amber 0.4–0.69, or red 0.0–0.39, marking committed vs aspirational. 3) For each amber or red KR, separate the cause into two columns that are NOT mixed: legitimate learning vs execution failure, using {{CONTEXTO}} as evidence; if the data is ambiguous, declare what more you need. 4) Calculate the average score per Objective and issue a quarter verdict (over-ambitious / calibrated / under-ambitious) with the signal justifying it. 5) Deliver decisions for {{SIGUIENTE_CICLO}}: which KR to retire, which to reformulate, which to scale up, which risk to watch; each decision with a suggested owner and, where applicable, a tentative target metric.

Edge cases: binary KR scored 0 or 1, not proportional; qualitative KR evaluated by declared evidence with the criterion marked; over-achievement reported without capping at 1.0 but flagged as possibly under-ambitious target; all-green Objective is a low-calibration signal, not automatic success.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Rephrase what you understand and present your plan before executing. Deliver in professional neutral English, no emojis: scoring table per Objective/KR, learning-vs-execution diagnosis block, and decision list with owners.

Act as a senior team leader and OKR coach, expert in Measure What Matters (John Doerr) and in health metrics / guardrail metrics in the style of Google and Spotify. Your task is to separate what the team must pursue as a growth target from what it must only protect, to prevent optimizing one number from breaking the operational base (cobra effect) or triggering metric gaming.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First rephrase what you understand and present your classification plan; only then execute.

Context:
- Team and mission: {{EQUIPO_Y_MISION}}
- Quarterly objective: {{OBJETIVO_TRIMESTRE}}
- Current indicators (with value or trend): {{INDICADORES_ACTUALES}}
- Decisions and incentives tied to each indicator: {{DECISIONES_INCENTIVOS}}
- Risk tolerance (how much the operational base can degrade without pain): {{TOLERANCIA_RIESGO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If the mission, quarterly objective, indicators, or decisions and incentives are missing, ask before continuing — do not invent them.

Classify each indicator in exactly one category: (a) candidate OKR or Key Result we want to move, (b) health metric to protect, (c) vanity metric or noise to retire; justify each in one line. Apply the decision test: if pushing it aggressively can damage something valuable, it is an OKR with guardrails; if its healthy role is to stay stable or above a threshold, it is a health metric; if no one decides with it, it is noise. For each OKR, name at least one concrete gaming or cobra-effect risk and pair it with the health metric that guards it, with a minimum threshold according to {{TOLERANCIA_RIESGO}} (if not given, mark the threshold [ASSUMPTION]). For each health metric, define an alarm threshold, review frequency, and owner — with no growth target. Keep few OKRs for real quarterly focus.

Deliver: (1) table Indicator | Category | Justification; (2) OKR set with Key Results, gaming or cobra-effect risk, and paired health metric with minimum threshold; (3) list of health metrics with alarm threshold, cadence, and owner; (4) list of indicators to retire. Close with 2 or 3 focus decisions. Mark assumptions with [ASSUMPTION]; do not invent values. Professional neutral English, no emojis.

Act as a senior OKR coach with experience implementing the Doerr (Measure What Matters) and Grove (High Output Management) model in product and engineering teams. Diagnose and rewrite this OKR set.

Current set to diagnose: {{OKRS_ACTUALES}}
Team, area, or quarter: {{EQUIPO_O_TRIMESTRE}}
Context and desired ambition level (committed vs aspirational): {{CONTEXTO_AMBICION}}
Baselines, previous targets, or historical metrics (optional): {{LINEAS_BASE}}

Before executing, rephrase the received set, flag what is missing, and present your plan; if data is missing to rewrite without inventing figures, ask first and never fabricate numbers (mark illustrative ones as assumption).

Steps: 1) Inventory and number each Objective and each Key Result. 2) Evaluate each element against five tests: inspiring Objective vs disguised task; outcome KR vs output or effort; sandbagging (risk-free target); health KPI smuggled in as advance; binary KR or one without baseline or numeric target. 3) Classify each finding with a label (task-list, sandbagging, disguised-kpi, output-not-outcome, no-metric, ambiguous), severity (high/medium/low), and a one-line justification. 4) Rewrite the full set preserving the intent: memorable and ambitious Objectives, 2 to 4 measurable KRs per Objective with baseline and target, calibrated so that 70 percent is success, separating committed from aspirational. 5) Derive 3 anti-relapse rules from the dominant pattern.

Deliver: (1) findings table with severity, (2) rewritten set, (3) three anti-relapse rules. If an Objective is already healthy, say so instead of forcing a finding.

Act as a senior team leader and internal communicator, expert in the OKR framework (Andy Grove, John Doerr) and in purpose-driven communication (Simon Sinek's Golden Circle: why before what). I need to communicate to my team the OKRs for the quarter that is starting. The risk to avoid is that they arrive as a list of imposed goals: compliance without commitment. I want every person to see how their daily work moves the mission.

Data:
- Team mission: {{MISION_EQUIPO}}
- Quarter's strategic priority: {{PRIORIDAD_TRIMESTRE}}
- OKRs (objectives with their 2 to 4 measurable key results): {{OKRS}}
- Team roles or functions: {{ROLES_EQUIPO}}
- Follow-up cadence and format: {{CADENCIA_SEGUIMIENTO}}
- Desired channel and tone (optional): {{CANAL_Y_TONO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret and present your plan before executing. When interpreting, order the objectives by hierarchy (aspirational objective, then its measurable key results) and flag every key result without a metric, every objective that is actually a task, and every role without a line of sight to a key result. Do not invent figures: mark missing data as [to be filled in] and unconfirmed reasoning as [ASSUMPTION]. If an essential piece of data is missing, ask me before drafting.

Deliver a prose message with titled sections, ready for the meeting and for archiving, with: (1) opening with a purpose statement connecting {{MISION_EQUIPO}} to the quarter, before any goal; (2) the why: {{PRIORIDAD_TRIMESTRE}} explained and what is unlocked; (3) each objective in {{OKRS}} narrated as an aspiration, with its measurable key results and a sentence explaining why each one matters; (4) line of sight for each role in {{ROLES_EQUIPO}} to specific key results; (5) rules of the game: {{CADENCIA_SEGUIMIENTO}}, green/amber/red criteria, and a note about what an OKR is NOT (neither tasks nor individual evaluation); (6) close that invites commitment and opens questions, adjusted to {{CANAL_Y_TONO}} if provided. No prices, no the word free, neutral professional Spanish, no emojis.

Act as a senior strategy and operations leader, specialist in OKR design and portfolio prioritization under capacity constraints. I need to reduce an inflated list of objectives to the few that truly matter, with explicit and traceable criteria.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Before evaluating, restate what you understand I need to prioritize against {{PRIORIDAD_ESTRATEGICA}} in {{HORIZONTE_CICLO}}, and present your plan; then execute.

Data:
- Candidate objectives (one per line): {{LISTA_OBJETIVOS}}
- Cycle duration: {{HORIZONTE_CICLO}}
- Team's real capacity (people, available time, commitments already made): {{CAPACIDAD_EQUIPO}}
- Dominant strategic priority of the period: {{PRIORIDAD_ESTRATEGICA}}
- Maximum objectives to commit to: {{MAX_OBJETIVOS}} (if empty, assume 3–5)

Verify that you have {{LISTA_OBJETIVOS}}, {{HORIZONTE_CICLO}}, {{CAPACIDAD_EQUIPO}} and {{PRIORIDAD_ESTRATEGICA}}. If something is missing or vague, ask me before scoring; do not invent context.

Evaluate each objective through three lenses on a scale of 1 to 5: Impact on {{PRIORIDAD_ESTRATEGICA}}, Focus (alignment versus dispersion) and Capacity (real executability given {{CAPACIDAD_EQUIPO}}). Calculate a combined score, make each score's assumption visible and mark [ASSUMPTION] where there is no hard data. Flag dependencies or competition for the same people.

Order in a table by combined score and draw the cut line respecting {{CAPACIDAD_EQUIPO}}, not just the ranking: if the top items do not fit, lower the cut and explain why. Classify into COMMITTED (up to {{MAX_OBJETIVOS}}), ON HOLD (with the condition that reactivates each one) and DISCARDED (with each one's reason). Zero objectives left unclassified.

Close with the coherence test: if what is committed is achieved, is {{PRIORIDAD_ESTRATEGICA}} reached? If there is a gap, name it and say which on-hold objective would fill it. Deliver the portfolio in three groups, the scoring table with assumptions, the justified cut line and the verdict.

Act as a senior people manager and talent-onboarding specialist, trained in Michael Watkins' transitions framework (The First 90 Days, HBR Press). Design a 90-day onboarding plan in three horizons (1-30 learn, 31-60 contribute, 61-90 lead with autonomy) that accelerates the person's break-even point, reduces early turnover, and aligns expectations among the person, their leader, and the stakeholders. This is not a welcome checklist.

Input data:
- Role and level: {{ROL_NIVEL}}
- Responsibilities and expected outcomes: {{RESPONSABILIDADES}}
- Team context (size, maturity, moment): {{CONTEXTO_EQUIPO}}
- Transition type (stable area, in-change, or start-up from scratch): {{TIPO_TRANSICION}}
- Day-90 success criterion: {{CRITERIO_EXITO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before drafting, restate what you understand and present your plan. If any key data is missing, ask for it before proceeding; do not invent it.

For each horizon, develop in this order: (1) objective in one sentence; (2) learning milestones covering product, processes, tools, and customer, each with an observable completion criterion; (3) contribution milestones: verifiable deliverables and realistic early wins for the role's level; (4) key relationships: stakeholder archetypes and the purpose of each link; (5) coaching rhythm: cadence of 1:1s, checkpoints, and who supports; (6) early-warning signals of drift, with the suggested corrective action.

Adjust depth to the transition type (start-up = more diagnosis; in-change = stabilize before proposing; stable = earlier wins) and calibrate win ambition to the role level. Close with a summary table of the three horizons, the list of conditions the leader must guarantee (access, mentor or buddy, clarity of expectations, first deliverable), and the explicit connection between horizon 90 and {{CRITERIO_EXITO}}. Flag every inference with [ASSUMPTION]. Do not invent names, metrics, or dates.

Act as a senior people leader specialized in onboarding experience, with experience coordinating preboarding across leadership, human resources, technical teams, and systems. Design an actionable preboarding sequence, ready to execute, for the window between offer signing and the first day — the most fragile moment of the entire onboarding: the person has already said yes, but does not yet belong, is still exposed to counter-offers and pre-start regret.

Data:
- Role joining: {{ROL}}
- Work modality: {{MODALIDAD_TRABAJO}}
- Time until start: {{TIEMPO_HASTA_INGRESO}}
- Team culture: {{CULTURA_EQUIPO}}
- Business priorities: {{PRIORIDADES_NEGOCIO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Follow the MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate role, modality, time, and culture; if a critical data point is missing, ask before proceeding and flag assumptions with [ASSUMPTION]. Present the plan (objectives and skeleton of timeline and four workstreams) before drafting the messages.

Deliver: (1) 4 to 6 objectives, each with an observable success signal; (2) milestone timeline that fits within {{TIEMPO_HASTA_INGRESO}}, with action, responsible party (leader, human resources, team, or systems), and channel per milestone; (3) the four parallel workstreams with concrete actions: administrative and legal, logistics and access, social and cultural, role and expectations, adjusted to {{CULTURA_EQUIPO}} and {{PRIORIDADES_NEGOCIO}}; (4) the three drafted messages from the hiring leader ready to send (welcome after signing, team introduction, day-one agenda); (5) 3 to 4 warning signals with their concrete response action; (6) day-before-day-one checklist across all four workstreams. Neutral professional English, no emojis.

Act as a senior people leader expert in probationary-period evaluation, evidence-based feedback (SBI model: Situation-Behavior-Impact), and difficult performance conversations. I am closing the 90-day probationary period for {{PERSONA_Y_ROL}} and I need to move from scattered impressions to an evidence-based evaluation and to an unambiguous continuity decision: confirm, extend, or not continue.

Context:
- Person, role, level, and start date: {{PERSONA_Y_ROL}}
- Expectations agreed at the start (responsibilities, goals, behaviors, onboarding milestones): {{EXPECTATIVAS_INICIALES}}
- Evidence observed during the 90 days, with concrete examples: {{EVIDENCIA_OBSERVADA}}
- Prior feedback already delivered and the person's response: {{FEEDBACK_PREVIO}}
- Preliminary inclination and risk or strength signals: {{INCLINACION_Y_SENALES}}
- Legal or probationary-period policy framework: {{MARCO_POLITICAS}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before executing, restate what you understand about the case and present your plan; if an essential data point is missing, ask me for it rather than inventing it. Deliver: (1) the reconstructed expectation baseline, flagging as [ASSUMPTION] anything not documented; (2) a table of expectation vs. evidence with a rating of exceeds/meets/in progress/below, separating facts from interpretations; (3) two to three reinforcement messages and two to three adjustment messages in SBI format, actionable and free of personality judgments; (4) a single continuity recommendation with reasoning, verifiable criteria, and the actions requiring HR or legal validation flagged before communicating; (5) the scenario plan (development focus, improvement plan with goals/dates/consequences, or respectful communication) and the conversation script with opening, body, handling of likely reactions, next steps, and commitments. Neutral professional tone, no emojis. Do not present interpretations as facts or make judgments about the person.

Act as a senior talent leader and employer branding specialist, expert in employee value propositions (EVP), honest recruitment narratives, and messages that filter by genuine fit rather than inflating expectations. I need to articulate why it is worth joining my team with an honest narrative of mission, challenge, and growth, avoiding the double risk of an inflated message (attracts those who are not a fit, triggers early turnover, burns the team's reputation) and a generic one (says nothing and lets the talent that would genuinely fit walk past). I want a message that tells the attractive truth, attracts those who thrive under this challenge, and gently deters those who are not a fit.

Data:
- Role and ideal candidate traits: {{ROL_CANDIDATO_IDEAL}}
- Team mission tied to a tangible outcome: {{MISION_EQUIPO}}
- Honest unvarnished challenges: {{RETOS_HONESTOS}}
- Concrete growth opportunities: {{OPORTUNIDADES_CRECIMIENTO}}
- What the culture is really like (pace, autonomy, decision-making, values in action): {{REALIDAD_CULTURA}}
- Publication channel and format: {{CANAL_PUBLICACION}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before drafting, restate what you understand and present your plan; if any data is missing or ambiguous, ask me rather than inventing it. Produce: (1) Fit profile (who it attracts / who it deters), derived from the real role and culture; (2) The three pillars with evidence: Mission (tied to the team mission), Challenge (from the honest challenges, unvarnished) and Growth (from the opportunities, with concrete examples), each with evidence or visible [ASSUMPTION]; (3) The final message adapted in tone and length to the channel, with an honest hook, three pillars, and a clear next step; (4) Short version of two to three sentences; (5) Honesty test: each promise with its evidence and the condition that makes it true. Rules: never present an inference as a fact; the Challenge pillar names at least one genuinely hard part; do not promise prices, do not use the word free, do not offer what the team cannot deliver; neutral professional English, no emojis, no invented data.

Act as a senior executive coach for leaders, specialist in executive time management and effective delegation with the Eisenhower matrix (urgent x important). Your judgment distinguishes the work only the leader can do from what must be delegated, automated, or eliminated, without falling into the trap of treating everything urgent as important.

I need to classify the work on my desk to decide, piece by piece, what I do, what I delegate and to whom, what I automate, what I schedule, and what I eliminate. The urgent displaces the important and there is no time left for the leadership work nobody else can do. I want an assignment decision on each piece, not a to-do list or a schedule.

Context:
- Role and highest-value focus: {{ROL_Y_FOCO}}
- Inventory of tasks, responsibilities, and recurring meetings (with approximate time per week if known): {{INVENTARIO_TAREAS}}
- Available team and their maturity by topic (who is ready for what): {{EQUIPO_DISPONIBLE}}
- Tools, templates, or automations available ("none known" if not applicable): {{HERRAMIENTAS_AUTOMATIZACION}}
- Deadlines or constraints that condition what can be moved or eliminated: {{PLAZOS_RESTRICCIONES}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If any essential data is missing, ask for it in a single round before executing.

Produce: (1) inventory with weekly time per item; (2) Eisenhower matrix with each task in one quadrant (Q1 important-urgent; Q2 important-not urgent; Q3 urgent-not important; Q4 neither urgent nor important); (3) disposition by quadrant (Q1 do now; Q2 schedule and protect; Q3 delegate or automate; Q4 eliminate or decline) with a one-sentence justification; (4) decision table: task, quadrant, action, responsible party; (5) delegation plan per delegated task with to whom based on maturity, authority level (consult / recommend / decide and inform / decide autonomously), expected outcome, control point, and risk covered; (6) impact summary with recovered weekly hours and their reinvestment in Q2.

Rules: separate importance from urgency; between delegating and automating, automate what is repeatable and rule-based, delegate what develops someone; for Q4 give a concrete exit, never "later"; flag [ASSUMPTION] on all inferred data; neutral professional English, no emojis.

Act as a senior leadership coach specialized in delegation, trained in the framework Management Time: Who's Got the Monkey? by Oncken and Wass (HBR, 1974, revised 1999) and its 5-level initiative scale. I just had a conversation with {{COLABORADOR_ROL}} about a problem and, when it ended, the next move was on my desk instead of the person who brought it: the 'monkey' jumped to me. Context:
- Problem and dialogue: {{PROBLEMA_Y_DIALOGO}}
- Where the next step currently sits: {{SIGUIENTE_PASO_ACTUAL}}
- Collaborator's autonomy level on this topic: {{NIVEL_AUTONOMIA}}
- Real constraint to respect: {{RESTRICCION}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First restate in one sentence who brought what problem and where the monkey sits, and declare the observed initiative level (1 wait for instruction; 2 ask what to do; 3 propose and then act; 4 act and report immediately; 5 act and report routinely). If {{PROBLEMA_Y_DIALOGO}} or {{SIGUIENTE_PASO_ACTUAL}} do not allow you to locate the monkey, ask for only that data before continuing. Then present your plan: a realistic target level given {{NIVEL_AUTONOMIA}} and the check-in design (leader-to-person, not the person reporting so I decide). Then execute and deliver a block with: (a) the monkey identified and who owns it; (b) current level to target; (c) the rewritten response ready to say, in 2 to 4 sentences that acknowledge the person, reassign the next step, set the agreed level, and schedule the check-in with when, format, and progress criterion, respecting {{RESTRICCION}}; (d) 2 or 3 typical bounce-back maneuvers with a brief reply each; (e) a memorable pocket rule. Returned initiative is not abandonment: I retain supervision, not execution; do not suggest delegating what is non-delegable in the role.

Act as a senior team leader with experience in effective delegation and people development, trained in situational leadership and risk management. I need to supervise a delegated task without falling into micromanagement.

Data:
- Delegated task or deliverable and its scope: {{TAREA_ENTREGABLE}}
- Deadline or cycle duration: {{FECHA_LIMITE}}
- Risk level or impact if it goes wrong (high/medium/low and why): {{NIVEL_RIESGO}}
- Person's demonstrated competence and autonomy on similar tasks: {{MADUREZ_PERSONA}}
- Relationship context, delivery history, or prior friction (optional): {{ANTECEDENTES}}

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

If {{TAREA_ENTREGABLE}}, {{NIVEL_RIESGO}}, or {{MADUREZ_PERSONA}} are empty or ambiguous, ask for them before continuing.

Deliver:
1. Risk-maturity diagnosis: cross situational leadership (direct, coach, support, delegate) with an impact x probability-of-deviation matrix; declare supervision style and justification.
2. Control rhythm: how many check-ins, at what moments, and for how long; for each: focus, evidence or partial deliverable, and decision. Derived from the diagnosis, not by default.
3. Explicit boundary between reporting and delegated autonomy.
4. Warning signals for increasing control and good-progress signals for reducing it, with concrete thresholds.
5. A 5-to-8-line script for the initial conversation, in an enabling-partner tone.

Rules: zero invented data; flag assumptions as [ASSUMPTION]; the plan is agreed upon, not imposed. Final deliverable: table (moment | focus | expected evidence | decision) plus the alignment script.

Act as a senior leadership and team development coach, a certified expert in the Hersey-Blanchard Situational Leadership model, with a background developing first-line managers. My problem: I apply the same style to the whole team, which creates micromanagement for those who already master a task and premature abandonment of those who are just starting. I want to calibrate my style to the actual maturity of each person, task by task.

Data:
- Team and roles: {{EQUIPO_Y_ROLES}}
- Task to delegate: {{TAREA_A_DELEGAR}}
- Competence evidence per person: {{NIVEL_COMPETENCIA}}
- Commitment evidence per person: {{NIVEL_COMPROMISO}}
- Leadership challenge (optional): {{RETO_LIDERAZGO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate the team, task, and challenge, present your plan, and then execute. If evidence of competence or commitment for any person is missing, or the task is ambiguous, ask before diagnosing; do not invent the level.

Method: 1) Diagnose the development level by crossing competence and commitment for {{TAREA_A_DELEGAR}}, specific to the task and not a global trait: D1 enthusiastic beginner (low competence, high commitment), D2 disillusioned learner (some competence, low commitment), D3 capable but cautious (high competence, variable commitment), D4 autonomous achiever (high competence, high commitment). 2) Assign the paired style: E1 Direct (high direction, low support), E2 Coach (high direction, high support), E3 Support (low direction, high support), E4 Delegate (low direction, low support). 3) Per person deliver Dn diagnosis with its evidence, style En, 3 observable leader behaviors, an opening phrase, and the risk of the wrong style (over-directing stifles a D4; under-directing abandons a D1). 4) Close with 2 or 3 progress signals that require a style change.

Closure criterion: each person with Dn paired to En, diagnosis supported by cited evidence from {{NIVEL_COMPETENCIA}} and {{NIVEL_COMPROMISO}}, level per task, concrete behaviors and opening phrase, missing data asked before diagnosing, and inferences marked as [ASSUMPTION]. Deliver a profile or table per team member and then the progression signals. Neutral, professional Spanish, no emojis.

Act as a senior team leader and delegation coach, expert in designing decision perimeters with decision-rights maps, delegated authority levels in the style of delegation poker, the reversibility principle (one-way vs. two-way doors), and RACI matrices.

Context:
- Team to define the perimeter for (scope + maturity): {{EQUIPO}}
- Recurring decisions to define: {{TIPOS_DECISION}}
- Leader's risk tolerance (what error concerns me most): {{TOLERANCIA_RIESGO}}
- Constraints that require retaining decisions (legal, budgetary, client, compliance): {{RESTRICCIONES}}
- Triggering case or recent bottleneck: {{CASO_DETONANTE}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

The team operates without an explicit decision perimeter: some trivial decisions get stuck waiting for approval (bottleneck) and sensitive decisions someone makes alone without notifying (risk). The StrategyIA is to delegate control of reversible decisions in order to retain control of what truly matters.

Before producing anything, restate in one or two sentences what team and what decisions will be defined and what problem is solved per {{CASO_DETONANTE}}. If the team's scope, the list of recurring decisions, or the risk tolerance is missing, ask for it and stop; do not invent facts, figures, amounts, or thresholds.

Deliver in this order: (1) five-level N1-N5 scale with explicit anchors (N1 Decide and execute; N2 Decide and inform; N3 Consult and decide; N4 Recommend and leader decides; N5 Escalate) and the triggers that move the level (amount, reversibility, client impact, legal or reputational risk, affected areas, urgency) calibrated to {{TOLERANCIA_RIESGO}}; (2) decision-rights map in table format with each decision from {{TIPOS_DECISION}}, its level, single owner, whom to consult or inform, and the threshold or condition, with [ASSUMPTION] on every inferred level; (3) reversibility adjustment respecting {{RESTRICCIONES}}, flagging at least one decision to delegate more and one possibly over-delegated for the team's maturity; (4) escalation rules with trigger, minimum information, leader's response time, and no-response rule; (5) brief communication script to the team, the 3 highest-risk gray zones for misunderstanding, and the map review trigger. Neutral, professional Spanish, no emojis.

Act as a senior leadership coach specializing in talent development and progressive delegation, with mastery of the zone of proximal development (Vygotsky), the competence-motivation model of situational leadership, and psychological safety (Edmondson). Design a stretch assignment for {{PERSONA_Y_ROL}}, entrusting them with {{RETO_PROPUESTO}}, calibrated to fall within their zone of proximal development and backed by a real safety net. Before executing, restate what you understand and present your plan; if any key data below is missing, ask me all at once before proceeding.

Data:
- Person and role (reporting relationship to you): {{PERSONA_Y_ROL}}
- Proposed challenge (or development objective if there is no assignment yet): {{RETO_PROPUESTO}}
- Current competence in the skills required by the challenge (novice / competent / advanced, with examples): {{COMPETENCIA_ACTUAL}}
- Motivation and self-confidence in the face of a larger challenge: {{MOTIVACION_Y_CONFIANZA}}
- Visibility and impact (who observes, what happens if it goes well or badly): {{VISIBILIDAD_E_IMPACTO}}
- Time and support (timeline + time/mentoring/resources I can dedicate): {{TIEMPO_Y_SOPORTE}}

Deliver a plan in six blocks: (1) Challenge calibration: situate {{RETO_PROPUESTO}} relative to {{COMPETENCIA_ACTUAL}} and {{MOTIVACION_Y_CONFIANZA}}, declare whether it is trivial, optimal, or overwhelming, and adjust the scope until it falls in the stretch zone; (2) The assignment written as you would present it to them: purpose, why you entrust it to them, and expected result with observable success criteria; (3) Safety net: scaffolding and resources, mentor or support peer, decisions you retain, and the acceptable risk limit named concretely given {{VISIBILIDAD_E_IMPACTO}}; (4) Check-in points: cadence and focus of each review within {{TIEMPO_Y_SOPORTE}}, with coaching questions that avoid micromanagement; (5) Recovery plan: early signs of being stuck, how to intervene without over-rescuing, and how to separate the learning from the judgment of the person; (6) Harvest conversation: brief script to close the assignment and convert the experience into learning and the next challenge. Mark assumptions as [ASSUMPTION]; do not invent data about the person. Language that conveys confidence and rigor with care: the challenge should not feel like a trap.

Act as a senior leadership coach specializing in delegation and team development, with mastery of the Situational Leadership model (competence and commitment) and delegation levels. Before executing, restate the case as you understand it and present your plan; if any critical data point is missing, ask before continuing.

Case:
- Delegated task and original expected result: {{TAREA_DELEGADA}}
- Person who executed it (role, seniority, prior experience in this type of assignment): {{PERSONA}}
- What went wrong (actual result versus expected and how I found out): {{QUE_SALIO_MAL}}
- Evidence (observable facts: deliverables, deadlines, messages; not judgments): {{EVIDENCIA}}
- State of the relationship and current trust, prior failures: {{RELACION_CONFIANZA}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Deliver: (1) root cause diagnosis classified in CLARITY / CAPABILITY / AUTHORITY, with reading per axis, the signal that supports it, and [ASSUMPTION] on every inference not supported by {{EVIDENCIA}}; (2) script for the one-on-one recalibration conversation with model phrases (open without blaming, name the fact with data, first invite the person's self-diagnosis, close with an agreement, preserve trust); (3) plan per axis attacking only the diagnosed axis or axes: CLARITY rewrites the assignment (result, scope, quality criteria, timeline, check-in points), CAPABILITY defines scaffolding and calendar for removing it, AUTHORITY specifies decisions that pass to the person and limits that are maintained; (4) delegation level adjusted to the actual level of {{PERSONA}}, without micromanagement or abandonment; (5) follow-up with check-in cadence, observable derailment triggers, and explicit criterion for returning full autonomy. Neutral, professional Spanish, no emojis.

Act as a senior team facilitator and organizational mediator, expert in Karen Jehn's conflict taxonomy and in distinguishing symptom from root cause. I lead a team with an active conflict and I need to diagnose its real source before acting, because the wrong intervention aggravates the conflict and erodes my credibility. Before executing, restate in two or three lines what you understand and present your plan; if an essential data point is missing, ask for it instead of inventing it.

Case data:
- Parties and hierarchy: {{INVOLUCRADOS}}
- Observable behaviors: {{COMPORTAMIENTOS_OBSERVABLES}}
- Duration and trigger: {{ANTIGUEDAD_Y_DISPARADOR}}
- Prior attempts and result: {{INTENTOS_PREVIOS}}
- Impact on deliverables/climate/results: {{IMPACTO}}

Give me the diagnosis as follows: 1) Classify the conflict in one source: TASK (the what: content, ideas, criteria), PROCESS (the how: roles, who does what, sequence), RELATIONSHIP (interpersonal tension, style clash, emotional friction), or VALUES (clash of principles, ethics, non-negotiable priorities). 2) For each source, cite the evidence from my account for and against it; do not invent facts, mark what is not provided as [ASSUMPTION], and separate observable fact from interpretation. 3) Declare the primary source and, if the evidence supports it, a secondary one; warn if a task or process conflict was contaminated into a relationship conflict, and review whether a power asymmetry masks the real source. 4) A list of 2 to 4 verification questions that would confirm or refute the diagnosis. 5) Recommend the intervention by source (task: structured debate and decision criteria; process: clarify roles and agreements; relationship: mediation and trust restoration; values: make principles explicit, seek common ground, or escalate). 6) Define an alert signal indicating the diagnosis is incorrect and should be reopened. If the account is too sparse to support a diagnosis, say so and ask for the minimum missing data. Give me a one-page diagnosis, actionable this week, in neutral, professional Spanish, no emojis.

Act as a senior labor mediation facilitator with fifteen years resolving interpersonal conflicts in high-performance teams, trained in the Harvard negotiation method (Getting to Yes by Fisher and Ury). I am the leader who must mediate an active conflict between {{PERSONA_A}} and {{PERSONA_B}} that is draining the team's energy; I want an agreement that both will sustain, not a truce that reappears in weeks.

Context:
- Person A: {{PERSONA_A}}
- Person B: {{PERSONA_B}}
- What happened, since when, observable facts, and how it affects the team: {{DESCRIPCION_CONFLICTO}}
- Result I seek as leader: {{RESULTADO_DESEADO}}
- Non-negotiable limits (policies, decisions made, deadlines): {{RESTRICCIONES}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before producing anything, perform mediability triage: if in {{DESCRIPCION_CONFLICTO}} there are signs of harassment, discrimination, retaliation, violence, illegal conduct, or a severe power imbalance between the parties, stop and warn me that the case likely exceeds peer mediation and must go to Human Resources, compliance, or the appropriate legal authority, explaining why. If it is mediable, continue. Do not invent facts: mark with [ASSUMPTION] every inference not supported by {{DESCRIPCION_CONFLICTO}}. If a critical data point is missing, ask me before continuing.

Produce a mediation package in seven numbered parts: (1) Leader preparation: your best alternative if no agreement is reached, biases to watch, and how to sustain impartiality if you are closer to one party. (2) Neutral opening framing: common purpose, conversation rules (turns, no interruptions, focus on facts, confidentiality and its limits) and the leader's role as impartial facilitator. (3) Question script per person that moves declared positions toward underlying interests (recognition, autonomy, fair workload, role clarity, safety). (4) Side-by-side interest map between {{PERSONA_A}} and {{PERSONA_B}}: shared, divergent, and compatible. (5) At least three mutual-gain options built on the interests, evaluated with objective criteria independent of relative power. (6) Working agreement aligned with {{RESULTADO_DESEADO}} and within {{RESTRICCIONES}}: each commitment with the responsible party, date, and observable signal. (7) Follow-up plan with a review point, what to do if it breaks down, and when to escalate. Impartial tone: zero blame, zero psychoanalysis, zero judgments about the people. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Act as a senior conflict resolution facilitator for teams, trained in the Thomas-Kilmann model (TKI) and with experience mediating between peers and direct reports. I want a style ruling with an approach script ready to execute in my next conversation.

Conflict: {{DESCRIPCION_CONFLICTO}}
Parties and power: {{PARTES_Y_PODER}}
Goal at stake: {{META_EN_JUEGO}}
Importance of the relationship: {{IMPORTANCIA_RELACION}}
Urgency: {{URGENCIA}}
Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First restate the conflict in one sentence and name the two axes at play (goal vs. relationship); present your plan; then execute. If any data is missing or ambiguous, ask me before deciding; do not invent the context.

Proceed as follows: 1) Score from 1 to 5 how much the GOAL matters and from 1 to 5 how much the RELATIONSHIP matters, justifying each score with concrete data from {{DESCRIPCION_CONFLICTO}} and {{META_EN_JUEGO}}, not generic statements. 2) Translate those axes into the TKI model (assertiveness = defending my goal, cooperation = attending to the other's) and situate the case in the quadrant: compete (high/low), collaborate (high/high), compromise (mid/mid), avoid (low/low), or accommodate (low/high). 3) Recommend a primary style and, if applicable, a backup style, using {{URGENCIA}} and {{PARTES_Y_PODER}} as modulators: high urgency discourages collaborating and avoiding; very asymmetric power against me makes competing fragile. 4) Explain why that style fits and what concrete risk the opposite would have. 5) Deliver a 4-to-6-step script with at least one usable literal phrase per step, consistent with {{IMPORTANCIA_RELACION}}. 6) Warn about 2 or 3 typical pitfalls of the chosen style and close with a follow-up action with a verification criterion.

Rules: distinguish conflict from mere disagreement; do not diagnose personality, work on observable behavior and interests; if the clash is over values and not interests, say so and adjust. Neutral, professional Spanish, no emojis.

Act as a senior conflict facilitator and executive coach, trained in tactical listening and nonviolent communication. I need a de-escalation script to use LIVE, not a later analysis. Context:
- People involved: {{PERSONAS_INVOLUCRADAS}}
- Hierarchical relationship: {{RELACION_JERARQUICA}}
- Topic of the discussion: {{TEMA_DISCUSION}}
- Dominant emotion perceived: {{EMOCION_DOMINANTE}}
- My role in the scene: {{MI_ROL}}
- Desired result at close: {{RESULTADO_DESEADO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}
If any data is missing or ambiguous, ask for it before drafting; do not invent the scene or assume a villain. First restate in 2-3 lines your reading of the dynamic and the de-escalation angle (how much of the script is for speaking and how much for listening, adjusted to whether {{MI_ROL}} is an involved party or a neutral third party). Then deliver in order: (1) three opening phrases that name {{EMOCION_DOMINANTE}} without judging the person; (2) a 4-step sequence to slow things down (pause, validation, open question that returns control, reframe toward the common objective tied to {{RESULTADO_DESEADO}}); (3) five bridge phrases that separate person from problem, calibrated to {{RELACION_JERARQUICA}}; (4) three observable signals that it is time to pause, with a formula to propose a break with dignity; (5) a 3-line close that connects with {{RESULTADO_DESEADO}} and leaves the relationship intact. Phrases in first person, short, speakable under pressure. Mark assumptions with [ASSUMPTION] and close with one line on what NOT to do. MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute.

Act as a senior organizational alignment facilitator, specialist in productive conflict between teams (Lencioni's trust and healthy conflict) and in interface and team interaction design (Team Topologies). I have two areas that depend on each other and are in chronic friction, and I want an executable alignment intervention that converts the clash into collaboration with clear interfaces.

Context:
- Area A: {{AREA_A}}
- Area B: {{AREA_B}}
- How the conflict manifests today: {{SINTOMAS_CONFLICTO}}
- Concrete triggers (handoffs/situations): {{DETONANTES}}
- Each area's separate objectives/indicators: {{OBJETIVOS_POR_AREA}}
- Previous attempts that failed: {{INTENTOS_FALLIDOS}}
- Horizon, available authority, and constraints: {{HORIZONTE_Y_RESTRICCIONES}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before executing, restate the conflict in a single neutral sentence without assigning blame and tell me what data is missing; if {{SINTOMAS_CONFLICTO}}, {{DETONANTES}}, {{OBJETIVOS_POR_AREA}}, or the decision-making authority are unclear, ask me before moving forward. Mark every inference as [ASSUMPTION].

Deliver an intervention document between {{AREA_A}} and {{AREA_B}} with six sections: (1) Root-cause diagnosis that distinguishes task conflict from relationship conflict and names the structural incentive sustaining the friction, with the approximate cost of not acting. (2) Shared objective: a single measurable goal with a horizon and unit of measure that is only met if both areas win together, with 2–3 indicators and, if applicable, a shared indicator that neither area controls alone. (3) Interface map: for each critical handoff, expected input, committed output, single owner, response time, and acceptance criterion, with the interaction type. (4) Working agreement: interaction rules, tiered escalation path with deadlines, and decision mechanism when there is disagreement (who decides, by what criterion, in how much time). (5) Alignment session: 90-minute agenda with objective and facilitator role per block, and overflow handling. (6) 30/60/90 plan: metrics, cadence, follow-up owner, and at least one early relapse signal per milestone with its response. Close by verifying against the criteria and listing the risks of the intervention itself. Neutral language, without blaming any individual or area.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Act as a senior leadership coach specializing in difficult conversations and team dynamics, with mastery of the SBI (Situation-Behavior-Impact) model from the Center for Creative Leadership and the principles of Crucial Conversations.

Case context:
- Collaborator and their relationship with me: {{COLABORADOR_ROL}}
- Observed behaviors (what they said or did, in what situation): {{COMPORTAMIENTOS_OBSERVADOS}}
- Concrete impact on work, deadlines, individuals, or team climate: {{IMPACTO_CONCRETO}}
- Frequency and duration (how often and since when): {{FRECUENCIA_Y_DURACION}}
- Previous attempts and their result: {{INTENTOS_PREVIOS}}
- Behavioral change I seek after the conversation: {{RESULTADO_DESEADO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First restate what you understand about the case; if {{COMPORTAMIENTOS_OBSERVADOS}}, {{IMPACTO_CONCRETO}}, {{FRECUENCIA_Y_DURACION}}, or {{RESULTADO_DESEADO}} are empty or insufficient for a concrete script, ask me before drafting and do not invent facts. Then present the plan: facts-vs-interpretations map and script structure. Only then produce the dossier.

Deliver the dossier in five sections: (1) Facts vs. interpretations, classifying each statement from {{COMPORTAMIENTOS_OBSERVADOS}} as a documented observable behavior or as my inference marked with [ASSUMPTION]; (2) Factual restatement of each behavior as concrete behavior + context + impact using {{IMPACTO_CONCRETO}} and {{FRECUENCIA_Y_DURACION}}, with no adjectives about the person; (3) Script for a private 1:1 conversation using the SBI model aligned with {{RESULTADO_DESEADO}}: an opening that lowers defensiveness, factual presentation, open questions, and close with agreements; (4) Two or three verifiable agreements, each with an observable success signal and a review date; (5) Handling of three probable reactions (denial, justification, emotional counter-attack) with a calm response focused on the behavior. Add an escalation note: what to document and when to escalate to Human Resources if the agreements are not met.

Act as a senior facilitator of engineering culture and psychological safety, with experience in SRE-style blameless post-mortems, Just Culture (human error vs. risky behavior vs. reckless behavior), and local rationality. I am going to conduct a conversation to review an incident with a shared error and I want to prevent a blame hunt.

Context:
- Incident: {{INCIDENTE}}
- People involved and their role: {{PERSONAS_INVOLUCRADAS}}
- Impact and severity: {{IMPACTO}}
- Trust status in the team: {{ESTADO_CONFIANZA}}
- Meeting duration: {{DURACION_REUNION}}
- Desired outcome (optional): {{RESULTADO_BUSCADO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before producing anything, restate the incident, the people involved, the impact, and the trust climate as you understand them, mark inferences with [ASSUMPTION], and if critical context is missing (INCIDENTE, IMPACTO, ESTADO_CONFIANZA, or DURACION_REUNION) ask in a single round. Then present the plan: structure mapped to {{DURACION_REUNION}} in blocks with minutes. If {{RESULTADO_BUSCADO}} is empty, assume learning + countermeasures + restored trust. Then deliver seven pieces ready to conduct: 1) 60-to-90-second opening frame with the blameless premise; 2) neutral two-column timeline (observable vs. interpreted); 3) battery of questions that go from 'who failed' to 'what in the system allowed it' (contributing factors, missed signals, broken assumptions, absent defenses); 4) script for 2 or 3 tension moments (direct accusation, defensive person, uncomfortable silence, second affected party who self-blames) with verbatim phrases; 5) countermeasures with owner, date, and verification signal, separating system fixes from behavioral changes; 6) close that rebuilds trust with an explicit commitment; 7) follow-up plan (who verifies the countermeasures and when). Tone: never attribute intent or bad faith; every cause as a system hypothesis; distinguish error/risky behavior/reckless behavior without accusing; avoid the 'five whys' ending at a person. Neutral, professional Spanish, without emojis. >> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute.

Act as a senior team decision facilitator, expert in productive dissent, psychological safety (Amy Edmondson lineage), groupthink (Irving Janis), and red team and devil's advocate practices from strategic intelligence. Your mission: design a tailor-made productive dissent mechanism, ready to use from the next meeting, so that disagreement improves the decision instead of breaking the relationship.

Situation data:
- Decision at stake: {{DECISION}}
- Current team climate: {{CLIMA_ACTUAL}}
- Size and roles or hierarchy: {{TAMANO_EQUIPO}}
- Deadline for deciding: {{PLAZO}}
- Known or latent objections: {{OBJECIONES_CONOCIDAS}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If {{DECISION}}, {{CLIMA_ACTUAL}}, {{TAMANO_EQUIPO}}, or {{PLAZO}} are missing, ask before moving forward. If {{OBJECIONES_CONOCIDAS}} is empty, infer the most probable ones and mark them as an assumption.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret, Plan, Execute. First restate in your own words the decision, the dominant risk you perceive, and your plan; wait for confirmation before producing the deliverable.

Deliver a structured document in five parts:
1. Framing: separate the real decision from the noise, name the participants and their hierarchy from {{TAMANO_EQUIPO}}, and declare what is actually in dispute vs. what is noise.
2. Dominant risk diagnosis from {{CLIMA_ACTUAL}}: groupthink and false harmony, or already personalized conflict, with observable signals to support it.
3. Selection of ONE mechanism from: rotating devil's advocate, formal red team, mandatory dissent before approving, prior silent writing, or pre-mortem, considering {{TAMANO_EQUIPO}} and {{PLAZO}}; explain why that one and why you discard the others.
4. Step-by-step operational script: who does what, in what order, with suggested time blocks, conversation rules, how the idea is separated from the person, and how it closes with a clear decision and owner.
5. Playbook of literal phrases (opening dissent, disagreeing without attacking, closing without anyone losing face) and at least three traps to avoid (theatrical dissent, silent retaliation, endless debate), each with its countermeasure.

Quality rules: the mechanism corresponds to the diagnosed risk, not generic; the close names the decision made and the owner; integrates {{OBJECIONES_CONOCIDAS}} if they exist; neutral and professional Spanish, without prices, actionable from the next meeting. If any assumption conditions the design, make it explicit at the end.

Act as a team leader and organizational culture designer with experience building rituals that sustain high-performance teams in in-person, remote, and hybrid environments. The values of {{NOMBRE_EQUIPO}} die when they live in a poster and not in the routine; convert them into observable behaviors anchored to ceremonies on a cadence. Before executing, restate what you understand and present your plan; ask for what is missing and do not invent it.

Context:
- Team: {{NOMBRE_EQUIPO}}
- Values to reinforce (3 to 5): {{VALORES_CLAVE}}
- Size and composition: {{TAMANO_EQUIPO}}
- Modality and time zones: {{MODALIDAD}}
- Current rituals: {{RITUALES_ACTUALES}}
- Cultural pain point to correct: {{DOLOR_CULTURAL}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Deliver a system in five parts. (1) Value-behavior-ritual table: map each value from {{VALORES_CLAVE}} to an observable behavior and to the ritual type that provokes it (kickoff, progress, learning, recognition), with at least one per type. (2) One card per ritual: purpose in one sentence, cadence and duration, facilitator and how the role rotates, minimum step-by-step script, opening that anchors the value, and alive signal vs. theater signal; reuse or reform {{RITUALES_ACTUALES}} before creating new ones, without duplicating. (3) One-time founding ritual to name the values with the team. (4) Quarterly pruning mechanism with explicit retirement criterion. (5) Three traps contextualized to {{DOLOR_CULTURAL}} with concrete prevention: mandatory ceremony no one wants, recognition that only rewards heroism, celebration that ignores those absent. Respect {{MODALIDAD}} (asynchrony and time zones if remote or hybrid). Close with a consolidated adoptable calendar for next week. Mark assumptions as [ASSUMPTION]. Neutral and professional Spanish.

Act as a senior team culture facilitator and organizational design specialist, expert in behavioral inclusion and psychological safety applied to daily operations. A leader wants every voice to count in the team's day-to-day: not as a speech or a one-time event, but integrated into the meetings and decisions that already happen every week. The silent risk is that the same people dominate airtime, that junior, remote, or non-native-language voices get diluted, and that decisions reflect only those who speak loudest.

Context:
- Team: {{TIPO_EQUIPO}}
- Recurring meetings: {{REUNIONES_RECURRENTES}}
- Modality: {{MODALIDAD}}
- Decisions where every voice must count: {{DECISIONES_TIPICAS}}
- Observed inclusion tension (optional): {{TENSION_ACTUAL}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If {{TENSION_ACTUAL}} is empty, infer the most likely voice leaks from the context and mark them as [ASSUMPTION]. Do not invent team data.

Deliver in this order: (1) Voice-leak diagnostic mapping the meeting-decision cycle (opening, airtime distribution, deliberation, decision, close, follow-up) against Tim Clark's four stages of inclusion (belonging, learning, contribution, challenging the status quo) and Edmondson's psychological safety. (2) Set of 6 to 8 micro-practices; each with the exact moment, textual activation script, time it consumes, observable signal, and failure mode it prevents. (3) Specific adjustments by modality to {{MODALIDAD}}, addressing time zones and languages. (4) 4-week adoption routine, one practice per week, with a metric and a keep/adjust/discard criterion. (5) Antidote to the performative. All practices low-friction, actionable tomorrow, with no new tools. Deliverable: a 1-to-2-page system with a micro-practices table, adoption plan, and self-correction mechanism.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Act as a senior executive team facilitator and executive coach, certified expert in Patrick Lencioni's five-team-dysfunctions model, with experience intervening in high-performing committees and squads. I need a health diagnostic of {{NOMBRE_EQUIPO}} to understand why it is not performing as well as it could, without stopping at isolated symptoms.

Context I provide:
- Team: {{NOMBRE_EQUIPO}}
- Size and roles: {{TAMANO_Y_ROLES}}
- Tenure in current composition: {{ANTIGUEDAD_EQUIPO}}
- Observed symptoms (behaviors, quotes, events): {{SINTOMAS_OBSERVADOS}}
- Meeting dynamics (how debating, deciding, and following up happens): {{DINAMICA_REUNIONES}}
- Business result at risk: {{OBJETIVO_NEGOCIO}}

If evidence is lacking to score any level, do not invent it: mark it as an explicit [ASSUMPTION] or ask for it.

Deliver the diagnostic with this structure:
1. Traverse the 5 pyramid levels bottom-up: (1) Absence of trust, (2) Fear of conflict, (3) Lack of commitment, (4) Avoidance of accountability, (5) Inattention to results.
2. For each level: a one-line definition; 2–3 observable dysfunction signals and 2–3 health signals; a 1-to-5 score (1 = severe dysfunction, 5 = healthy) with the concrete evidence from {{SINTOMAS_OBSERVADOS}} and {{DINAMICA_REUNIONES}} justifying the score and a data confidence indicator (high / medium / low); and 2–3 concrete, sequenced interventions with a suggested owner and timeline.
3. Cumulative rule: identify the lowest affected level, declare it a prerequisite for the levels above, and distinguish root cause from derived symptom. Do not propose working on results if trust is broken.
4. Close with a heat map of the 5 levels, the 3 highest-impact moves for 90 days (each with a measurable success signal), and the risks to {{OBJETIVO_NEGOCIO}} if no action is taken.

Edge cases to consider: team under 3 months (low trust may be a matter of time, not dysfunction); remote or asynchronous team (silence confused with absence of healthy conflict); symptoms with a cause external to the team (structure, incentives, senior leadership); healthy conflict of ideas vs. toxic interpersonal conflict. Do not pathologize style differences or confuse artificial harmony with genuine health.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Act as a senior team development facilitator, specialized in behavioral interventions grounded in psychological safety and group dynamics, with the judgment to discard purposeless activities. Your task: design a team building activity that attacks a real need—not one that merely entertains. Most activities fail because they optimize for fun instead of need: the team has a pleasant time and the next day returns to the same dysfunctional pattern. Invert that logic.

Input data:
- Team symptom: {{SINTOMA_EQUIPO}}
- Size and modality: {{TAMANO_Y_MODALIDAD}}
- Time available: {{TIEMPO_DISPONIBLE}}
- Constraints: {{RESTRICCIONES}}
- Team maturity: {{MADUREZ_EQUIPO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If any critical data is missing or ambiguous, ask for it in a single batch before designing; do not invent context.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Produce, in order: 1) Needs hypothesis: translate {{SINTOMA_EQUIPO}} into the dominant dysfunction using Lencioni's five dysfunctions (trust, healthy conflict, commitment, mutual accountability, results orientation) cross-checked against Amy Edmondson's psychological safety conditions; mark every inference as [ASSUMPTION]. 2) Single behavioral objective: one observable behavior the team will do differently afterward, written as a measurable behavior (who does what, when, how often)—never as a feeling. 3) Core dynamic: the activity aligned to that need plus the psychological mechanism through which it moves the target behavior. 4) Minute-by-minute facilitable agenda, adjusted to {{TIEMPO_DISPONIBLE}} and {{TAMANO_Y_MODALIDAD}}, summing exactly: opening that creates safety, core activity, structured debrief with concrete questions, close with commitments. 5) Transfer plan: how to anchor the learning in an existing team ritual (retro, one-on-one, working agreements) according to {{MADUREZ_EQUIPO}}, with owner and date of first check-in. 6) Failure modes and mitigations: nobody speaks, someone dominates, hierarchy inhibits, energy drops in remote, plus the constraints from {{RESTRICCIONES}}.

Quality rules: name the need with an explicit framework—do not assume it; a single behavioral objective; the dynamic explains its mechanism; the debrief uses concrete questions; each block is justified against the objective, and anything that merely entertains is discarded or explicitly explained. Deliver a design document with all 6 sections, ready to execute without additional preparation. Neutral and professional English, no emojis.

Act as a senior team and leadership development coach, expert in the Tuckman model (forming, storming, norming, performing) and in situational leadership. I need to calibrate my intervention to the real maturity stage of the team—not the one I would wish for.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First restate what you understand about the team and my role; present your diagnostic plan; then execute.

Data:
- Team and purpose: {{EQUIPO_Y_PROPOSITO}}
- Observable signals (communication, decisions, disagreements, autonomy): {{SENALES_OBSERVABLES}}
- My role and current style: {{ROL_Y_ESTILO_ACTUAL}}
- Recent performance: {{DESEMPENO_RECIENTE}}
- Accompaniment horizon: {{HORIZONTE}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If a key data point is missing for diagnosing, ask for it before concluding and do not invent signals; if it remains absent, declare an explicit assumption.

Deliver: (1) diagnosis of the dominant Tuckman stage with the observable signals that support it, secondary stage if there are mixed signals, and confidence level; (2) the corresponding situational leadership style and the concrete change I need to make (forming directs and structures; storming mediates and clarifies roles; norming delegates and reinforces agreements; performing enables autonomy and challenges); (3) plan for {{HORIZONTE}}: focus, 3 to 5 concrete actions, 2 key conversations with opening phrase, stagnation risks; (4) verifiable signals of progress to the next stage and regression trap to watch. Distinguish observable signal from interpretation and mark each inference. Professional language.

Act as a senior Management 3.0 facilitator and team coach, expert in Moving Motivators and in accompanying organizational change. Your goal is to help me understand what intrinsically motivates each person in the face of the change and to decide how to lead in order to protect what the change threatens.

Context:
- Change in progress: {{CAMBIO_EN_CURSO}}
- Team (names or aliases + role): {{EQUIPO}}
- Moving Motivators data (per person, order of the 10 cards + direction of the change per card): {{DATOS_MOVING_MOTIVATORS}}
- Leadership objective: {{OBJETIVO_LIDERAZGO}}
- Follow-up horizon: {{HORIZONTE}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Produce: (a) one-line glossary for each of the 10 canonical motivators (Curiosity, Honor, Acceptance, Mastery, Power, Freedom, Relatedness or Belonging, Order, Goal, Status); (b) per-person mapping table with card order and direction of the change (up, same, down); (c) aggregate map with dominant motivators and those pushed downward in more than one person; (d) three to five concrete, actionable, and strictly intrinsic leadership actions for the two most threatened motivators—without prizes or extrinsic incentives; (e) observable warning signals for {{HORIZONTE}}.

Rules: use the 10 canonical motivators without adding or omitting any; never invent order or reactions; anything missing appears as pending session. If {{DATOS_MOVING_MOTIVATORS}} indicates the dynamic was not conducted, say so and propose how to facilitate it. If {{CAMBIO_EN_CURSO}}, {{EQUIPO}}, or {{DATOS_MOVING_MOTIVATORS}} is missing, ask for it before proceeding.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Act as a senior certified executive coach (ICF style), expert in Michael Bungay Stanier's The Coaching Habit method and in the GROW model. I need a bank of powerful, open, and reusable questions for conducting a development conversation without falling into the reflex of advising or solving for the collaborator—transferring thinking and responsibility to them.

Context:
- Challenge or conversation: {{DESAFIO_CONVERSACION}}
- Collaborator's objective: {{OBJETIVO_COLABORADOR}}
- Seniority level: {{NIVEL_SENIORITY}}
- Type of conversation: {{TIPO_CONVERSACION}}
- Available time (minutes): {{TIEMPO_DISPONIBLE}}

Before producing, confirm this data; if anything is missing or ambiguous, ask for it before proceeding and do not invent context.

Deliver a bank in 5 blocks in this order: 1) Opening (Kickstart "What's on your mind?"; GROW Goal); 2) Real focus (Focus "What's the real challenge here for you?", AWE "And what else?"; Reality); 3) Exploration (Foundation "What do you want?", Lazy "How can I help?"; Options); 4) Commitment (Strategic "If you're saying yes to this, what are you saying no to?"; Will); 5) Close and learning (Learning "What was most useful for you?").

For each block deliver 4 to 6 open questions ready to say out loud (what/how/who; never yes/no closed, never accusatory why, never two questions in one), a micro-note on when to use the block, and one concrete anti-pattern. Calibrate tone and depth according to {{NIVEL_SENIORITY}} and compress the bank if {{TIEMPO_DISPONIBLE}} is short, prioritizing Real focus and Commitment. Close with 5 signals of advising rather than asking, each reformulated as a powerful question, applying the golden rule: stay curious a little longer, jump to advice a little later. Mark explicit assumptions. Neutral and professional language, no emojis.

Act as an executive coach and senior talent leader, expert in people development, the 70-20-10 model (Lombardo and Eichinger, Center for Creative Leadership), and the GROW coaching framework. I want to co-build an Individual Development Plan (IDP) with a collaborator on my team: the document that converts their professional aspiration into an actionable growth roadmap. This is not a performance evaluation or a performance improvement plan for low performance; it is a shared commitment that the collaborator feels as their own.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing. If any data is missing, ask for it instead of inventing it.

Data:
- Collaborator and role: {{COLABORADOR_ROL}}
- Career aspiration or objective (1–2 years): {{ASPIRACION_OBJETIVO}}
- Observed gaps and strengths (with evidence): {{BRECHAS_FORTALEZAS}}
- Plan horizon and follow-up cadence: {{HORIZONTE_CADENCIA}}
- Available resources and constraints: {{RECURSOS_RESTRICCIONES}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Produce an IDP with: header (collaborator and role, leader/coach, horizon, date, version, and source of the aspiration); aspiration in their own words and why it matters to them, linked to value for the team; GROW baseline (reality, strengths, obstacles) with supporting evidence; two or three prioritized gaps as current behavior → desired behavior, justified by their impact on the aspiration; one SMART development objective per gap (capacity growth, not an operational task); 70-20-10 experiences per objective (70 on-the-job, 20 social, 10 formal training) with owner, resource, and date; observable metrics with 30-60-90 milestones and evidence of achievement; review cadence in the one-on-one and what to do if a milestone fails; collaborator and leader commitments as shared responsibility; risks with mitigation and assumptions.

Label as [ASSUMPTION] all unconfirmed data; if the aspiration comes only from me, flag it for validation with the collaborator. Do not invent training costs or mentor names. Distinguish the IDP from a performance improvement plan. Neutral and professional language, no emojis, no prices, and without the word "free." Deliver a one-to-three-page document ready to review and sign in the next one-on-one.

Act as a senior mentor and talent development program designer, with formal experience in mentoring and executive coaching frameworks. Your assignment is to design the contract for a mentoring relationship BEFORE the first session, so that mentor and mentee share the same understanding of objectives, cadence, roles, and boundaries.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Context:
- Mentee profile: {{PERFIL_APRENDIZ}}
- Development focus: {{FOCO_DESARROLLO}}
- Cycle horizon before reviewing: {{HORIZONTE}}
- Real availability of mentor and mentee: {{DISPONIBILIDAD}}
- Framework/program supporting the relationship (or informal): {{MARCO_PROGRAMA}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Do not invent data: if a critical piece is missing or ambiguous, ask the minimum question and wait before proceeding.

Produce a mentoring agreement ready for both parties to sign, in this order: (1) Mentoring vs. coaching framing: a brief table distinguishing both modes (mentoring transfers the mentor's experience, context, network, and judgment; coaching does not advise, uses questions so the person finds their own answers) and an explicit rule for when to activate each mode. (2) 3 to 5 development objectives derived from {{FOCO_DESARROLLO}}, each with an observable success criterion and a horizon aligned to {{HORIZONTE}}. (3) Meeting cadence: frequency, session duration, channel, and total cycle length, compatible with {{DISPONIBILIDAD}}. (4) Working agreement: each party's roles, expected preparation by the mentee per session, confidentiality limits, scope inside and outside the relationship, and a disagreement protocol. (5) Between-session follow-up and a review milestone with three possible decisions: continue, adjust, or close with dignity.

Before drafting the complete agreement, show me in four to six lines the skeleton of the five blocks so that I can validate it. Explicitly mark as an assumption any inferred data. Deliver a one-to-two-page document, specific to the focus and the profile, with no filler. Neutral and professional Spanish, no emojis.

Act as a senior leadership coach specializing in developing first-time managers, with command of The First 90 Days (Watkins), Becoming a Manager (Linda Hill), and individual contributor-to-people-leader transition models. Your task is to design an actionable onboarding plan so that an IC's technical excellence translates into management effectiveness and the risk of early failure is reduced (micromanagement, reverting to IC work, avoiding difficult conversations, treating former peers as before).

Context:
- IC being promoted: {{NOMBRE_O_PERFIL}}
- Team to lead: {{TAMANO_Y_COMPOSICION_EQUIPO}}
- Sector and team context: {{SECTOR_Y_CONTEXTO}}
- Observed strengths and gaps: {{FORTALEZAS_Y_BRECHAS}}
- Available organizational support: {{SOPORTE_ORGANIZACIONAL}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before executing, restate in one or two sentences what you understand the assignment to be and present your work plan; then proceed. If any key data is empty or ambiguous, ask for it before proceeding; do not invent context.

Deliver a structured document with: (1) a matrix contrasting IC competencies against manager competencies and prioritizing the three most critical gaps with their evidence; (2) central mindset shifts, in particular success = team result, not personal output; (3) a 90-day schedule in three phases (1-30 learn and listen; 31-60 build credibility and first adjustments; 61-90 set own rhythm) with objectives, activities, and a deliverable per phase; (4) manager rituals (weekly one-on-ones, feedback cycle, retrospectives, career conversations) with cadence and purpose; (5) a table of success metrics and differentiated early warning signals, with an escalation threshold; (6) a matrix of common traps with a concrete countermove for each; (7) an editable script for the first one-on-one with each direct report, with a variant for former peers.

Mark with [ASSUMPTION] all data you infer. Flag where the plan depends on the direct manager's style or the company's culture. Close with the three highest-leverage moves for the first fourteen days. Neutral and professional Spanish, no emojis.

Act as a senior executive coach, certified in leadership, with 15 years accompanying managers in development plans based on multi-source feedback, the Johari window, and habit science. I need a personal leader self-development plan for {{HORIZONTE}}.

Context:
