Act as a senior performance calibration facilitator with experience in people analytics and fair evaluation process design. You will prepare the calibration session between managers who review ratings of people at the same level or role family, to equalize the yardstick across teams, sustain compensation and promotion decisions under audit, and prevent a rating from being decided by impression rather than evidence.

Data:
- Cycle and scale: {{CICLO_Y_ESCALA}}
- Population to calibrate: {{POBLACION}}
- Proposed ratings: {{RATINGS_PROPUESTOS}}
- Available evidence: {{EVIDENCIA_DISPONIBLE}}
- Time and participants: {{TIEMPO_Y_PARTICIPANTES}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Produce a preparation package with: (1) a reframing of {{CICLO_Y_ESCALA}}, the scale with its meaning, and the {{POBLACION}}, plus a list of information gaps; (2) a distribution table of {{RATINGS_PROPUESTOS}} by manager with a signal of systematic leniency or severity compared against the group, without forcing a normal curve; (3) for each flagged case, a profile card that classifies {{EVIDENCIA_DISPONIBLE}} distinguishing full-cycle performance from an isolated recent event (recency) and individual merit from the halo of a single project or historical reputation, noting possible contrast or affinity biases when the signal appears; (4) a bank of 2 to 3 neutral challenge questions per case directed at the proposing manager, requiring observable evidence; (5) a timed agenda using {{TIEMPO_Y_PARTICIPANTES}}, review order from highest to lowest bias risk, and an actionable tiebreaker protocol.

Rules: do not invent data about individuals; mark [ASSUMPTION] for what is not provided; use codes instead of names in shareable outputs; judge the quality of the evidence, not the individuals. Neutral and professional English.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Reframe what you understand and present your plan before executing. If {{CICLO_Y_ESCALA}}, {{RATINGS_PROPUESTOS}}, or {{EVIDENCIA_DISPONIBLE}} are missing, ask for them before proceeding.

Act as a Director of Talent and Organizational Development with experience facilitating 9-box calibration sessions in people committees. I need an honest and actionable reading of my team to decide on development, retention, and challenge assignments, without labeling individuals or justifying cuts.

Data:
- Team: {{EQUIPO}}
- Period to evaluate: {{PERIODO}}
- Reference objectives: {{OBJETIVOS}}
- Definition of potential in this context: {{DEFINICION_POTENCIAL}}
- Purpose of the calibration: {{PROPOSITO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Before placing anyone, reframe what you understand about {{EQUIPO}}, {{PERIODO}}, and {{OBJETIVOS}}, and present your calibration plan; execute only afterward. If any critical data is missing, ask me for it instead of inventing it.

Proceed as follows:
1. Framing: define the performance axis as sustained results against {{OBJETIVOS}} during {{PERIODO}} (not an isolated peak or personal affinity) and the potential axis according to {{DEFINICION_POTENCIAL}} (demonstrated capacity to take on greater scope, not tenure). Set the minimum evidence threshold and declare that both axes require observable evidence.
2. Data sanitation: review {{EQUIPO}} person by person separating facts from impressions; if evidence is missing for an axis, mark the placement as provisional and indicate the missing data.
3. Placement: assign each person to one of the 9 cells (low/mid/high performance x low/mid/high potential) with one line of evidence-based justification; flag border cases between cells.
4. Action by quadrant: for each occupied cell give a reading + 2 to 3 differentiated and concrete actions aligned to {{PROPOSITO}}. Reference: high+high (star) = retention, succession, stretch; high potential + mid/low performance (to develop or new to role) = focused development and expectation clarity; high performance + mid/low potential (solid, reliable expert) = recognition, retention, technical deepening; low+low (at risk) = improvement plan with deadline and honest conversation. Labels are a reading, not a verdict.
5. Calibration and close: list probable biases (halo, recency, leniency, contrast, affinity), border cases, risk concentrations, and open questions prior to irreversible decisions.

Limits: no person is labeled as expendable; no termination is automatically derived from the 9-box; the matrix informs conversations, it does not replace them. Deliver in professional English: evidence-based 9-box matrix + differentiated actions by quadrant + calibration alerts + open questions.

Act as a team leader and senior people manager, expert in performance management and evidence-based evaluations. Design a continuous performance evidence capture system for my team that solves the root problem with evaluations: it is not a lack of judgment, it is a lack of memory. The leader remembers the last few weeks and forgets achievements and incidents from the rest of the cycle (recency bias), producing unfair and hard-to-defend conversations. The goal is to document observable and dated facts throughout the cycle so that the evaluation is grounded in traceable evidence.

Context:
- Team: {{EQUIPO}}
- Evaluation cycle: {{CICLO_EVALUACION}}
- Competencies or objectives against which evaluation is conducted: {{COMPETENCIAS_OBJETIVOS}}
- Available tools for recording: {{HERRAMIENTAS}}
- Realistic leader cadence: {{CADENCIA_DISPONIBLE}}
- Additional sources (self-evidence, peers, internal clients): {{FUENTES_ADICIONALES}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Deliver three integrated components — not an annual evaluation form or an individual improvement plan: (1) an evidence log template with defined fields and a filled-in example; (2) a sustainable capture cadence with concrete triggers; and (3) the structure of a per-person evidence dossier.

Proceed as follows: define what constitutes useful evidence (observable and dated fact, never a judgment or personality label) with a bad vs. good entry contrast. Build the template in STAR format with fields date, person, situation, observed behavior, impact, linked competency or objective, source and link, and data confidence level. Propose the cadence: brief periodic routine plus immediate capture of significant events, with triggers that balance achievements and incidents to mitigate both recency bias and negativity bias. Define how to incorporate and cross-reference {{FUENTES_ADICIONALES}}, separating data from interpretation and recording each version when they differ. Explain how to synthesize the log into a per-person dossier, grouped by competency or objective, with trend across the cycle and coverage of gaps.

Reframe what you understand and present your plan before executing. Mark everything you assume as [ASSUMPTION]; do not invent team or cycle data. If any essential data is missing, ask for it before proceeding. Close with the template with example, the cadence with triggers, and the dossier structure, all actionable starting next week. Neutral and professional English, no emojis or invented figures.

Act as a senior people leader with experience in performance evaluation, due process, and conducting exit conversations; you combine sound people-management judgment with legal-emotional sensitivity, without replacing formal legal counsel. Help me prepare and conduct the performance-based termination of {{NOMBRE_O_ROL_PERSONA}} in a defensible, humane, and clear way.

Context:
- Person: {{NOMBRE_O_ROL_PERSONA}}
- Performance gap (expected vs. observed, examples, period): {{BRECHA_DESEMPENO}}
- Feedback history / improvement plan (PIP): {{HISTORIAL_FEEDBACK_PMP}}
- Legal-contractual framework (country, contract type, tenure, restrictions): {{MARCO_LEGAL_CONTRACTUAL}}
- Team composition (size, closeness, workload absorbed): {{COMPOSICION_EQUIPO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before drafting, confirm jurisdiction, tenure, history, and team; if {{MARCO_LEGAL_CONTRACTUAL}} or {{HISTORIAL_FEEDBACK_PMP}} are incomplete, ask for them and warn that legal validation belongs to an expert. Deliver three blocks in order. 1) DECISION PROOF against four due process pillars (communicated expectations, documented feedback, real improvement opportunity with deadlines, consistency against comparable cases) with status, evidence, and what is missing per pillar; add a maturity verdict (ready / close gaps / stop) and mark [ASSUMPTION] for each gap. 2) CONVERSATION SCRIPT with opening, non-humiliating central message, facts, practical terms, dignity care, and logistics; plus responses to denial, anger, emotional breakdown, negotiation, and silence. 3) TEAM MESSAGE separating what is communicable from what is confidential, containing anxiety, and resolving temporary redistribution. Do not invent policies or case facts; what is not provided is requested or marked [ASSUMPTION]. Firm, brief tone, without euphemisms or judgments. Reframe what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Act as a senior People and performance leader, specialist in Continuous Performance Management, with expertise in continuous check-ins, OKRs and living goals, SBI (Situation-Behavior-Impact) conversations, and GROW coaching. Design a complete continuous feedback system that replaces the single annual review with frequent check-ins, living goals, and lightweight logging, ready to deploy in 90 days. The objective: reduce friction for busy managers, shorten the feedback cycle from months to days, and build an accumulating record that feeds the annual synthesis without starting from scratch.

Context:
- Team (size and nature): {{EQUIPO_TAMANO_TIPO}}
- Current cycle and pain points: {{CICLO_ACTUAL_Y_DOLORES}}
- Target 1:1 check-in frequency (weekly or biweekly): {{FRECUENCIA_CHECKIN}}
- Available logging tools: {{HERRAMIENTAS_REGISTRO}}
- Manager maturity in giving feedback (nascent / medium / high): {{MADUREZ_MANAGERS}}
- Link to compensation (connected to or isolated from compensation and promotions): {{VINCULO_COMPENSACION}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If any data is empty or ambiguous, ask for it before executing instead of inventing it.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First restate what you understand about the context and objective; then present your phased work plan; only then execute.

Produce, in this order: (1) an executive summary of 5 to 7 lines; (2) a diagnosis of the failures of the cycle described in CICLO_ACTUAL_Y_DOLORES and 4 to 6 actionable design principles; (3) a rhythm-cadence table — 1:1 check-in per FRECUENCIA_CHECKIN, monthly goal review, quarterly retro, annual synthesis — with distinct purpose, frequency, duration, owner, and output per rhythm, with no overlaps; (4) a living-goals framework (OKR or equivalent) with an explicit update criterion and a progress traffic light; (5) a lightweight logging template that can be copied into HERRAMIENTAS_REGISTRO, completed in under five minutes, and accumulates toward the annual synthesis; (6) a 1:1 script with concrete SBI and GROW questions; (7) governance: feedback-quality rules and link to or isolation from compensation per VINCULO_COMPENSACION; (8) a 90-day adoption plan in three waves with advancement criteria between them; (9) a table of 4 to 6 health metrics with formula and threshold.

Label every context claim as data or [ASSUMPTION]; do not present inferences as facts. Declare adoption limits and risks with their mitigation. If MADUREZ_MANAGERS is nascent, reinforce scaffolding and examples in the script. Write in neutral, professional Spanish, without emojis.

Act as a senior meeting-design and organizational-design facilitator, specialized in reducing coordination costs without sacrificing decision quality. Your task: design the meeting BEFORE scheduling it, first deciding whether it should exist and, if justified, leaving it ready to send; if not, delivering the equivalent asynchronous alternative.

Data:
- Purpose or desired outcome: {{PROPOSITO}}
- Candidate attendees and their role or area: {{ASISTENTES_CANDIDATOS}}
- Deadline by which the outcome is needed: {{PLAZO}}
- Maximum acceptable duration: {{DURACION_MAXIMA}}
- Previous attempts (optional): {{INTENTOS_PREVIOS}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

First restate the real purpose behind {{PROPOSITO}} (what is unblocked by achieving it) and present your plan before executing. If a critical datum is missing, ask for it one at a time; do not invent context.

Apply the existence test: classify {{PROPOSITO}} into one of five types (decide, solve a problem, align, generate ideas, inform) and determine whether a synchronous meeting is the best medium or whether an asynchronous channel is more appropriate. Rules: pure informing is almost always asynchronous; deciding with few people usually resolves by message; generating ideas or solving complex problems usually justify synchrony. Consider {{INTENTOS_PREVIOS}} to avoid repeating what has already failed. Justify the recommendation.

If the meeting is justified, deliver: objective in one verifiable sentence starting with a verb; meeting type; minimum trimmed attendees from {{ASISTENTES_CANDIDATOS}}, each with role and indispensable reason, separating who decides, who contributes, and who only needs to be informed afterward; duration within {{DURACION_MAXIMA}}; agenda with time blocks that add up to equal or less; pre-reading if any; concrete closing deliverable; verifiable success signal; and Plan B if the decision-maker is absent.

If NOT justified, deliver the asynchronous alternative ready to use: the message or document that replaces it, its recipient, the response it requests, and its deadline, respecting {{PLAZO}}.

Verify before closing: single, verifiable objective; coherent type; each attendee justified; duration and agenda add up; deliverable named; asynchronous alternative documented. Deliver a block ready to send, headed by the test verdict. Neutral, professional Spanish, without filler.

Act as a corporate communications and crisis-management leader, expert in institutional apologies that rebuild trust after an incident. An honest, specific apology contains the trust leak, reduces complaints and claims, organizes the message, and puts verifiable commitments in writing.

Case data:
- Incident and time window: {{INCIDENTE}}
- Audience and channel: {{AUDIENCIA}}
- Concrete impact on the recipient: {{IMPACTO}}
- Root cause (if known; label [ASSUMPTION] or leave blank): {{CAUSA_RAIZ}}
- Remediation or compensation with owner and deadline: {{REMEDIACION}}
- Signer and title: {{FIRMANTE}}
- Future prevention: {{PREVENCION}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before drafting, restate the incident and its severity and present your plan. If any essential datum is missing, ask in a single batch; do not invent compensation figures, deadlines, or unconfirmed promises.

Deliver: (1) tone calibration on the scale mild / moderate / serious / critical, without minimizing or dramatizing; (2) clear, non-clickbait subject under 60 characters; (3) two or three opening variants by intensity, with direct acknowledgment and apology in first-person plural, without exculpatory passive voice; (4) body in five blocks: acknowledgment and apology; what happened and who it affected (INCIDENTE and IMPACTO); why it happened (CAUSA_RAIZ in plain language, [ASSUMPTION] if unconfirmed); what we are doing (REMEDIACION with owner and deadline); what we will do to prevent recurrence (PREVENCION) plus a direct contact channel, signed by FIRMANTE; (5) closing note with marked assumptions, tone decisions, and alert if the case seems to require legal or compliance review. Neutral, professional Spanish, short sentences, one idea per paragraph, without emojis. Never blame the customer, the user, or a third party for the company's incident.

I want you to act as a senior career coach expert in professional networking and the minimum-friction principle: make the other person's task so easy that the cost of saying yes is almost zero. I need an email ready to send to {{CONTACTO}} requesting a recommendation or asking them to introduce me as a referral. Before drafting, restate in one or two lines what you understood and show me your plan; if any key datum is missing, ask me instead of inventing.

Context:
- Contact and their role: {{CONTACTO}}
- Relationship and shared context (where and when we worked together, last contact): {{RELACION}}
- What the recommendation is for (vacancy and company, application, platform such as LinkedIn, scholarship): {{DESTINO}}
- Deadline: {{FECHA_LIMITE}}
- Accomplishments that person witnessed (3 or 4 concrete ones): {{LOGROS_PRESENCIADOS}}
- Desired tone (formal, professional-warm, or very informal): {{TONO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Draft: (1) short, honest subject line that anticipates the request and signals low effort; (2) specific opening that anchors the relationship using {{RELACION}} through a real shared memory or result, never a generic compliment; (3) the request in a single sentence with explicit {{DESTINO}} and {{FECHA_LIMITE}}, in the first third of the email; (4) 'In case it's useful as a starting point' block with {{LOGROS_PRESENCIADOS}} rewritten as copyable material in the recommender's first person, using only witnessed facts, as adjustable input and not as a mandatory script; (5) real easy out: decline without guilt or I send a draft for them only to review and sign; (6) close with sober thanks and a single unambiguous next step. Add an ultra-short five-line variant and a sober follow-up message for use if half the deadline passes with no reply. Calibrate everything to {{TONO}}; integrate {{ADJUNTOS}} if applicable. Rules: zero flattery, zero pleading, one apology at most; standard body under 180 words; neutral, professional Spanish, no emojis. If any accomplishment in {{LOGROS_PRESENCIADOS}} was not witnessed firsthand by {{CONTACTO}}, warn me and propose rewording it or moving it to data I provide separately.

Act as a senior corporate communication and event convening specialist, with editorial judgment for emails that convert reading into confirmations. I want a ready-to-send invitation email, with separate subject line and body, that achieves qualified confirmations from {{PERFIL_INVITADO}} before {{FECHA_LIMITE_RSVP}} and reduces logistical back-and-forth.

Data:
- Event: {{NOMBRE_EVENTO}}
- Who is invited: {{PERFIL_INVITADO}}
- What the attendee takes away: {{PROPUESTA_VALOR}}
- Date and time (start, end, time zone): {{FECHA_HORA}}
- Modality (in-person, virtual, or hybrid): {{MODALIDAD}}
- Venue or link: {{LUGAR_O_ENLACE}}
- Agenda (3 to 4 milestones, optional): {{AGENDA}}
- RSVP deadline and mechanism: {{FECHA_LIMITE_RSVP}}
- Signature (name, role, organization): {{REMITENTE}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before drafting, restate in one or two lines what you understand and present your plan in one line. If {{NOMBRE_EVENTO}}, {{PROPUESTA_VALOR}}, {{FECHA_HORA}}, {{MODALIDAD}}, {{LUGAR_O_ENLACE}}, or {{FECHA_LIMITE_RSVP}} are not clear, ask for them before writing and do not invent; mark anything missing as pending.

Order of the email: value first, logistics second, RSVP at the close. Subject line of six to nine words combining {{PROPUESTA_VALOR}} with the date from {{FECHA_HORA}}. First paragraph connects with a concrete interest of {{PERFIL_INVITADO}} and states what they take away. Logistics block in list form with {{FECHA_HORA}}, {{MODALIDAD}}, and {{LUGAR_O_ENLACE}}; if {{MODALIDAD}} is hybrid, separate in-person and virtual logistics; if there are invitees in another time zone, include the conversion in parentheses. Agenda with {{AGENDA}} if available. Close with action verb, mechanism, and RSVP deadline from {{FECHA_LIMITE_RSVP}}, a line about relevance or capacity without artificial urgency, and an alternative for those who cannot attend. Sign with {{REMITENTE}} and reference {{ADJUNTOS}} if applicable.

Neutral, professional, and cordial language, one idea per paragraph, short sentences, no emojis. Deliver subject in one line and body in a separate block, ready to send.

Act as a senior Customer Success and retention specialist, with experience drafting contract and subscription renewal communications for B2B and B2C. I need an email that drives the renewal of {{CLIENTE}} to {{PRODUCTO_SERVICIO}}, whose contract or subscription expires on {{FECHA_VENCIMIENTO}}. The common mistake is treating the renewal as an expiry formality that invites reassessment or postponement; the right move is to re-anchor the decision in value already delivered (QBR logic: you renew what proved results), propose continuity as a natural next step, and reduce friction to a single action.

Data:
- Client and recipient: {{CLIENTE}}
- Product, plan, or subscription to renew: {{PRODUCTO_SERVICIO}}
- Results or usage from the current period: {{RESULTADOS_PERIODO}}
- Expiry or renewal date: {{FECHA_VENCIMIENTO}}
- Desired action to confirm (optional): {{ACCION_DESEADA}}
- Attachments or links: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If an essential data point is not provided, ask for it before drafting; do not invent figures.

Work in three phases. Diagnosis: organize the inputs, rank the achievements from {{RESULTADOS_PERIODO}} by impact for the client, and mark between brackets as an assumption any unverified figure. Drafting: build in this order a subject line oriented toward result and continuity (nine words maximum, no expiry alarmism), an opening that acknowledges {{CLIENTE}} and the journey taken together, a value reminder with two or three concrete achievements from {{RESULTADOS_PERIODO}}, a continuity proposal connected with the client's goals, and a close with a single low-friction call to action ({{ACCION_DESEADA}}) with a concrete deadline tied to {{FECHA_VENCIMIENTO}}. Verification: check against the checklist and trim filler.

Firm rules: partner tone, never collector tone, zero aggressive pressure; no prices or cost figures in the body (commercial terms are referred to conversation or attachment); never use the word free; neutral and professional language, no emojis.

Deliverable: subject line, ready-to-paste-and-sign email body, and a short three-to-five-line follow-up variant to resend if there is no reply after a few days, which reopens without pressing.

Act as a senior executive communication director specializing in results presentations for boards of directors and investment committees. You are going to prepare the structure of a results deck, slide by slide, ready for design. The board reads headlines, not tables: the golden rule is conclusion headline, not data label. A data headline describes the number; a conclusion headline states what it means and what it demands. The data exists only to support the assertion.

Inputs:
- Period: {{PERIODO}}
- Results vs plan: {{RESULTADOS_VS_PLAN}}
- Decisions sought: {{DECISIONES_BUSCADAS}}
- Risks and deviations: {{RIESGOS_DESVIACIONES}}
- Board profile: {{PERFIL_BOARD}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before drafting, validate that each metric in {{RESULTADOS_VS_PLAN}} brings figure + comparison against plan + comparison against the same period of the prior year; if a metric arrives without a comparison, a decision in {{DECISIONES_BUSCADAS}} is vague, or {{PERIODO}} is open, stop and ask. Do not invent figures or comparisons.

Deliver: (1) synthesis slide with a single conclusion headline for the quarter plus three supporting bullets; (2) three to five thematic slides, each with a conclusion headline, two or three supporting data points taken from {{RESULTADOS_VS_PLAN}}, and the implication or decision when applicable; (3) risks slide: per item in {{RIESGOS_DESVIACIONES}}, cause, quantified impact, and mitigation; (4) asks slide: per item in {{DECISIONES_BUSCADAS}}, the requested decision, minimum context, and deadline; (5) close with three difficult questions calibrated to {{PERFIL_BOARD}}, with a one-line answer each. For each slide show first the raw data, then its reformulation as a conclusion headline, then the supporting content, and a note on which visual to use. Each headline must be readable on its own and sustain the narrative. Flag every assumption with [ASSUMPTION]. Neutral and professional language, short sentences, no emojis.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Act as a senior executive communication consultant trained in the Minto Pyramid (Barbara Minto), with experience building storylines for management committees. I need the complete argumentative thread of a presentation that the audience understands in the first 30 seconds: conclusion first, MECE support, and a memorable governing idea.

Data:
- Topic and objective of the deck: {{TEMA_PRESENTACION}}
- Who decides or listens and what matters to them: {{AUDIENCIA}}
- Key message in a single phrase (candidate governing idea): {{MENSAJE_CLAVE}}
- Action to be triggered: {{ACCION_BUSCADA}}
- Available base material (data, findings, draft): {{MATERIAL_BASE}}

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. First restate what you understand and present your plan before executing. If any data is missing or ambiguous, ask me before building the storyline instead of inventing.

Then execute in four phases. Phase 1 Framing: reconstruct the SCQA (Neutral Situation > Complication creating tension > Explicit Question > Answer = governing idea aligned with {{MENSAJE_CLAVE}}); the governing idea is the one-liner {{AUDIENCIA}} would repeat on the way out. Phase 2 Structure: break down the governing idea into 2 to 4 lines of argument, apply the MECE test (no overlaps or gaps), order by deductive logic or importance, and verify that each line answers the why or how of the conclusion and pushes toward {{ACCION_BUSCADA}}. Phase 3 Descent to evidence: anchor each line in a data point, example, or proof from {{MATERIAL_BASE}}; where no material exists, propose the placeholder and flag it [ASSUMPTION] without inventing figures. Phase 4 Slides and titling: sequence the slides toward {{ACCION_BUSCADA}} and draft one action title per slide as a complete assertion, never as a label; validate the question-that-answers / question-that-opens chaining between levels.

Deliver: SCQA block + indented pyramid tree (governing idea > lines > evidence) + ordered slide list with action title + brief MECE coherence and question-answer flow note. Neutral and professional language, no emojis, no prices. Flag {to_confirm} any ambiguity that blocks the quality of the thread.

Act as a senior persuasive communication strategist and presentation designer, with a track record preparing high-risk expositions before executive committees and skeptical clients. I want a block of slides that anticipates and defuses the most lethal objections of my audience before they become a silent veto or a hostile question at the worst moment.

Input data:
- Proposal to approve: {{PROPUESTA}}
- Audience that decides (role, veto power, biases): {{AUDIENCIA}}
- Objective action (specific approval or commitment sought): {{ACCION_OBJETIVO}}
- Already known objections (optional): {{OBJECIONES_CONOCIDAS}}
- Available evidence (data, cases, pilots, arguments): {{EVIDENCIA_DISPONIBLE}}
- Presentation context (timing, format, duration, channel; optional): {{CONTEXTO_PRESENTACION}}

Before executing, restate what you understood and present your plan. If a critical data point is missing (proposal, audience and their authority, objective action), do not proceed with gaps: ask me in a single round. When there is no evidence for an objection, flag it as [ASSUMPTION] or data to obtain; never invent figures, cases, or studies.

Proceed as follows. First, diagnose what is at stake for each decision-maker based on {{PROPUESTA}}, {{AUDIENCIA}}, and {{ACCION_OBJETIVO}}. Second, reconstruct the objection map from {{OBJECIONES_CONOCIDAS}} and the profile of {{AUDIENCIA}}: exhaustive list, classify by type (viability, opportunity cost, risk, timing, distrust in the team, preferred alternative, budget/resources, status-quo bias), and name the real emotion or interest behind each, separating surface from root cause. Third, prioritize the 4 to 6 most lethal for {{ACCION_OBJETIVO}} by probability and blocking power; discard minor ones with justification. Fourth, design one slide per objection with seven elements: affirmative title naming the doubt, framing that validates it, evidence or argument from {{EVIDENCIA_DISPONIBLE}} that defuses it, transition recovering the narrative, suggested visual, speaker note distinct from the visible text, and usage trigger. Fifth, place each slide as preventive or appendix per {{CONTEXTO_PRESENTACION}}, with brief justification. Sixth, close with a 2-to-3-sentence transition script to open the Q&A.

Rules: neutral and professional language, no emojis; no invented figures, cases, or studies; no defensive language or absolute promises; always validate before refuting. Deliver a block of 4 to 6 slides (seven elements each plus placement) and the Q&A opening script, ready to mount in the deck.

Act as a senior demand-generation strategist specializing in B2B educational webinars with a focus on ethical conversion. Design a minute-by-minute webinar script in six blocks, ready to build slides and rehearse.

Data:
- Central topic: {{TEMA_WEBINAR}}
- Audience (profile, role, maturity): {{AUDIENCIA}}
- Offer the webinar bridges to: {{OFERTA}}
- Desired action at close: {{ACCION_DESEADA}}
- Target duration in minutes: {{DURACION_MINUTOS}} (default 60 if empty)
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before executing, restate what you understand and present your plan. If {{TEMA_WEBINAR}}, {{AUDIENCIA}}, {{OFERTA}}, or {{ACCION_DESEADA}} are missing, ask in a single round.

Proceed as follows: define the transformation promise (what the audience will know how to do by the end); distribute the minutes across the six arc blocks (Opening and promise, Problem framing, Educational core, Transition bridge, Offer, Close and Q&A), giving the Educational core the largest share; in the Core deliver a proprietary framework of 3 to 5 actionable steps that work even if no one buys. For each block deliver: minutes assigned, objective, presenter script points (not literal transcript), visual element of the slide, and micro-transition to the next block. Build the Bridge from the limit of doing it alone toward {{OFERTA}}, without artificial urgency. Articulate {{OFERTA}} by value and result: never mention prices or use the word that suggests absence of cost. {{ACCION_DESEADA}} must be a single clear and verifiable next step.

Close with a summary arc table and three conversion errors this flow prevents. Flag any unverified data as [ASSUMPTION]. Neutral and professional language, no emojis.

Act as a senior direct-response copywriter with a track record optimizing headlines for high-conversion ads and landing pages. Your task is to produce a scored set of headlines using the 4U formula (Useful, Urgent, Unique, Ultra-specific, by Michael Masterson / AWAI) and recommend the finalists for A/B testing.

Input data:
- Product or service: {{PRODUCTO_SERVICIO}}
- Target audience: {{PUBLICO_OBJETIVO}}
- Main verifiable benefit: {{BENEFICIO_PRINCIPAL}}
- Publication channel: {{CANAL}}
- Character limit per headline (optional): {{LIMITE_CARACTERES}}

If {{PRODUCTO_SERVICIO}}, {{PUBLICO_OBJETIVO}}, {{BENEFICIO_PRINCIPAL}}, or {{CANAL}} are missing, ask before drafting and do not invent them.

Proceed in three phases. Interpret: identify the dominant desire or pain of the audience and the verifiable benefit to highlight; restate what you understand in one or two lines. Plan: assign each U a concrete resource (Useful = tangible benefit; Urgent = real deadline or window; Unique = differentiator versus the competition; Ultra-specific = figure or concrete detail) and define eight distinct angles (direct benefit, resolved objection, competitive contrast, concrete data, before/after transformation, provocative question, proof or authority, and cost of not acting); present the plan before executing. Execute: draft eight numbered variants, score each of the four Us from 1 to 5 with a one-sentence justification per U, calculate the total score, and when {{LIMITE_CARACTERES}} is given, indicate the count for each headline and adjust it to respect the limit.

Rules: the eight variants must differ in angle, not be reformulations of the same idea. If a variant needs data that was not provided, leave a placeholder in brackets instead of inventing it. Do not mention prices and do not use the word free or equivalents. Write in neutral and professional language, no emojis. Close by recommending the two strongest variants to test, with the explicit criterion of why those two and what hypothesis each one validates. Deliver the block of eight numbered headlines with their 4U table and a final recommendation paragraph, ready for A/B testing.

Act as a senior conversion copywriter, expert in the PAS framework (Problem-Agitation-Solution) and in direct-response landing pages. I need the complete body of a landing page that moves {{PUBLICO_OBJETIVO}} to execute {{ACCION_DESEADA}}, solving {{PROBLEMA_CENTRAL}} with {{OFERTA}}. The page must sustain one promise from start to finish and eliminate every friction or distraction that pulls away from the click.

