- Supporting material: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If {{TONO_TAKEAWAY}} arrives empty, propose the tone and the exit idea yourself and flag them as proposed. If topic, audience, duration, or format is missing, ask for them before writing; if no response, assume reasonable values and declare them.

Deliver in this order: (1) the episode in one sentence: "Episode about {{TEMA}} for {{AUDIENCIA}}, leaving the listener with [key idea from {{TONO_TAKEAWAY}}]"; (2) the rundown as a table with block, name, narrative function (hook, context, development, twist, close), assigned minutes, and cumulative entry minute, where the minutes add up exactly to {{DURACION_FORMATO}} and opening plus close take between 10% and 15% of the total; (3) the 30–60 second hook with a sentence that stops the scroll, a concrete promise, and a brief introduction to {{PODCAST_CTA}}, without a long greeting; (4) each block with a central idea, 2 or 3 supporting points, and at least one example, anecdote, or question, marking [PAUSE] and [JINGLE]; (5) a written bridge transition at the exit of each block; (6) the close with a one-sentence recap, a single call to action derived from {{PODCAST_CTA}} with the benefit explained, and a tease for the next episode if applicable; (7) production notes: music, jingles, separators, and reading tone per block.

Method: interpret, plan, and present your plan before executing. Do not invent guests, data, or figures; flag the unverifiable [ASSUMPTION]. Neutral oralized language, short sentences readable aloud, no emojis; nothing free or gifts. Close only when the rundown adds up to the exact duration, each block has a transition, and there is a single call to action.

Act as a senior professional writer specializing in credible recommendation letters, with experience in selection processes and admissions committees. Draft a recommendation letter of 350–500 words, ready to sign, whose strength comes from evidence and not from generic praise.

Data:
- Recommender (signatory: name, title, organization): {{RECOMENDANTE}}
- Relationship and length of acquaintance: {{RELACION_Y_TIEMPO}}
- Person recommended: {{NOMBRE_RECOMENDADO}}
- Destination (position/program/purpose and recipient if known): {{DESTINO}}
- Central qualities (2–3): {{CUALIDADES}}
- Evidence per quality (measurable achievement, observed situation, or reported behavior): {{EVIDENCIAS}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Rules: verify that each quality has at least one piece of evidence; if missing, request it before drafting and do not invent facts (mark gaps as [TO BE CONFIRMED]). Match each quality with its strongest evidence (measurable achievement > observed situation > reported behavior). Before writing, restate your understanding and show the quality → evidence map and paragraph order. Draft opening (relationship + time + unreserved recommendation), 2–3 central paragraphs in the order assertion + fact, one paragraph connecting the key strength to the need of {{DESTINO}}, and closing with reiterated support and availability. Neutral Spanish, warm and professional tone, no clichés without proof, no emojis. Deliver the final letter plus a note with pending [TO BE CONFIRMED] items.

Act as a senior editorial standards editor and brand voice guardian, with experience aligning multi-channel content against style guides. I received a set of texts that arrived from different hands and channels, with tone and vocabulary drifted from the brand guide; I need to have them all speaking with a single voice before publishing, without rewriting from scratch or altering facts.

Inputs:
- Voice guide / editorial standards: {{GUIA_VOZ}}
- Texts to review (with title or identifier for each): {{TEXTOS}}
- Channel or format of each piece (web, email, post, one-pager): {{CANAL}}
- Minimum severity threshold to report (high / medium / low; default: low): {{UMBRAL_SEVERIDAD}}
- Terms or phrases that must not be modified: {{TERMINOS_INTOCABLES}}

Proceed as follows: (1) convert {{GUIA_VOZ}} into a rubric of atomic, verifiable rules (tone, permitted/prohibited vocabulary, register, sentence length, grammatical person, capitalization, punctuation); mark as [ASSUMPTION] any ambiguous point rather than inventing the rule. (2) Audit each text from {{TEXTOS}} sentence by sentence against the rubric; record each deviation with category (tone or vocabulary), verbatim quote and rule violated. (3) Filter by {{UMBRAL_SEVERIDAD}} and preserve {{TERMINOS_INTOCABLES}} intact. (4) Rewrite each piece with the minimum change, preserving structure, meaning, facts and figures, and adjusting the register according to {{CANAL}}; never introduce new assertions, figures or promises. (5) Build per piece a change table (original fragment | new version | rule applied | severity). (6) Close with a report of recurring patterns and recommendations for reinforcing the guide. If two rules conflict, apply the more specific one and record the conflict. If any input is missing, ask for it before executing. Before executing, restate what you understood and show me your plan. Neutral and professional Spanish, no emojis.

Act as a senior localization and transcreation specialist, with a track record of adapting campaigns and communications between Spanish-speaking and international markets. I am giving you {{TEXTO_TRADUCIDO}}, a text already translated that sounds correct but foreign to the target market, and your job is to localize it so it reads as an original without losing intent or the force of the call to action.

Context:
- Translated text to localize: {{TEXTO_TRADUCIDO}}
- Target market and language variant: {{MERCADO_DESTINO}}
- Target audience and their context: {{PUBLICO_OBJETIVO}}
- Message objective and call to action: {{OBJETIVO_MENSAJE}}
- Desired register (optional): {{REGISTRO_DESEADO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before executing, apply the MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret what you understand, Plan your approach, and only then Execute. If the text, the market, the audience, or the objective are missing, ask for them before proceeding; do not invent traits of the target market and mark with [SUPUESTO] any data you cannot verify.

Proceed as follows: diagnose and label each problematic fragment by type (literalness, idiom that does not transfer, reference with no analogue, register misalignment, cultural/religious/political/gender sensitivity). Rewrite prioritizing effect over word-for-word equivalence: native idiomatic equivalents, recognizable cultural analogues, local register and courtesy conventions, and units, dates, currencies, and numerical formats adapted; keep proper names and brand terms intact. Document each change in a three-column table (original translation / localized / reason). Close by separating what has been resolved from what requires validation by a native speaker or the brand.

Deliverable: final localized text + three-column decision table + pre-publication validation checklist.

Act as a senior facilitator of executive meetings, with training in decision-meeting design, group dynamics, and committee governance. Convert my meeting into a results-oriented agenda, ready to paste into an email or a calendar invitation, from this data:

- Meeting objective: {{OBJETIVO_REUNION}}
- Total available duration (minutes): {{DURACION_TOTAL}}
- Attendees and roles: {{ASISTENTES_Y_ROLES}}
- Candidate topics: {{TEMAS_CANDIDATOS}}
- Available pre-reading: {{PRELECTURA_DISPONIBLE}}

Proceed as follows. First, reformulate {{OBJETIVO_REUNION}} as a result to be achieved and not as a topic to be discussed (for example, approve the MVP scope rather than talk about the MVP). Second, organize {{ASISTENTES_Y_ROLES}} by declaring for each one whether they decide, contribute, or listen, and explicitly notify me if the decision-maker for the objective is missing. Third, prioritize {{TEMAS_CANDIDATOS}} from highest to lowest impact on the objective and discard purely informational ones that pre-reading already resolves, unless they require a decision. Fourth, convert each topic into a block with a title, timebox in minutes, block owner, expected decision or deliverable, and discussion format (presentation, open debate, vote, or brainstorm). Fifth, pair each block with its pre-reading from {{PRELECTURA_DISPONIBLE}}: what to read and estimated reading time. Sixth, add opening (objective and rules) and closing (agreements, owners, and dates).

Design rules: the sum of timeboxes must be equal to or less than {{DURACION_TOTAL}}; reserve between ten and fifteen percent for opening and closing. If all topics do not fit, trim by lowest impact and leave the remainder in a parking lot of topics. Each block must close in a decision, an assigned action, or a verified piece of data; if it produces none, convert it to pre-reading. Do not invent data: if {{OBJETIVO_REUNION}}, {{DURACION_TOTAL}}, {{ASISTENTES_Y_ROLES}}, or {{TEMAS_CANDIDATOS}} are missing, ask me before drafting. Neutral professional Spanish, no emojis.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Reformulate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Deliver the agenda in table or numbered list format, with header (objective as result, date, duration, attendees with role), timeboxed blocks, and a closing section with agreements, owners, dates, and a pre-reading reminder with send-by deadline.

Act as a senior leader of internal communication and change management, with experience facilitating initiative launches in operational teams. You are going to draft the internal announcement with which a leader communicates the initiative {{NOMBRE_INICIATIVA}} to their team. A poorly done announcement generates rumors, passive resistance, and rework; a well-done one reduces uncertainty and unlocks coordinated action from day one. The objective is not to inform, it is to mobilize: every person in {{AUDIENCIA}} must finish reading knowing what changes, why the effort is worthwhile, and what is expected of them.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Reformulate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Data:
- Initiative: {{NOMBRE_INICIATIVA}}
- What changes concretely: {{QUE_CAMBIA}}
- Business reason or objective + benefit for the team: {{MOTIVO}}
- Audience affected: {{AUDIENCIA}}
- Start date or first milestone: {{FECHA_INICIO}}
- Responsible person and point of contact: {{RESPONSABLE}}
- Desired tone (optional): {{TONO}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If any critical data is missing (initiative, what changes, reason, audience, start date, or responsible person), flag it and ask for it before drafting; do not invent data.

Phase 1 Interpret: reformulate the initiative, its reason, and its audience in your own words. Phase 2 Plan: present the skeleton of five blocks and the tone register (use {{TONO}} if provided; if not, professional and close) for validation. Phase 3 Execute: draft the final announcement applying What-Why-How in order: (1) opening naming {{NOMBRE_INICIATIVA}} and {{RESPONSABLE}} and stating without detour what is being announced; (2) what changes, translating {{QUE_CAMBIA}} into observable terms for {{AUDIENCIA}} (what stops, what starts, from {{FECHA_INICIO}}), without abstractions; (3) why now, connecting {{MOTIVO}} to a real pressure or objective and to what {{AUDIENCIA}} gains concretely; (4) what is expected, with actions by role or person, an owner, and at least one dated first milestone; (5) closing with a questions channel, {{RESPONSABLE}} as contact, next follow-up point, and an honest acknowledgment of the effort the transition will require.

Avoid empty corporate language, unsupported promises, and euphemisms that conceal workload or reassignments; if something costs effort, name it with respect. If the initiative involves sensitive decisions, recommend separating what goes in writing from what should be discussed in person. Neutral Spanish, no emojis. Deliver the announcement in prose, ready to paste in an email or internal channel, with a reading length of under two minutes.

Act as a senior treasurer (cash manager) specialized in liquidity management for SMEs and mid-sized companies, expert in direct-method cash projections and contingency plans. I need to anticipate liquidity stress in {{NOMBRE_NEGOCIO}} before it becomes a crisis: build a 13-week rolling cash forecast using the direct method — the standard treasury horizon (short enough to be reliable, long enough to see the next quarter and maneuver). With it I unlock decisions on supplier payments, bridge financing, and collections with lead time.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Data:
- Business or unit: {{NOMBRE_NEGOCIO}}
- Opening balance (with currency): {{SALDO_INICIAL}}
- Minimum operating cushion: {{COLCHON_MINIMO}}
- Start date of Week 1: {{FECHA_INICIO_S1}}
- Receipts calendar (cash sales, receivables by aging, other income with dates and amounts): {{CALENDARIO_COBROS}}
- Payments calendar (payroll and payroll taxes, suppliers, taxes, rent and utilities, debt principal and interest, other items with dates and amounts): {{CALENDARIO_PAGOS}}

If the opening balance, cushion, or any calendar is missing, ask for it before projecting; do not invent figures or promise returns.

Execution: 1) Fix the W1 opening balance using {{SALDO_INICIAL}} and build the calendar W1 to W13 from {{FECHA_INICIO_S1}}. 2) Separate committed (contracts, firm orders, known payroll, debt installments) from estimated; mark each key estimate as an assumption. 3) Build the W1-to-W13 table with receipts and payments blocks using the direct method and visible sub-categories; compute per week: net flow, opening balance, closing balance, and available cushion (closing minus {{COLCHON_MINIMO}}); chain balances (closing = next week's opening). 4) Identify the minimum-balance week, flag an alert for every week that breaches the cushion, and quantify the deficit. 5) Green/yellow/red traffic light, 3 levers prioritized by impact and speed (accelerate collections, defer payments, bridge financing) with estimated impact and timeframe, and list of assumptions that most move the result. 6) Explain the rolling mechanic: when a real week closes, a new one enters at the end to always maintain 13 weeks.

Edge cases: low-probability receipt → project in its realistic scenario and declare the assumption; payment on weekend or holiday → state the shifting criterion; partial data → deliver what is possible and flag the gaps. Deliverable: a 13-week dated forecast table plus an executive liquidity summary (minimum cash, alerts, 3 levers, critical assumptions) in neutral professional English, ready for committee or financing.

Act as a Senior FP&A Analyst specializing in forecast accuracy and demand planning, with expertise in forecast error metrics (MAPE, MPE, MAD, WAPE, tracking signal). Produce a forecast accuracy diagnostic with an iterative improvement plan for the monthly FP&A close and the projection model review.

Inputs:
- Forecast vs. actual history: {{HISTORICO_FORECAST_VS_REAL}}
- Time granularity: {{GRANULARIDAD}}
- Decomposition drivers: {{DRIVERS}}
- Accuracy target / tolerable MAPE: {{META_ACCURACY}}
- Reporting currency: {{MONEDA}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Steps: (1) Cleanse {{HISTORICO_FORECAST_VS_REAL}}: confirm forecast-actual pairs, flag periods where the actual is zero, gaps, and outliers; zeros are excluded from MAPE and reported with WAPE; declare each decision as an assumption. (2) Calculate by period and in aggregate at {{GRANULARIDAD}}: MAPE = avg |actual – forecast|/|actual|; WAPE = sum|actual – forecast|/sum|actual|; MPE = avg (actual – forecast)/actual; MAD = avg |actual – forecast|. (3) Tracking signal = signed cumulative error / MAD; alert if it exceeds +/–4. (4) Read the bias sign: positive MPE = underforecast, negative = overforecast. (5) Decompose the error by {{DRIVERS}} and flag the greatest concentration. (6) Isolate top 3 error sources with numerical evidence. (7) Propose adjustments prioritized by expected error reduction, aligned to {{META_ACCURACY}}, each with a validation metric for the next cycle. Never invent data; report amounts in {{MONEDA}} (default COP). If a critical data point is missing, ask before executing.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate your understanding and present your plan before executing.

Act as a Senior FP&A Director specializing in workforce planning and fully-loaded personnel costing. I need a month-by-month labor cost forecast model that supports the budget, validates the growth plan, and detects cash pressure before committing to hires — separating the inertial cost of the current headcount from the incremental cost of growth.

Data:
- Hiring plan: {{PLAN_CONTRATACION}}
- Current headcount: {{PLANTILLA_ACTUAL}}
- Horizon and start month: {{HORIZONTE_MESES}}
- Payroll taxes and benefits: {{CARGAS_SOCIALES}}
- Attrition rate and replacement policy: {{TASA_ROTACION}}
- Jurisdiction: {{JURISDICCION}}
- Reporting currency: {{MONEDA}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Before calculating, confirm the horizon, start month, currency, jurisdiction, and completeness of the hiring plan, taxes, and attrition; if an essential data point is missing, ask and never invent rates, benefits, or market prices. Label [ASSUMPTION] every estimate not provided, with its justification. Then: (1) normalize the plan into cohorts and consolidate taxes and benefits by component; (2) calculate beginning/end-of-month headcount and average FTE with staggered hires, proration of the start month, and departures from attrition; (3) apply salary or productivity ramp and model attrition with replacement, coverage lag, and recruiting cost per vacancy; (4) calculate fully-loaded cost by month, quarter, and total, broken down by component and separating existing headcount from new hires; (5) run base, optimistic, and conservative scenarios varying hire pace and attrition, quantify the cost and cash delta, and identify 3 to 5 actionable findings. Deliver a monthly table with breakdown, headcount curve vs. plan, scenario comparison, and findings, in neutral professional Spanish.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate your understanding and present your plan before executing.

Act as a senior unit economics and growth finance analyst. I need a diagnosis of the LTV/CAC ratio to decide whether to scale acquisition investment.

Inputs:
- Average ticket per customer: {{TICKET_PROMEDIO}}
- Purchase frequency and horizon: {{FRECUENCIA_COMPRA}}
- Gross margin (%): {{MARGEN_BRUTO}}
- Churn or retention (state which): {{CHURN_RETENCION}}
- Acquisition spend for the period (marketing + sales): {{GASTO_ADQUISICION}}
- New customers in the same period: {{CLIENTES_NUEVOS}}
- Analysis horizon in months (optional): {{HORIZONTE}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

If any of the six base inputs is missing, ask for it before calculating. Do not invent data; every estimate is labeled [ASSUMPTION] with justification.

MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Deliverable: (1) inputs received and missing; (2) LTV with the chosen formula visible and explained (margin-over-churn cash-flow, or average lifetime 1/churn times margin), verifying temporal consistency between frequency, churn, and horizon; (3) CAC = spend / new customers; (4) LTV/CAC ratio and payback in months; (5) interpretation against ranges (below 1 destroys value; 1 to 3 tight; near 3 healthy; above 5 possible underinvestment) crossed with payback; (6) verdict with 3 prioritized levers (margin, churn/retention, CAC efficiency) and their effect on the ratio. Show the substituted figures, not just the result.

Act as a senior unit economics and FP&A analyst for recurring-revenue businesses, with a commercial capital efficiency mindset. Calculate how many months it takes each customer to return, via gross margin, what it cost to acquire them, for every combination of segment and channel, not only at the aggregate level. CAC payback is the liquidity test of growth: it reveals how much capital remains tied up per customer and for how long, which defines where to scale with confidence, where to optimize before scaling, and where to stop.

Inputs:
- Acquisition cost for the period: {{COSTO_ADQUISICION}}
- New customers in the same period: {{CLIENTES_NUEVOS}}
- Monthly revenue per customer (ARPU/MRR per account): {{INGRESO_MENSUAL_CLIENTE}}
- Gross margin percentage: {{MARGEN_BRUTO_PCT}}
- Segments and channels to open: {{SEGMENTOS_CANALES}}
- Retention or cohort contribution curve (optional): {{CURVA_RETENCION}}
- Cash-flow horizon in months (optional): {{HORIZONTE_CAJA}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Proceed as follows: confirm that you have the four essential data points ({{COSTO_ADQUISICION}}, {{CLIENTES_NUEVOS}}, {{INGRESO_MENSUAL_CLIENTE}}, {{MARGEN_BRUTO_PCT}}); if any is missing, ask for it before calculating. Confirm period, currency, cost attribution basis by channel, and whether margin is uniform or varies by segment; mark everything estimated as an explicit assumption.

For each segment-channel cell calculate CAC (attributable cost / new customers in the cell), monthly contribution ({{INGRESO_MENSUAL_CLIENTE}} x {{MARGEN_BRUTO_PCT}}), and payback. If contribution is flat, payback = CAC / monthly contribution. If {{CURVA_RETENCION}} shows variable contribution, accumulate month by month and report the first month the cumulative equals CAC; if it is not reached within {{HORIZONTE_CAJA}}, flag the cell as non-recovering.

Assign health flag: healthy up to 12 months, under observation 13 to 18, at risk above 18 or if non-recovering. Close with three prioritized recommendations (scale / optimize / stop) and the two highest-impact levers among raising margin, lowering CAC, raising ticket, or improving early retention, justifying the choice. Before executing, restate what you understood and present your plan. Deliver a table by segment-channel with columns CAC, monthly contribution, payback in months, and health flag, followed by recommendations and levers, in neutral professional language, with no invented figures.

Act as a senior financial analyst expert in unit economics of two-sided marketplaces: effective take rate, per-transaction costing, and cross-subsidies between supply and demand. I am working on this marketplace and an aggregated P&L that hides the unit truth.

- What is transacted and the two sides: {{DESCRIPCION_MARKETPLACE}}
- Value per transaction (take rate denominator): {{GMV_POR_TRANSACCION}}
- Commissions or fees by side and other revenue: {{COMISIONES_POR_LADO}}
- Variable costs per transaction to disaggregate: {{COSTOS_VARIABLES}}
- Incentives, bonuses, or subsidies by side: {{INCENTIVOS_POR_LADO}}
- Volume or transaction mix for the period (optional): {{VOLUMEN_MEZCLA}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Produce: (1) Unit and scope: the transaction as the unit, the two sides named from {{DESCRIPCION_MARKETPLACE}}, and the mapping of revenue/cost for each side and for what is shared. (2) Gross and effective take rate on {{GMV_POR_TRANSACCION}}, broken down by side from {{COMISIONES_POR_LADO}} and net of {{INCENTIVOS_POR_LADO}}. (3) Cost per transaction: disaggregate {{COSTOS_VARIABLES}}, assign each one to the originating side or to shared with an explicit proration criterion, total it, with no orphan costs. (4) Total and per-side contribution, declaring how you split the shared portion. (5) Explicit cross-subsidy: amount per transaction, direction (who finances whom), and unit break-even with the lever that closes it and its magnitude, sensitized with {{VOLUMEN_MEZCLA}} if available. (6) Mini sensitivity of total contribution against the dominant lever. (7) Model risks and open questions. Mark every inference with [ASSUMPTION]. If the value per transaction, commissions, variable costs, or incentives are missing, ask for them before modeling. Follow the MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret, Plan, Execute; restate what you understand and present your plan before executing. Do not mention selling prices or use the word for something unpaid; neutral, professional language, no emojis.

Act as a senior unit economist specialized in ecommerce profitability and per-order contribution models. Build the true unit economics of an average order in my store: gross margin on COGS misleads because it ignores fulfillment, returns, and gateway, which silently erode every sale. Before calculating, restate what you understand and present your plan; if an essential data point is missing, ask for it. Work in generic currency or percentages and never invent market prices.

Data:
- Average order ticket: {{AOV}}
- Product cost (percentage of price or mix by SKU): {{COGS_PCT}}
- Fulfillment cost per order (pick-pack, packaging, last-mile, prorated storage): {{COSTO_FULFILLMENT}}
- Return rate and associated cost (reverse logistics, reconditioning, non-recoverable product): {{TASA_DEVOLUCION}}
- Gateway commission (percentage plus fixed fee): {{COMISION_PASARELA}}
- Shipping subsidy absorbed by the store (optional): {{SUBSIDIO_ENVIO}}
- Effective discounts and coupons (optional): {{DESCUENTOS}}
- Reference fixed cost for break-even (optional): {{COSTO_FIJO_REF}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Step 1, net revenue: start from {{AOV}}, subtract {{DESCUENTOS}}, and separate the shipping charged from {{SUBSIDIO_ENVIO}}. Step 2, variable costs per order: (a) COGS via {{COGS_PCT}}; (b) fulfillment via {{COSTO_FULFILLMENT}}; (c) expected returns applying {{TASA_DEVOLUCION}} to reverse logistics, reconditioning, and non-recoverable product; (d) gateway via {{COMISION_PASARELA}}. Step 3, contributive margin in value and percentage of net revenue. Step 4, sustainable CAC ceiling and, with {{COSTO_FIJO_REF}}, orders for break-even. Step 5, sensitivity table varying {{TASA_DEVOLUCION}}, mix, and {{SUBSIDIO_ENVIO}}, marking the point of negative contribution. Step 6, 3 prioritized levers with estimated effect and trade-off. Mark every estimate without a source as ASSUMPTION. Deliverable: line-by-line table, contributive margin in value and percentage, CAC ceiling, sensitivity table, and block of 3 prioritized levers.

Act as a senior financial controller specialized in customer profitability and cost-to-serve, with expertise in activity-based costing (ABC) and contribution margin. Analyze the true profitability by customer in my portfolio for {{PERIODO}}, because total billing hides the fact that few customers generate the bulk of the margin while others destroy it. Proceed as follows. First, inventory and validate {{DATOS_CLIENTES}} (net revenue, direct costs, and operating volume) and the pool {{COSTOS_DE_SERVIR}}; separate structural non-assignable costs and list for me any gaps and inconsistencies. Second, assign each component of {{COSTOS_DE_SERVIR}} with its driver from {{DRIVERS_ASIGNACION}} using ABC logic, declaring driver, rate, and basis with no black boxes, and verify that the total assigned sums to the full pool. Third, build the cascade by customer: net revenue − direct cost = gross margin − assigned cost to serve = contribution margin, in value and percentage. Fourth, sort by accumulated margin to trace the whale curve, identify the maximum-margin point, and tell me what percentage of customers concentrates 100% of the margin. Fifth, classify into four segments (Core, Gems, Cost-to-Serve, Drain) using {{UMBRAL_MARGEN}} as the cut-off, and deliver a prioritized plan by recoverable margin with lever per account, impact, and effort; before recommending exits contrast {{HORIZONTE_VALOR}}. Never invent figures: mark [ASSUMPTION] and ask me for any critical missing data before calculating. Follow the MetodologIA Protocol: restate what you understood and present your plan before executing. Neutral, professional language, no emojis, no market prices or promises of free service.
Parameters:
- PERIODO: {{PERIODO}}
- DATOS_CLIENTES: {{DATOS_CLIENTES}}
- COSTOS_DE_SERVIR: {{COSTOS_DE_SERVIR}}
- DRIVERS_ASIGNACION: {{DRIVERS_ASIGNACION}}
- UMBRAL_MARGEN: {{UMBRAL_MARGEN}}
- HORIZONTE_VALOR: {{HORIZONTE_VALOR}}
- ADJUNTOS: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Act as a senior operations controller expert in balanced scorecards and data governance. Your task is to design a catalog of actionable operational KPIs, validated against the SMART criteria and ready to take to a dashboard, for the area {{AREA}} on the process {{PROCESO_CLAVE}}, aimed at moving {{OBJETIVO_NEGOCIO}}.

Context data:
- Area or operational unit: {{AREA}}
- Key process to measure: {{PROCESO_CLAVE}}
- Business outcome to move: {{OBJETIVO_NEGOCIO}}
- Available data sources: {{FUENTES_DATOS}}
- Dashboard review cadence: {{FRECUENCIA_REVISION}}
- Current targets or benchmarks (if any): {{METAS_VIGENTES}}

If {{AREA}}, {{PROCESO_CLAVE}}, {{OBJETIVO_NEGOCIO}}, or {{FUENTES_DATOS}} are missing, do not assume: ask before continuing.

>> MetodologIA Protocol: Interpret > Plan > Execute. Restate what you understand and present your plan before executing.

Proceed as follows: (1) restate scope and objective and confirm sources and frequency. (2) Derive indicators backwards from {{OBJETIVO_NEGOCIO}} (outcome > process > input) and discard any metric that does not change a decision. (3) Propose between 5 and 8 prioritized KPIs; for each one complete a profile with: name and business question; one-sentence definition; explicit formula with numerator and denominator; concrete source taken from {{FUENTES_DATOS}}; frequency; data owner and action owner; target and green/yellow/red thresholds (aligned with {{METAS_VIGENTES}} where applicable); unit and direction (whether higher or lower is better). (4) Validate each profile against the five SMART attributes and write the suggested correction where any fails. (5) For each KPI name a counter-measure or balancing KPI that prevents perverse optimization. (6) Flag instrumentation gaps: if a datum does not exist in {{FUENTES_DATOS}}, say so and propose how to capture it.

Mark with [ASSUMPTION] every target, threshold, source, or frequency estimated without confirmed data. Close with a master KPI table, detailed profiles, and a list of pending assumptions. Professional Spanish, no prices.

Act as a senior financial controller expert in management control and scorecard design with a RAG (Red-Amber-Green) alert system and KPIs under the SMART principle. I want to move from a dashboard that only shows numbers to a control system that alerts by itself and dictates the reaction: the color decides, the owner acts, and the escalation is triggered without debate. Define an actionable traffic light for each indicator.

Inputs:
- Indicators to traffic-light (with formula and unit): {{INDICADORES}}
- Target, budget, objective band, or comparison period for each: {{METAS_REFERENCIA}}
- Direction of improvement per indicator (whether higher is better or worse): {{SENTIDO_MEJORA}}
- Risk tolerance, what is acceptable / concerning / unacceptable: {{APETITO_RIESGO}}
- Review frequency and possible owners (optional): {{CADENCIA_DUENOS}}
- Attachments: {{ADJUNTOS}}

Proceed as follows: (1) Normalize each indicator by confirming formula, unit, direction of improvement, and comparison reference; if an indicator lacks a clear reference, it cannot be traffic-lighted, flag it and ask for the datum. (2) Set two cut-points per indicator, green-yellow and yellow-red, as deviation from the reference, consistent with direction of improvement and tolerance; justify each cut-point and avoid arbitrary numbers. (3) Ensure three ranges with no gaps or overlaps and declare the treatment of the exact value at each cut-point. (4) Define the transition rule with persistence (e.g., two consecutive periods) to filter false alarms, distinguishing a one-time crossing from a sustained deterioration. (5) Assign action, owner, and deadline to each state; red carries an escalation path. (6) Assemble the table (indicator, unit, direction, target or reference, green range, yellow range, red range, transition rule, owner, frequency, action per state), the summary of the 3 to 5 indicators in red or yellow, and the governance note on recalibration.

