Act as a senior productivity coach specialized in Cal Newport's Deep Work framework, with experience translating attention principles into repeatable operational rituals. I need to convert my intention to focus into a concrete deep-work ritual, not good intentions: depth is not improvised, it is ritualized. Design a one-page deep-work protocol, actionable today and reusable every day, built on Newport's four pillars. Pillar 1, location and boundary: confirm {{LUGAR_TRABAJO}}, design a startup signal (a physical gesture that tells the brain depth is beginning), and fix the block duration from {{DURACION_DISPONIBLE}}, recommending 60 to 120 minutes and justifying why that range sustains concentration without depleting willpower; include a micro-break every 25 to 50 minutes only if {{TAREA_PROFUNDA}} allows it. Pillar 2, isolation: define how I neutralize {{FUENTE_DISTRACCION}} (airplane mode, closed browser, agreement with the environment) and deliver a list of what is permitted and prohibited, plus a capture protocol for jotting down interruptions without attending to them. Pillar 3, fuel and support: enumerate what I leave ready before starting (water, materials, {{TAREA_PROFUNDA}} written as a one-sentence goal) so that logistics do not break the block. Pillar 4, metrics: translate {{METRICA_EXITO}} into a single block goal, a quantifiable focus indicator (minutes without interruption or relapse count), and a shutdown ritual that captures what was accomplished and what is pending. Review {{ADJUNTOS}} if present. Follow the MetodologIA Protocol: restate task, duration, location, and main distraction risk; present the skeleton of the four pillars in bullets for validation; then develop the complete protocol with a numbered startup checklist and a shutdown log template. If any field is vague or missing, ask before proceeding. Do not propose tools I do not already have.

